{"basis" 1828 "lineage" "01a12a15-3e0a-75a1-9dac-f8b8c5d5c3f1" "questions" [[{:db/id 17592186348230 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id 17592186147430 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "e17fe7fc-3b85-5f2c-aa69-2336b05b16b2" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Solutions to Simple Harmonic Motion Equations"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "13447381-8721-54cc-93ad-ec3451ed8c7a" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id 17592186345519} {:answer/id #uuid "548a412d-769e-5f37-b287-b5e8a2f3516b" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id 17592186346069} {:answer/id #uuid "8312aa12-0acb-51e1-b48d-e1dd50ef851e" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id 17592186346492} {:answer/id #uuid "9c74b04c-077f-5abe-93e4-5b311d8196e3" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id 17592186346714} {:answer/id #uuid "e110475f-605d-52d6-9b4e-ec752d3c2333" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id 17592186347329}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "548a412d-769e-5f37-b287-b5e8a2f3516b" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id 17592186346069} :answer-field/id #uuid "ea32768f-9a8b-5730-aa6f-faea2f793e5e" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id 17592186348014}] :question/difficulty {:db/id 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "38fdf4c6-f5f9-5439-b136-b509926fb43c" :question/math-academy-id "q-79487" :question/problem "A particle is moving with simple harmonic motion. The position of the particle $y(t)$ at time $t$ is governed by the equation\n\n$$\n{y}^{″}+2y=0.\n$$\n\nWhich of the following could be used to represent the general solution for $y(t)?$\n\n- $y=R\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\sqrt{2}t+{ϕ}_{1})$\n- $y=R\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t+{ϕ}_{2})$\n- $y=A\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\sqrt{2}t+B\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\sqrt{2}t$\n\nNote that $R,{ϕ}_{1},{ϕ}_{2},A$ and $B$ are constants." :question/worked-solution "The SHM equation is\n\n$$\n{y}^{″}+{\\omega }^{2}y=0.\n$$\n\nThe general solution to the SHM equation can be written in three different forms:\n\n- $y=R\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\omega t+{ϕ}_{1})$\n\n- $y=R\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\omega t+{ϕ}_{2})$\n\n- $y=A\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\omega t+B\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\omega t$\n\nIn our case\n\n$$\n{\\omega }^{2}=2\\,⟹\\,\\omega =\\sqrt{2}.\n$$\n\nThus, we have:\n\n- $y=R\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\sqrt{2}t+{ϕ}_{1})$\n\n- $y=R\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\sqrt{2}t+{ϕ}_{2})$\n\n- $y=A\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\sqrt{2}t+B\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\sqrt{2}t$\n\nTherefore, only functions I and III could be used to represent $y(t).$"}] [{:db/id 17592186348239 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id 17592186119848 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "1d847ec9-c379-5013-ab77-a9b92dc8ca27" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying the Matrix With a Given Rank by Inspection"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0fcc3707-5257-56f7-a94a-c16be3c655ca" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$A$ only" :db/id 17592186345482} {:answer/id #uuid "1488839f-b78a-5c71-b12c-fd396aefb4d2" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$B$ only" :db/id 17592186345526} {:answer/id #uuid "79f9061d-b91e-5d06-8205-d8cf49d7a89e" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$C$ only" :db/id 17592186346419} {:answer/id #uuid "e4865247-2fc7-50f5-8dc6-90b9921dd7ec" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$B$ and $C$ only" :db/id 17592186347365} {:answer/id #uuid "ebc7ff50-8edf-53da-a902-6aa67ba79a1c" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$A$ and $B$ only" :db/id 17592186347430}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "1488839f-b78a-5c71-b12c-fd396aefb4d2" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$B$ only" :db/id 17592186345526} :answer-field/id #uuid "cee01bee-957b-5375-bc24-a42dae9c7573" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id 17592186347961}] :question/difficulty {:db/id 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "7de7e9fe-a671-5464-bba9-92a57599131b" :question/math-academy-id "q-79568" :question/problem "Which of the following matrices have a rank of $5?$\n\n$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 1 & 0 & 4 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 2 & 1 & -3 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 2 & 0 & -2\\end{aligned}],\\,B=[\\begin{aligned}2 & -1 & 3 & 0 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & -2 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 2 & 4 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 0 & -1\\end{aligned}],\\,C=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 3 & 2 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 3 & 4 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]\n$$" :question/worked-solution "First, notice that a matrix of rank $5$ must have at least $5$ columns and at least $5$ rows. \nSo, we disregard the matrix $A$ since it has only $3$ rows and the matrix $C$ since it has only $4$ columns. \n\nAlso, the matrix must have $5$ pivot columns. As we can see, the matrix $B$ has exactly $5$ pivot columns. So, $\\text{rank}(B)=5.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"$B$ only\"."}] [{:db/id 17592186348241 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id 17592186147450 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "813ec1d4-93ec-5a9e-996f-b2e672714927" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Solutions to Forced Harmonic Motion Equations"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "02dd969f-3835-50e2-8bfc-d425532d61e9" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id 17592186345375} {:answer/id #uuid "90284f75-7093-5853-a1bf-dfd348956afc" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id 17592186346606} {:answer/id #uuid "a6006a37-315c-535b-84c5-4aae73a3316f" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id 17592186346800} {:answer/id #uuid "c7e5dd56-c7d7-5b38-9d36-b6ef7d0b527a" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id 17592186347087} {:answer/id #uuid "f4150680-d532-528d-904e-713e4f2b8907" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id 17592186347513}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "c7e5dd56-c7d7-5b38-9d36-b6ef7d0b527a" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id 17592186347087} :answer-field/id #uuid "43f9a281-44ca-5645-8aea-5eda107eeda2" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id 17592186347719}] :question/difficulty {:db/id 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "41288342-236a-5f59-a0ab-b7381746dd7e" :question/math-academy-id "q-79575" :question/problem "A particle is moving with forced harmonic motion with damping. The position of the particle $y(t)$ at time $t$ is governed by the equation\n\n$$\n{y}^{″}+4{y}^{′}+20y=40\\operatorname{cos}⁡2t.\n$$\n\nWhich of the following could be used to represent the general solution for $y(t)?$\n\nI. $y={e}^{-2t}(A\\operatorname{cos}⁡4t+B\\operatorname{sin}⁡4t)+10\\operatorname{cos}⁡2t$\n\nII. $y=R{e}^{-4t}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(t+ϕ)+2\\operatorname{cos}⁡2t+\\operatorname{sin}⁡2t$\n\nIII. $y=R{e}^{-2t}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4t+ϕ)+2\\operatorname{cos}⁡2t+\\operatorname{sin}⁡2t$\n\nNote that $A,$ $B,$ $R$ and $ϕ$ are constants." :question/worked-solution "The general solution is the sum of the complementary solution and the particular solution:\n\n$$\ny(t)={y}_{c}(t)+{y}_{p}(t)\n$$\n\nFirst, we find the complementary solution ${y}_{c}(t),$ which is the solution to the homogeneous equation ${y}^{″}+4{y}^{′}+20y=0.$ Writing down and solving the characteristic equation gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\lambda }^{2}+4\\lambda +20 & =0 \\\\ ({\\lambda }^{2}+4\\lambda +4)-4+20 & =0 \\\\ (\\lambda +2)^{2}+16 & =0 \\\\ (\\lambda +2)^{2} & =-16 \\\\ \\lambda +2 & =\\pm 4\\text{i} \\\\ \\lambda  & =-2\\pm 4\\text{i}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore the complimentary function ${y}_{c}(t)$ can be expressed in any of the following forms:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{c}(t) & ={e}^{-2t}(A\\operatorname{cos}⁡4t+B\\operatorname{sin}⁡4t), \\\\ {y}_{c}(t) & =R{e}^{-2t}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4t+ϕ), \\\\ {y}_{c}(t) & =R{e}^{-2t}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4t+ϕ).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we find the particular solution ${y}_{p}(t).$ The particular solution is of the form\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}(t)=\\alpha \\operatorname{cos}⁡2t+\\beta \\operatorname{sin}⁡2t.\n$$\n\nBy substituting the particular solution into the differential equation and equating coefficients, we find that $\\alpha =2$ and $\\beta =1.$ So, the particular solution is\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}(t)=2\\operatorname{cos}⁡2t+\\operatorname{sin}⁡2t.\n$$\n\nFinally, then, the general solution is given by the sum of the complementary and particular solutions. So, it can be written in any of the following forms:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y(t) & ={e}^{-2t}(A\\operatorname{cos}⁡4t+B\\operatorname{sin}⁡4t)+2\\operatorname{cos}⁡2t+\\operatorname{sin}⁡2t, \\\\ y(t) & =R{e}^{-2t}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4t+ϕ)+2\\operatorname{cos}⁡2t+\\operatorname{sin}⁡2t, \\\\ y(t) & =R{e}^{-2t}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4t+ϕ)+2\\operatorname{cos}⁡2t+\\operatorname{sin}⁡2t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nOnly III matches the forms above. Therefore, the correct answer is \"III only\"."}] [{:db/id 17592186348242 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id 17592186147592 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "fc7eacaa-2cf2-5cae-a40c-737c98124bcf" :knowledge-point/title "Determining the Degree of a Homogeneous Polynomial Using the Fast Method"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "045d95b9-c578-58ae-804d-b1f18050dded" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id 17592186345387} {:answer/id #uuid "1c552b52-c446-5ba7-b15e-630f222699c0" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id 17592186345597} {:answer/id #uuid "4e3e8db4-c9a7-54bf-bb89-13376400df6a" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id 17592186346020} {:answer/id #uuid "9c4634b0-4c75-5c24-a60d-26d06a0b1dbc" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id 17592186346710} {:answer/id #uuid "d32e21c2-215e-513f-b844-3f5720b2a2a4" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id 17592186347199}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "d32e21c2-215e-513f-b844-3f5720b2a2a4" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id 17592186347199} :answer-field/id #uuid "420c198c-b60f-5531-84a3-03e2cd3be765" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id 17592186347718}] :question/difficulty {:db/id 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "a1d20ac9-bf7d-58e9-8fa7-b423eaa3f414" :question/math-academy-id "q-79578" :question/problem "Which of the following polynomials are homogeneous?\n\n- $f(x)=3xy$\n- $g(x,y)={y}^{2}+3{x}^{2}y$\n- $h(x,y)={x}^{3}-4x{y}^{2}+3{y}^{3}$" :question/worked-solution "Let's go through each of the options.\n\n- The function $f(x)$ is homogeneous of degree $2.$  It is a polynomial of degree $2,$ with no linear or constant terms.\n\n- The function $g(x,y)$ is not homogeneous. It is a polynomial of degree $3.$ However, it also contains a term $({y}^{2})$ that is of degree $2.$ Therefore, not all of its terms are of degree $3.$\n\n- The function $g(x,y)$ is homogeneous of degree $3.$ It is a polynomial of degree $3,$ and all of its terms are of degree $3.$"}] [{:db/id 17592186348244 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id 17592186147450 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "813ec1d4-93ec-5a9e-996f-b2e672714927" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Solutions to Forced Harmonic Motion Equations"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "4a7e4d86-e681-55dc-851f-626c243db8a7" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id 17592186345989} {:answer/id #uuid "4c0f622d-c941-546a-ad1f-8acfacf65b39" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id 17592186346006} {:answer/id #uuid "ae6f9093-a9f2-5e62-b0ac-2ba309a44af7" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id 17592186346873} {:answer/id #uuid "edc2ccf5-8acb-5ce7-851c-d8e35173456f" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id 17592186347450} {:answer/id #uuid "fe8725d2-f3d6-5d3b-b62d-26c66f72440d" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id 17592186347587}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "4a7e4d86-e681-55dc-851f-626c243db8a7" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id 17592186345989} :answer-field/id #uuid "9becafd4-a5f8-56dc-8174-c3f2dd0c9c7b" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id 17592186347879}] :question/difficulty {:db/id 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "b57b422d-c4f7-5a90-ae50-c581e716c943" :question/math-academy-id "q-79586" :question/problem "A particle is moving with forced harmonic motion without damping. The position of the particle $y(t)$ at time $t$ is governed by the equation\n\n$$\n{y}^{″}+4y=2.\n$$\n\nWhich of the following could be used to represent the general solution for $y(t)?$\n\n- $y=R\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t+ϕ)+2$\n- $y=R\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t+ϕ)+\\frac{1}{2}$\n- $y={e}^{-t}(A\\operatorname{cos}⁡2t+B\\operatorname{sin}⁡2t)+\\frac{1}{2}$\n\nNote that $A,$ $B,$ $R$ and $ϕ$ are constants." :question/worked-solution "The general solution is the sum of the complementary solution and the particular solution:\n\n$$\ny(t)={y}_{c}(t)+{y}_{p}(t)\n$$\n\nFirst, we find the complementary solution ${y}_{c}(t),$ which is the solution to the simple harmonic motion equation ${y}^{″}+{\\omega }^{2}y=0.$\nIt can be written in three different forms:\n\n- ${y}_{c}=R\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\omega t+ϕ)$\n\n- ${y}_{c}=R\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\omega t+ϕ)$\n\n- ${y}_{c}=A\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\omega t+B\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\omega t$\n\nIn our case\n\n$$\n{\\omega }^{2}=4\\,⟹\\,\\omega =2.\n$$\n\nThus, we have:\n\n- ${y}_{c}=R\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t+ϕ)$\n\n- ${y}_{c}=R\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2+ϕ)$\n\n- ${y}_{c}=A\\operatorname{cos}⁡2t+B\\operatorname{sin}⁡2t$\n\nNext, we find the particular solution ${y}_{p}(t).$ Since the external force is a constant, we assume that the particular solution is also a constant,\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}(t)=\\alpha .\n$$\n\nBy substituting the particular solution into the differential equation and equating coefficients, we find that $\\alpha =\\frac{1}{2}.$ So, the particular solution is\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}(t)=\\frac{1}{2}.\n$$\n\nFinally, then, the general solution is given by the sum of the complementary and particular solutions. So, it can be written in any of the following forms:\n\n- ${y}_{c}=R\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t+ϕ)+\\frac{1}{2}$\n\n- ${y}_{c}=R\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t+ϕ)+\\frac{1}{2}$\n\n- ${y}_{c}=A\\operatorname{cos}⁡2t+B\\operatorname{sin}⁡2t+\\frac{1}{2}$\n\nOnly II match the forms above. Therefore, the correct answer is \"II only\"."}] [{:db/id 17592186348278 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id 17592186152192 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "42bc95a3-4e0c-5487-8427-31cee0ecf80b" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Solutions of Linear ODEs Using the Sum Rule"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0923514d-c5f5-5824-9559-5b62433d29c1" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id 17592186345422} {:answer/id #uuid "236eae5a-9ad6-5197-8d56-b3601675b294" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id 17592186345673} {:answer/id #uuid "71bf93d7-35da-5bf1-b1c4-67d62905acf2" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id 17592186346357} {:answer/id #uuid "ad47b4a5-f932-52e5-a826-24224069a452" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id 17592186346863} {:answer/id #uuid "ae59bf38-16be-58c7-bd1c-828957c06a49" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id 17592186346872}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "0923514d-c5f5-5824-9559-5b62433d29c1" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id 17592186345422} :answer-field/id #uuid "e9b8d684-4b0c-5a9a-8ebf-813cbe7bf7a5" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id 17592186348012}] :question/difficulty {:db/id 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "3fe9a401-2c33-5db4-91d8-f65cf3a85456" :question/math-academy-id "q-79770" :question/problem "Given that ${y}_{1}(x)={e}^{-2x}$ and ${y}_{2}(x)={e}^{3x}$ are solutions to the equation ${y}^{″}-{y}^{′}-6y=0,$ then according to the superposition principle which of the following must also be a solution to the equation?\n\n- $y(x)=\\frac{{e}^{-2x}}{{e}^{3x}}$\n- $y(x)={e}^{-2x}+{e}^{3x}$\n- $y(x)={e}^{-2x}\\cdot {e}^{3x}$" :question/worked-solution "According to the superposition principle, if the functions ${y}_{1}(x)$ and ${y}_{2}(x)$ are solutions to a homogeneous, linear differential equation, then $y(x)={y}_{1}(x)+{y}_{2}(x)$ is also a solution to the equation.\n\nLet's consider each option:\n\n- The function $y(x)=\\frac{{e}^{-2x}}{{e}^{3x}}$ is **not** guaranteed to be a solution of the equation by the superposition principle. The superposition principle does not extend to the quotients of the known solutions.\n\n- The function $y(x)={e}^{-2x}+{e}^{3x}$ is a solution of the equation by the superposition principle.\n\n- The function $y(x)={e}^{-2x}\\cdot {e}^{3x}$ is **not** guaranteed to be a solution of the equation by the superposition principle. The superposition principle does not extend to the products of the known solutions.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II only.\""}] [{:db/id 17592186348279 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id 17592186152193 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "b0829d4a-239a-5a8e-9c8e-3cdc76a993bc" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Solutions of Linear ODEs Using the Constant Multiple Rule"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "7622f2e1-4bc7-5052-8059-cb0739d3adba" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id 17592186346389} {:answer/id #uuid "8d2f1294-18fe-5249-8216-d3b2478a7f61" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id 17592186346580} {:answer/id #uuid "b47aa73a-cb38-5bf6-a296-c1eae1ea691a" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id 17592186346924} {:answer/id #uuid "e718ee24-6201-580b-b3d7-2418fe4bb5b6" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id 17592186347387} {:answer/id #uuid "ebd7344b-824c-54d9-95ae-9528861755ab" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id 17592186347431}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "ebd7344b-824c-54d9-95ae-9528861755ab" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id 17592186347431} :answer-field/id #uuid "b93f0d66-ae9b-56db-8736-14ac1a058bd9" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id 17592186347931}] :question/difficulty {:db/id 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "dc8e08c0-d0ce-557c-9060-08f946a61e52" :question/math-academy-id "q-79774" :question/problem "Given that ${y}_{1}(x)={e}^{4x}$ is a solution to the equation ${y}^{″}-2{y}^{′}-8y=0,$ then according to the superposition principle which of the following must also be a solution to the equation?\n\n- $y(x)=4x{e}^{4x}$\n- $y(x)=2{e}^{4x}$\n- $y(x)=\\frac{{e}^{4x}}{x}$" :question/worked-solution "According to the superposition principle, if the function ${y}_{1}(x)$ is a solution to a homogeneous, linear differential equation, and $c$ is a constant, then $y(x)=c{y}_{1}(x)$ is also a solution to the equation.\n\nLet's consider each option:\n\n- The function $y(x)=4x{e}^{4x}$ is **not** guaranteed to be a solution of the equation by the superposition principle. The superposition principle does not extend to the product of a non-constant function and a known solution.\n\n- The function $y(x)=2{e}^{4x}$ is a solution of the equation by the superposition principle. In this case, $c=2.$\n\n- The function $y(x)=\\frac{{e}^{4x}}{x}$ is **not** guaranteed to be a solution of the equation by the superposition principle. The superposition principle does not extend to the product of a non-constant function and a known solution.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II only.\""}] [{:db/id 17592186348280 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id 17592186152193 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "b0829d4a-239a-5a8e-9c8e-3cdc76a993bc" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Solutions of Linear ODEs Using the Constant Multiple Rule"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "28d51c4b-d54b-5abd-a602-97d128497895" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id 17592186345716} {:answer/id #uuid "2e42bc59-7be3-5fab-8e80-0f3c996bd387" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id 17592186345762} {:answer/id #uuid "5743a26a-1810-52e2-8511-82d7ec3b2ccd" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id 17592186346095} {:answer/id #uuid "9ce8b541-90cf-5ba1-bd89-fbad0ae4d6f1" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id 17592186346718} {:answer/id #uuid "f66a870a-6721-5d7a-a5d8-0275b073de0c" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id 17592186347536}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "9ce8b541-90cf-5ba1-bd89-fbad0ae4d6f1" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id 17592186346718} :answer-field/id #uuid "6b85d4a3-33e9-554e-bcc5-65fac753f546" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id 17592186347797}] :question/difficulty {:db/id 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "04a3c220-dde3-5739-ac25-163b805528ed" :question/math-academy-id "q-79775" :question/problem "Given that ${y}_{1}(x)={e}^{3x}$ is a solution to the equation ${y}^{″}-9y=0,$ then according to the superposition principle which of the following must also be a solution to the equation?\n\n- $y(x)=2x{e}^{3x}$\n- $y(x)=6{e}^{3x}$\n- $y(x)=-5{e}^{3x}$" :question/worked-solution "According to the superposition principle, if the function ${y}_{1}(x)$ is a solution to a homogeneous, linear differential equation, and $c$ is a constant, then $y(x)=c{y}_{1}(x)$ is also a solution to the equation.\n\nLet's consider each option:\n\n- The function $y(x)=2x{e}^{3x}$ is **not** guaranteed to be a solution of the equation by the superposition principle. The superposition principle does not extend to the product of a non-constant function and a known solution.\n\n- The function $y(x)=6{e}^{3x}$ is a solution of the equation by the superposition principle. In this case, $c=6.$\n\n- The function $y(x)=-5{e}^{3x}$ is a solution of the equation by the superposition principle. In this case, $c=-5.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II and III only.\""}] [{:db/id 17592186348281 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id 17592186152193 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "b0829d4a-239a-5a8e-9c8e-3cdc76a993bc" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Solutions of Linear ODEs Using the Constant Multiple Rule"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "565ac935-420d-5f95-a28d-92456faced76" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id 17592186346084} {:answer/id #uuid "991744d5-719c-562a-98e8-cbd3d093ac57" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id 17592186346685} {:answer/id #uuid "9afcf3c3-ebc6-5837-b7cb-42137df13251" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id 17592186346706} {:answer/id #uuid "9f998980-a9b5-5af7-8538-ea271ed86de1" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id 17592186346745} {:answer/id #uuid "f5cf9711-8831-5d9e-8592-607bdbb062ab" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id 17592186347531}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "9f998980-a9b5-5af7-8538-ea271ed86de1" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id 17592186346745} :answer-field/id #uuid "8a64b2bc-95cd-52af-bee8-0963efc75e7f" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id 17592186347845}] :question/difficulty {:db/id 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "107897db-d4be-5207-adc1-b404c9d01f49" :question/math-academy-id "q-79777" :question/problem "Given that ${y}_{1}(x)=\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x)$ is a solution to a homogeneous, linear, second-order ODE, then according to the superposition principle which of the following must also be a solution to the equation?\n\n- $y(x)=-3\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x)$\n- $y(x)={x}^{3}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x)$\n- $y(x)=\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x)}{2}$" :question/worked-solution "According to the superposition principle, if the function ${y}_{1}(x)$ is a solution to a homogeneous, linear differential equation, and $c$ is a constant, then $y(x)=c{y}_{1}(x)$ is also a solution to the equation.\n\nLet's consider each option:\n\n- The function $y(x)=-3\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x)$ is a solution of the equation by the superposition principle. In this case, $c=-3.$\n\n- The function $y(x)={x}^{3}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x)$ is **not** guaranteed to be a solution of the equation by the superposition principle. The superposition principle does not extend to the product of a non-constant function and a known solution.\n\n- The function $y(x)=\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x)}{2}$ is a solution of the equation by the superposition principle. In this case, $c=\\frac{1}{2}.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and III only.\""}] [{:db/id 17592186348285 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id 17592186152192 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "42bc95a3-4e0c-5487-8427-31cee0ecf80b" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Solutions of Linear ODEs Using the Sum Rule"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1472b95c-69ea-5b14-8dd4-89aef46f7fe9" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id 17592186345524} {:answer/id #uuid "3c0b76c8-9fcf-5df6-8c74-835bcbbfa612" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id 17592186345860} {:answer/id #uuid "4ff6d3eb-665a-5929-b7c0-8b79a662ccb9" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id 17592186346033} {:answer/id #uuid "e652fc59-a495-5990-be19-3d1a1a916abe" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id 17592186347382} {:answer/id #uuid "ed65c49d-dd46-5594-86e2-db77968435c1" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id 17592186347446}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "4ff6d3eb-665a-5929-b7c0-8b79a662ccb9" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id 17592186346033} :answer-field/id #uuid "97d56d4c-d604-591b-aee3-dc06c79970cb" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id 17592186347871}] :question/difficulty {:db/id 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "eb140c30-bdb5-5662-90f1-f05ca3e626cc" :question/math-academy-id "q-79800" :question/problem "Given that ${y}_{1}(x)={x}^{2}$ and ${y}_{2}(x)={x}^{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡x$ are solutions to a homogeneous, linear, second-order ODE, then according to the superposition principle which of the following must also be a solution to the equation?\n\n- $y(x)={x}^{4}\\operatorname{ln}⁡x$\n- $y(x)={x}^{2}(1+\\operatorname{ln}⁡x)$\n- $y(x)={x}^{2}+\\operatorname{ln}⁡x$" :question/worked-solution "According to the superposition principle, if the functions ${y}_{1}(x)$ and ${y}_{2}(x)$ are solutions to a homogeneous, linear differential equation, then $y(x)={y}_{1}(x)+{y}_{2}(x)$ is also a solution to the equation.\n\nLet's consider each option:\n\n- The function $y(x)={x}^{4}\\operatorname{ln}⁡x={x}^{2}\\cdot {x}^{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡x$ is **not** guaranteed to be a solution of the equation by the superposition principle. The superposition principle does not extend to the quotients of the known solutions.\n\n- The function $y(x)={x}^{2}(1+\\operatorname{ln}⁡x)={x}^{2}+{x}^{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡x$ is a solution of the equation by the superposition principle.\n\n- The function $y(x)={x}^{2}+\\operatorname{ln}⁡x$ is **not** guaranteed to be a solution of the equation by the superposition principle because $\\operatorname{ln}⁡x$ is not one of the known solutions.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II only.\""}] [{:db/id 17592186348296 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id 17592186152192 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "42bc95a3-4e0c-5487-8427-31cee0ecf80b" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Solutions of Linear ODEs Using the Sum Rule"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "399cfc3e-0e98-531e-8101-0c3517202d0f" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id 17592186345842} {:answer/id #uuid "5c5d5db9-56b1-580b-9ac9-53a7414e616b" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id 17592186346139} {:answer/id #uuid "66e8b755-dcc3-5191-b6e6-9ac03363fc00" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id 17592186346251} {:answer/id #uuid "7a9e482e-853e-5ee4-9487-d2fdaefb7d00" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id 17592186346423} {:answer/id #uuid "d42faa0f-d1bb-56c6-b164-a60c6f94f831" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id 17592186347207}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "66e8b755-dcc3-5191-b6e6-9ac03363fc00" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id 17592186346251} :answer-field/id #uuid "f2674e3a-87e7-58d5-b756-a0d1604a52a5" :answe