is \"III only.\""}] [{:db/id 17592186348297 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id 17592186152193 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "b0829d4a-239a-5a8e-9c8e-3cdc76a993bc" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Solutions of Linear ODEs Using the Constant Multiple Rule"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "148030af-1153-522c-96b9-9eb0be4c2c2e" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id 17592186345525} {:answer/id #uuid "2f60523b-219d-5690-9873-501f2188697d" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, II and III" :db/id 17592186345769} {:answer/id #uuid "342e3d6b-4da3-5580-9763-ba47ac41712a" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id 17592186345807} {:answer/id #uuid "4c268109-fcfb-52b1-9777-c062a09b91fd" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id 17592186346008} {:answer/id #uuid "b37d3193-f2be-5ee3-a085-b2132afbb3b7" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id 17592186346920}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "148030af-1153-522c-96b9-9eb0be4c2c2e" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id 17592186345525} :answer-field/id #uuid "2eb90f35-96bc-5290-a888-158dc034c574" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id 17592186347681}] :question/difficulty {:db/id 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "7f36feb8-418f-5686-b06d-68d66e729361" :question/math-academy-id "q-79830" :question/problem "Given that ${y}_{1}(x)={x}^{2}$ is a solution to the equation ${x}^{2}{y}^{″}+x{y}^{′}-4y=0,$ then according to the superposition principle which of the following must also be a solution to the equation?\n\n- $y(x)=3{x}^{4}$\n- $y(x)=-{x}^{2}$\n- $y(x)={x}^{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡x$" :question/worked-solution "According to the superposition principle, if the function ${y}_{1}(x)$ is a solution to a homogeneous, linear differential equation, and $c$ is a constant, then $y(x)=c{y}_{1}(x)$ is also a solution to the equation.\n\nLet's consider each option:\n\n- The function $y(x)=3{x}^{4}$ is **not** guaranteed to be a solution of the equation by the superposition principle because $y(x)={x}^{4}$ is not a solution.\n\n- The function $y(x)=-{x}^{2}$ is a solution of the equation by the superposition principle. In this case, $c=-1.$\n\n- The function $y(x)={x}^{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡x$ is **not** guaranteed to be a solution of the equation by the superposition principle. The superposition principle does not extend to the product of a non-constant function and a known solution.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II only.\""}] [{:db/id 17592186348298 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id 17592186152193 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "b0829d4a-239a-5a8e-9c8e-3cdc76a993bc" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Solutions of Linear ODEs Using the Constant Multiple Rule"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "7187055e-4336-5394-b471-640d966a2cf0" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id 17592186346355} {:answer/id #uuid "b18f51af-e4e0-58ca-aa7a-c88dc241fbd6" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id 17592186346901} {:answer/id #uuid "c1ea3a33-ce5b-5955-8b90-0480d5909e85" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id 17592186347027} {:answer/id #uuid "dcb7c664-14b5-562e-b4f8-c5651a7c35a7" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id 17592186347285} {:answer/id #uuid "e6773521-a6c7-5b14-904a-ce1085e27d02" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id 17592186347384}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "dcb7c664-14b5-562e-b4f8-c5651a7c35a7" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id 17592186347285} :answer-field/id #uuid "9ebd0d86-01ca-5699-9e4a-4ba5c5a24c49" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id 17592186347882}] :question/difficulty {:db/id 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "2273f6bc-da9e-5824-b9ba-6b0ea3f200d5" :question/math-academy-id "q-79832" :question/problem "Given that ${y}_{1}(x)={x}^{-5}$ is a solution to the equation ${x}^{2}{y}^{″}+5x{y}^{′}-5y=0,$ then according to the superposition principle which of the following must also be a solution to the equation?\n\n- $y(x)={x}^{-5}\\operatorname{ln}⁡x$\n- $y(x)=5{x}^{-4}$\n- $y(x)=-{x}^{-5}$" :question/worked-solution "According to the superposition principle, if the function ${y}_{1}(x)$ is a solution to a homogeneous, linear differential equation, and $c$ is a constant, then $y(x)=c{y}_{1}(x)$ is also a solution to the equation.\n\nLet's consider each option:\n\n- The function $y(x)={x}^{-5}\\operatorname{ln}⁡x$ is **not** guaranteed to be a solution of the equation by the superposition principle. The superposition principle does not extend to the product of a non-constant function and a known solution.\n\n- The function $y(x)=5{x}^{-4}$ is **not** guaranteed to be a solution of the equation by the superposition principle because $y={x}^{-4}$ is not a solution.\n\n- The function $y(x)=-{x}^{-5}$ is a solution of the equation by the superposition principle. In this case, $c=-1.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"III only.\""}] [{:db/id 17592186348299 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id 17592186152193 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "b0829d4a-239a-5a8e-9c8e-3cdc76a993bc" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Solutions of Linear ODEs Using the Constant Multiple Rule"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "33e30059-7bf8-5a3c-b7d4-de00bab20d2e" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id 17592186345804} {:answer/id #uuid "4be19b37-0106-5ed5-97e2-1edf64541bcd" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id 17592186346005} {:answer/id #uuid "83e3db0b-aba4-57c5-8eab-04d86a5e29bf" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id 17592186346496} {:answer/id #uuid "cdb6bb84-90d2-5079-a8e5-2d8a006b7f59" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id 17592186347152} {:answer/id #uuid "d257fa37-02d8-5ab9-99fc-e90f76ed1a07" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id 17592186347193}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "83e3db0b-aba4-57c5-8eab-04d86a5e29bf" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id 17592186346496} :answer-field/id #uuid "b25b4666-7553-569e-85fa-0c68793ee928" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id 17592186347915}] :question/difficulty {:db/id 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "60c58de3-1420-5803-8b3d-664cd468fc15" :question/math-academy-id "q-79833" :question/problem "Given that ${y}_{1}(x)={e}^{-6x}$ is a solution to the equation ${y}^{″}+5{y}^{′}-6y=0,$ then according to the superposition principle which of the following must also be a solution to the equation?\n\n- $y(x)=2{e}^{-6x}$\n- $y(x)={e}^{3-6x}$\n- $y(x)=-{e}^{6x}$" :question/worked-solution "According to the superposition principle, if the function ${y}_{1}(x)$ is a solution to a homogeneous, linear differential equation, and $c$ is a constant, then $y(x)=c{y}_{1}(x)$ is also a solution to the equation.\n\nLet's consider each option:\n\n- The function $y(x)=2{e}^{-6x}$ is a solution of the equation by the superposition principle. In this case, $c=2.$\n\n- The function $y(x)={e}^{3-6x}={e}^{3}\\cdot {e}^{-6x}$ is a solution of the equation by the superposition principle. In this case, $c={e}^{3}.$\n\n- The function $y(x)=-{e}^{6x}$ is **not** guaranteed to be a solution of the equation by the superposition principle because $y={e}^{6x}$ is not a solution.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and II only.\""}] [{:db/id 17592186348353 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id 17592186087746 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "d8c04b23-e341-57e9-a072-985e4b536e5a" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Linear Transformations"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "5ea428cc-3ce1-5069-904f-06975daa7d7b" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id 17592186346168} {:answer/id #uuid "60251f71-23e1-5eda-8cac-1f0b5f2cc9f0" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id 17592186346180} {:answer/id #uuid "b32a3fbc-0005-56e7-b487-e0a8c3da756d" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id 17592186346914} {:answer/id #uuid "d1402d14-95f4-54bf-b6af-79584e4da090" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, II, and III" :db/id 17592186347186} {:answer/id #uuid "ee9bbe41-91a1-5c98-a1fb-eb3fa9c8af98" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id 17592186347457}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "d1402d14-95f4-54bf-b6af-79584e4da090" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, II, and III" :db/id 17592186347186} :answer-field/id #uuid "6630a500-04b2-5ab0-aa27-0d3c241f057d" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id 17592186347782}] :question/difficulty {:db/id 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "00b02bef-cbb1-520c-b74e-661d82b27e5e" :question/math-academy-id "q-8022" :question/problem "Let \n$T:[\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y\\end{aligned}]⟶[\\begin{aligned}x+y \\\\ 2y\\end{aligned}].$\nWhich of the following statements are true?\n\n- $T(kv)=kT(v)$ for any real $2\\times 1$ column vector $v$ and real scalar $k$\n- $T({v}_{1}+{v}_{2})=T({v}_{1})+T({v}_{2})$ for any real $2\\times 1$ column vectors ${v}_{1}$ and ${v}_{2}$\n- $T$ is a linear transformation" :question/worked-solution "A linear transformation $T$ satisfies two properties:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & T[\\begin{aligned}kx \\\\ ky\\end{aligned}]=kT[\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y\\end{aligned}], \\\\  & T[\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}+{x}_{2} \\\\ {y}_{1}+{y}_{2}\\end{aligned}]=T[\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} \\\\ {y}_{1}\\end{aligned}]+T[\\begin{aligned}{x}_{2} \\\\ {y}_{2}\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Checking the first condition, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}T[\\begin{aligned}kx \\\\ ky\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}kx+ky \\\\ 2(ky)\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}k(x+y) \\\\ k(2y)\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =k[\\begin{aligned}x+y \\\\ 2y\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =kT[\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y\\end{aligned}].\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Checking the second condition, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}T[\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}+{x}_{2} \\\\ {y}_{1}+{y}_{2}\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}+{x}_{2}+{y}_{1}+{y}_{2} \\\\ 2({y}_{1}+{y}_{2})\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}+{y}_{1} \\\\ 2{y}_{1}\\end{aligned}]+[\\begin{aligned}{x}_{2}+{y}_{2} \\\\ 2{y}_{2}\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =T[\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} \\\\ {y}_{1}\\end{aligned}]+T[\\begin{aligned}{x}_{2} \\\\ {y}_{2}\\end{aligned}].\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $\\text{T}$ is a linear transformation.\n\nAs a result, all three statements are true."}] [{:db/id 17592186348355 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id 17592186054815 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "abc6b3ee-0251-5c9c-9358-bd192c434dd1" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Elements Belonging to Scripted Special Sets"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0e90c862-52fa-53ae-a511-041dd30973d5" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id 17592186345471} {:answer/id #uuid "70695b23-b4f5-57e1-a251-2611eda9c0fb" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id 17592186346335} {:answer/id #uuid "8bb85813-7338-5ff8-b6af-f63556700604" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id 17592186346560} {:answer/id #uuid "9df684a2-6582-5874-8681-182c9e2f57d1" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id 17592186346726} {:answer/id #uuid "c84265bd-f49e-5c00-ad61-a384f9c4731b" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id 17592186347094}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "8bb85813-7338-5ff8-b6af-f63556700604" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id 17592186346560} :answer-field/id #uuid "48a9cb3a-b57d-5c72-a70c-bb2c904ed902" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id 17592186347726}] :question/difficulty {:db/id 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "ac020dea-1486-5e82-bd01-29594c8fbf7a" :question/math-academy-id "q-80300" :question/problem "Which of the numbers below belong to  ${R}_{0}^{+}?$\n\n- $0$\n- $\\sqrt{18}$\n- $-2\\sqrt{5}$\n- $-\\frac{1}{5}$" :question/worked-solution "Remember that ${R}_{0}^{+}$ is the set of all positive real numbers and $0.$\n\n- $0$ belongs to ${R}_{0}^{+}.\\,✓$\n\n- $\\sqrt{18}=3\\sqrt{2}$ belongs to ${R}_{0}^{+}.\\,✓$\n\n- $-2\\sqrt{5}$ is a negative number, so it does *not* belong to ${R}_{0}^{+}.\\,\\times $\n\n- $-\\frac{1}{5}$ is a negative number, so it does *not* belong to ${R}_{0}^{+}.\\,\\times $\n\nIn conclusion, the correct answer is \"I and II only\"."}] [{:db/id 17592186348364 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id 17592186054814 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "3de9e532-559c-5fc1-a6ad-5fcd1ed77824" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Special Sets Containing a Given Element"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "32c05171-c3b9-54f4-92fe-1b24f8656f7d" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$Z,Q,R,$ and $C$ only" :db/id 17592186345793} {:answer/id #uuid "6bc1bb18-42f7-58d3-8976-ee79cc749dab" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$N,Z,Q,R,$ and $C$" :db/id 17592186346285} {:answer/id #uuid "9cbb60f9-e1d8-5e89-b64d-0065b660e30e" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$Z$ and $Q$ only" :db/id 17592186346717} {:answer/id #uuid "c5aff4da-b533-5efb-9001-708ceead8cf0" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$Q$ only" :db/id 17592186347070} {:answer/id #uuid "e3fe8bae-f57f-50f2-85f5-b8f34e5878dc" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$C$ only" :db/id 17592186347356}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "32c05171-c3b9-54f4-92fe-1b24f8656f7d" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$Z,Q,R,$ and $C$ only" :db/id 17592186345793} :answer-field/id #uuid "58e91bd7-048e-5613-a43a-b46d76068497" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id 17592186347760}] :question/difficulty {:db/id 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "39280445-a454-56b4-bdba-1c7ee371d8c7" :question/math-academy-id "q-80331" :question/problem "Consider the following sets:\n\n$$\nN,\\,Z,\\,Q,\\,R,\\,C\n$$\n\nTo which of these sets does the number $-\\frac{27}{3}$ belong?" :question/worked-solution "![](images/67/67ef97be4b21a74feaae15f3ff30d5077fc1316874df8de79155316a621e4487.png)\n\nRecall the following:\n\n- $N$ represents the set of **natural numbers**.\n\n- $Z$ represents the set of **integers**.\n\n- $Q$ represents the set of **rational numbers**.\n\n- $R$ represents the set of **real numbers**.\n\n- $C$ represents the set of **complex numbers**\n\nWith that in mind, we note the following:\n\n- The number $-\\frac{27}{3}=-9$ is negative, so it does *not* belong to $N,$ and we can write\n\n$$\n-\\frac{27}{3}\\notin N.\n$$\n\n- However, $-9$ is an integer, so it does belong to $Z,$ and we can write\n\n$$\n-\\frac{27}{3}\\in Z.\n$$\n\n- Every integer is a rational, real, and complex number. Consequently, $-\\frac{27}{3}$ belongs to $Q,R,$ and $C,$ and we can write\n\n$$\n-\\frac{27}{3}\\in Q,\\,-\\frac{27}{3}\\in R,\\,-\\frac{27}{3}\\in C.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"$Z,Q,R,$ and $C$ only.\""}] [{:db/id 17592186348365 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id 17592186054814 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "3de9e532-559c-5fc1-a6ad-5fcd1ed77824" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Special Sets Containing a Given Element"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0d72cc16-0cdd-5a82-95c0-0c5a7e18d862" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$Q,R,$ and $C$ only" :db/id 17592186345457} {:answer/id #uuid "2260ddfd-4e1e-554a-9431-88496fb06cc9" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$R$ and $C$ only" :db/id 17592186345660} {:answer/id #uuid "2e0d8997-757e-5de6-9d3a-88e9689b4e49" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$Z$ and $Q$ only" :db/id 17592186345759} {:answer/id #uuid "570fea6b-31e2-55d1-88f5-684ddc560514" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$Z,Q,R,$ and $C$ only" :db/id 17592186346092} {:answer/id #uuid "daf22f28-642d-5233-823a-ab90e42bc579" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$R$ only" :db/id 17592186347275}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "2260ddfd-4e1e-554a-9431-88496fb06cc9" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$R$ and $C$ only" :db/id 17592186345660} :answer-field/id #uuid "b017d8a4-b954-58df-b2f2-e6f75445868d" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id 17592186347909}] :question/difficulty {:db/id 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "3c87cce5-2762-52a9-b3ea-307f7f887046" :question/math-academy-id "q-80332" :question/problem "Consider the following sets:\n\n$$\nN,\\,Z,\\,Q,\\,R,\\,C\n$$\n\nTo which of these sets does the number $\\pi $ belong?" :question/worked-solution "![](images/67/67ef97be4b21a74feaae15f3ff30d5077fc1316874df8de79155316a621e4487.png)\n\nRecall the following:\n\n- $N$ represents the set of **natural numbers**.\n\n- $Z$ represents the set of **integers**.\n\n- $Q$ represents the set of **rational numbers**.\n\n- $R$ represents the set of **real numbers**.\n\n- $C$ represents the set of **complex numbers**\n\nWith that in mind, we note the following:\n\n- The number $\\pi $ is not an integer, so it does *not* belong to $Z$ nor to $N,$ so we can write\n\n$$\n\\pi \\notin N,\\,\\pi \\notin Z.\n$$\n\n- Also, notice that $\\pi =3.14159\\,…\\,$ can not be written as the ratio of two integers, so it does *not* belong to $Q$ either, and we can write \n\n$$\n\\pi \\notin Q.\n$$\n\n- Every irrational number is both a real number and a complex number. Consequently, $\\pi $ belongs to both $R$ and $C,$ and we can write\n\n$$\n\\pi \\in R,\\,\\pi \\in C.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"$R$ and $C$ only.\""}] [{:db/id 17592186348366 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id 17592186054815 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "abc6b3ee-0251-5c9c-9358-bd192c434dd1" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Elements Belonging to Scripted Special Sets"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0fd19e50-dcf1-57e6-af8f-a79caed15068" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id 17592186345483} {:answer/id #uuid "362253c0-52df-54f3-87e5-3cb927d6684c" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id 17592186345820} {:answer/id #uuid "6e4f80fb-60c0-5f01-a9a4-2f9d0a5b66fc" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id 17592186346317} {:answer/id #uuid "7d100010-5313-5757-b8f2-f58a985a6e12" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III and IV only" :db/id 17592186346444} {:answer/id #uuid "cf63fde6-700f-560f-9496-3bb2b62d87e1" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, II, and IV only" :db/id 17592186347165}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "cf63fde6-700f-560f-9496-3bb2b62d87e1" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, II, and IV only" :db/id 17592186347165} :answer-field/id #uuid "fcd7d946-b6ec-5d70-9edc-cd562cf24275" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id 17592186348046}] :question/difficulty {:db/id 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "4eae16cb-f514-5e15-a012-1e904a842a1d" :question/math-academy-id "q-80338" :question/problem "Which of the numbers below belong to  ${Z}_{0}^{-}?$\n\n- $-15$\n- $0$\n- $-\\pi $\n- $-\\frac{27}{3}$" :question/worked-solution "Remember that ${Z}_{0}^{-}$ is the set of all negative integer numbers and $0.$\n\n- $-15$ belongs to ${Z}_{0}^{-}.\\,✓$\n\n- $0$ belongs to ${Z}_{0}^{-}.\\,✓$\n\n- $-\\pi $ is not an integer, so it does *not* belong to ${Z}_{0}^{-}.\\,\\times $\n\n- $-\\frac{27}{3}=-9$ belongs to ${Z}_{0}^{-}.\\,✓$\n\nIn conclusion, the correct answer is \"I, II, and IV only\"."}] [{:db/id 17592186348399 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id 17592186152199 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "624215ff-efdc-5b12-8d61-9d66371672d9" :knowledge-point/title "Determining a Valid Region to Define a Double Integral Over a Non-Rectangular Domain"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2ab571c8-0222-59a1-92c8-ddf950978b89" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id 17592186345731} {:answer/id #uuid "36b6e878-5943-5e7e-933e-9eb7b2359885" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id 17592186345822} {:answer/id #uuid "7063b069-ce4f-59bf-91e9-83a857ab57a4" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id 17592186346334} {:answer/id #uuid "896ec991-3b3b-5531-a53b-a8b09fb7a789" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id 17592186346543} {:answer/id #uuid "a3aff3f8-307b-5065-8e34-3dd795dcc49c" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id 17592186346771}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "7063b069-ce4f-59bf-91e9-83a857ab57a4" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id 17592186346334} :answer-field/id #uuid "40a0bf72-11b0-5231-a068-fef16b3f8fb6" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id 17592186347714}] :question/difficulty {:db/id 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "fdfb4105-c8ab-5347-92cf-89faf417f305" :question/math-academy-id "q-80503" :question/problem "![](images/9d/9d4a0a08513d817fe841f1e45e5d363d6e64241837dea71f38aef452f86b08a7.png)\n\nConsider the region $D$ shown above. If \n\n$$\nf(x,y)={\\begin{aligned}\\frac{3{y}^{2}}{x}, & (x,y)\\in R\\cap D \\\\ 0, & (x,y)\\in R∖D\\end{aligned}\\,\\text{and}\\,{∬}_{D}\\frac{3{y}^{2}}{x}\\,\\text{d}A={∬}_{R}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}A,\n$$\n\nwhich of the following could be the definition of the rectangular domain $R?$\n\n- $R=[0,1]\\times [-1,1]$\n- $R=[0,2]\\times [-1,1]$\n- $R=[0,2]\\times [0,1]$" :question/worked-solution "Notice that if $R$ covers $D$ completely and we define \n\n$$\nf(x,y)={\\begin{aligned}\\frac{3{y}^{2}}{x}, & (x,y)\\in R\\cap D \\\\ 0, & (x,y)\\in R∖D,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwe obtain\n\n$$\n{∬}_{D}\\frac{3{y}^{2}}{x}\\,\\text{d}A={∬}_{R}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each of the given rectangles.\n\n- Rectangles I and III do not cover the region $D.$\n\n- Rectangle II covers the region $D$ completely.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II only.\""}] [{:db/id 17592186348410 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id 17592186152198 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "0ad76e6b-acef-5221-8852-71dc42625bf3" :knowledge-point/title "Determining a Correct Function Used to Define a Double Integral Over a Non-Rectangular Domain"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "415bb700-ad37-5b82-8f47-9fbd2f758c2e" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id 17592186345908} {:answer/id #uuid "957eeaba-b453-56e9-83a4-66194e19c6c1" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id 17592186346655} {:answer/id #uuid "a6c395b8-00cc-5a0d-8bfb-d4a91c203fff" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id 17592186346806} {:answer/id #uuid "dd801d1a-f53a-5e1e-a862-90c86397a850" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id 17592186347297} {:answer/id #uuid "f8290031-63e9-596d-b94a-459649d47293" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id 17592186347549}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "dd801d1a-f53a-5e1e-a862-90c86397a850" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id 17592186347297} :answer-field/id #uuid "5539bcfd-a712-5e1a-a393-3af3955fb0d0" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id 17592186347758}] :question/difficulty {:db/id 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "3b7813ca-41d9-58f5-aaf1-c07ba10ad235" :question/math-academy-id "q-80540" :question/problem "![](images/50/506a298041a875549ec60c5cf99ebdbeb5ca4b5717037083fa6ca196fce27279.png)\n\nConsider the region $D$ shown above. If $R=[-2,2]\\times [0,3],$ and\n\n$$\n{∬}_{D}\\frac{2x+y}{y}\\,\\text{d}A={∬}_{R}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}A,\n$$\n\nwhich of the following could be the definition of $f(x,y)?$\n\nI. $\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{2x+y}{y}, & (x,y)\\in D \\\\ 0, & (x,y)\\notin D\\end{aligned}$\n\nII. $\\{\\begin{aligned}0, & (x,y)\\in R \\\\ \\frac{2x+y}{y}, & (x,y)\\in D∖R\\end{aligned}$\n\nIII. $\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{2x+y}{y}, & (x,y)\\in R\\cap D \\\\ 0, & (x,y)\\in R∖D\\end{aligned}$" :question/worked-solution "First, let's draw our region $D$ and rectangle $R:$\n\n![](images/d2/d2b5c55b15f594680e8f299899d690cd1d92baa1ebef31ce5c79af52afec631e.png)\n\nNotice that the rectangle $R$ contains the region $D$ completely. \n\nNow, if we define $f(x,y)$ to be equal $\\frac{2x+y}{y}$ on $D$ and to be equal $0$ outside $D,$ we obtain\n\n$$\n{∬}_{D}\\frac{2x+y}{y}\\,\\text{d}A={∬}_{R}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each of the given functions.\n\n- We cannot use function II. Since $D∖R$ is empty, the function $f(x,y)$ will be identical to zero for all points in $R,$ including $D.$ But $\\frac{2x+y}{y}$ is not zero, for example, at $(x,y)=(0,2)\\in D.$\n\n- Functions I and III equals $\\frac{2x+y}{y}$ on $D$ and equals $0$ outside $D.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and III only.\""}] [{:db/id 17592186348412 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id 17592186152200 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "100ab73e-3dad-5b15-9da2-d244c67bcc9b" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying True Statements About Double Integrals Over Non-Rectangular Domains"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "4b2ac7da-ff30-54c5-b6e2-aae330f5d815" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id 17592186345995} {:answer/id #uuid "642ab18c-db32-58d2-9487-d906eb8a09dd" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id 17592186346221} {:answer/id #uuid "c543c12b-3186-52e1-9a03-d75929d934fe" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id 17592186347060} {:answer/id #uuid "cc9fe716-1e3a-5749-b7ca-0dc99f045f8e" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id 17592186347140} {:answer/id #uuid "f57afc4f-4d6e-5463-bc5d-44911cca7c7a" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id 17592186347525}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "c543c12b-3186-52e1-9a03-d75929d934fe" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id 17592186347060} :answer-field/id #uuid "d06deca5-843d-5a3c-854a-20223b1e00e3" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id 17592186347967}] :question/difficulty {:db/id 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "63f1d1b0-1722-552b-83dc-40f0511e40cd" :question/math-academy-id "q-80566" :question/problem "![](images/d9/d92fdb93f7564e1c205ab8603945eb6704c03db92c46130810e2b7cbce9a0e20.png)\n\nConsider the region $D$ shown above. If \n\n$$\nf(x,y)={\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}+xy & (x,y)\\in R\\cap D \\\\ 0, & (x,y)\\in R∖D,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \nwhich of the following statements are true?\n\n- ${∬}_{R}({x}^{2}+xy)\\,\\text{d}A={∬}_{D}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}A,\\,$ where $R=[0,2]\\times [-1,1]$\n- ${∬}_{D}({x}^{2}+xy)\\,\\text{d}A={∬}_{R}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}A,\\,$ where $R=[0,2]\\times [-2,2]$\n- ${∬}_{R}({x}^{2}+xy)\\,\\text{d}A={∬}_{D}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}A,\\,$ where $R=[0,3]\\times [-2,2]$" :question/worked-solution "Notice that if $R$ covers $D$ completely and we define \n\n$$\nf(x,y)={\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}+xy, & (x,y)\\in R\\cap D \\\\ 0, & (x,y)\\in R∖D,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwe obtain \n\n$$\n{∬}_{D}({x}^{2}+xy)\\,\\text{d}A={∬}_{R}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each of the given statements.\n\n- Statement I is false. The rectangle $R$ does not cover the region $D.$ Also, the integrals have not been stated correctly with respect to the domains $R$ and $D$ (they have been swapped).\n\n- Statement II is true. The rectangle $R$ covers the region $D$ completely.\n\n- Statement III is false. The integrals have not been stated correctly with respect to the domains $R$ and $D$ (they have been swapped).\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II only.\""}] [{:db/id 17592186348439 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id 17592186172016 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "d3e3fdc8-67d0-5b46-9c6b-c1ec8d4ea24f" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying an Equation That Generates a Slope Field Using Asymptotes"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "19716e5e-ba54-5ec5-aac9-d4f95db070b0" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, II, and III" :db/id 17592186345574} {:answer/id #uuid "2e7dac0f-eef7-5d67-8853-4e787af7a6cf" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id 17592186345764} {:answer/id #uuid "6cdec95f-c107-5b0b-9fd7-1869bea4b221" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id 17592186346299} {:answer/id #uuid "7123722a-1a3d-5dbd-a2b7-5a7a3b7d5da8" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id 17592186346350} {:answer/id #uuid "eb32eb91-0ee4-557a-aea9-d4d65ab4a47c" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id 17592186347426}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "7123722a-1a3d-5dbd-a2b7-5a7a3b7d5da8" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id 17592186346350} :answer-field/id #uuid "68305d18-bdb4-5169-b0ea-eb6ba6cac0bc" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id 17592186347783}] :question/difficulty {:db/id 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "50a7324b-9111-57a6-8ac7-64efd93f7ba4" :question/math-academy-id "q-80714" :question/problem "![](images/fd/fd7e9afcccbbb635d6bde139697aec0cbaa4bad486ea8b098e96f74bfbefebc9.png)\n\nWhich of the following equations could have generated the slope field shown above?\n\n- ${y}^{′}=\\frac{1}{y}$\n- ${y}^{′}=\\frac{1}{x}$\n- ${y}^{′}=\\frac{2}{y-1}$" :question/worked-solution "![](images/28/28c4751694caf50f08f73d7ccf3525877e6679901d49854951810f550a65ede2.png)\n\n \n\nNotice the following properties of the given slope field:\n\n- First, the slope lines on the same horizontal line are all the same. This means that the slope does not vary with $x.$ In other words, ${y}^{′}$ is a function of $y$ only.\n\n- Second, the slopes become vertical as $y\\to 1.$\n\nWith that in mind, let's consider each equation.\n\n- Equation I does *not* give the required behavior. The right-hand side of ${y}^{′}=\\frac{1}{y}$ is a function of $y$ only, but\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=\\frac{1}{y}\\to 1\\,\\text{as}\\,y\\to 1.\n$$\n\n- Equation II does *not* give the required behavior. The right-hand side of ${y}^{′}=\\frac{1}{x}$ is a function of $x$ only.\n\n- Equation III gives the required behavior. The right-hand side of ${y}^{′}=\\frac{2}{y-1}$ is a function of $y$ only, and \n\n$$\n|{y}^{′}|=|\\frac{2}{y-1}|\\to \\infty \\,\\text{as}\\,y\\to 1.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"III only.\""}] [{:db/id 17592186348444 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id 17592186124450 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "0b15f59e-ef74-5fa2-9af9-448fdcbbbd3d" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Possible Pairs of First-Order Partial Derivatives"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0544bc80-1365-5632-a296-a05da1bafe03" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id 17592186345398} {:answer/id #uuid "0c64741f-299e-5460-8811-c671d2a5a6d4" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id 17592186345448} {:answer/id #uuid "1798485b-0c6a-5572-a030-a13c598db229" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id 17592186345559} {:answer/id #uuid "274cde2d-e1fe-56af-8cd0-7813c0a484e9" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id 17592186345704} {:answer/id #uuid "2ed5a70b-0dfc-5371-a511-84ae013300d6" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id 17592186345766}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "0544bc80-1365-5632-a296-a05da1bafe03" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id 17592186345398} :answer-field/id #uuid "cf3548ed-65ef-581d-81b0-85e927603b7e" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id 17592186347963}] :question/difficulty {:db/id 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "22141837-7250-500e-8f49-e3a949023800" :question/math-academy-id "q-80753" :question/problem "Suppose that the function $f(x,y)$ and all of its first and second-order partial derivatives are continuous on an open set $U.$ Which of the following pairs could represent the first-order partial derivatives of $f(x,y)?$\n\n- ${f}_{x}(x,y)=2x{y}^{2}-y$ and ${f}_{y}(x,y)=2{x}^{2}y-2x$\n- ${f}_{x}(x,y)=yx{e}^{x}+{y}^{2}$ and ${f}_{y}(x,y)=(x-1){e}^{x}+2xy$\n- ${f}_{x}(x,y)=x-y$ and ${f}_{y}(x,y)=\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡y}-x$" :question/worked-solution "Since the function and its first and second-order partial derivatives are continuous on an open set $U,$ the mixed partial derivatives must be equal, that is, ${f}_{xy}(x,y)={f}_{yx}(x,y).$ Let's check if this condition is true for each of our pairs of first-order partial derivatives.\n\n- For the first pair, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{xy}(x,y) & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(2x{y}^{2}-y)=4xy-1 \\\\ {f}_{yx}(x,y) & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(2{x}^{2}y-2x)=4xy-2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAs ${f}_{xy}(x,y)\\ne {f}_{yx}(x,y)$, the first pair cannot represent the first-order partial derivatives of $f(x,y).$\n\n- For the second pair, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{xy}(x,y) & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(yx{e}^{x}+{y}^{2})=x{e}^{x}+2y \\\\ {f}_{yx}(x,y) & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}((x-1){e}^{x}+2xy)=x{e}^{x}+2y.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAs ${f}_{xy}(x,y)={f}_{yx}(x,y)$, the second pair could represent the first-order partial derivatives of $f(x,y).$\n\n- For the third pair, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{xy}(x,y) & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(x-y)=-1 \\\\ {f}_{yx}(x,y) & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡y}-x)=-1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAs ${f}_{xy}(x,y)={f}_{yx}(x,y)$,  the third pair could represent the first-order partial derivatives of $f(x,y).$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is 'II and III only'."}] [{:db/id 17592186348445 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id 17592186124450 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "0b15f59e-ef74-5fa2-9af9-448fdcbbbd3d" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Possible Pairs of First-Order Partial Derivatives"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "073fdb5d-0bf0-53ec-9b05-581d48c4f32d" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id 17592186345415} {:answer/id #uuid "1278b104-5c6b-5747-a4cb-f788b7ad603e" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id 17592186345510} {:answer/id #uuid "19da4515-ce77-55fb-8320-4d341a02db44" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id 17592186345577} {:answer/id #uuid "36144c74-e587-583a-b129-66bce59d2bcc" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id 17592186345818} {:answer/id #uuid "d096879f-ad55-5525-8639-33cc1186b3f4" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, II and III" :db/id 17592186347179}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "36144c74-e587-583a-b129-66bce59d2bcc" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id 17592186345818} :answer-field/id #uuid "5de9ecf8-1b93-55b8-b084-fe8277d7e5e1" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id 17592186347767}] :question/difficulty {:db/id 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "0fedf8ed-0c48-5105-b489-f0efda76d273" :question/math-academy-id "q-80759" :question/problem "Suppose that the function $f(x,y)$ and all of its first and second-order partial derivatives are continuous on an open set $U.$ Which of the following pairs could represent the first-order partial derivatives of $f(x,y)?$\n\n- ${f}_{x}(x,y)={e}^{-2x}-y$ and ${f}_{y}(x,y)={e}^{-y}-2x$\n- ${f}_{x}(x,y)={e}^{x}\\operatorname{cos}⁡x$ and ${f}_{y}(x,y)=5$\n- ${f}_{x}(x,y)=2{y}^{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡xy+{y}^{2}$ and ${f}_{y}(x,y)=2\\operatorname{sin}⁡xy+2xy\\operatorname{cos}⁡xy+2xy$" :question/worked-solution "Since the function and its first and second-order partial derivatives are continuous on an open set $U,$ the mixed partial derivatives must be equal, that is, ${f}_{xy}(x,y)={f}_{yx}(x,y).$ Let's check if this condition is true for each of our pairs of first-order partial derivatives.\n\n- For the first pair, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{xy}(x,y) & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}({e}^{-2x}-y)=-1 \\\\ {f}_{yx}(x,y) & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}({e}^{-y}-2x)=-2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAs ${f}_{xy}(x,y)\\ne {f}_{yx}(x,y)$, the first pair cannot represent the first-order partial derivatives of $f(x,y).$\n\n- For the second pair, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{xy}(x,y) & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}({e}^{x}\\operatorname{cos}⁡x)=0 \\\\ {f}_{yx}(x,y) & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(5)=0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAs ${f}_{xy}(x,y)={f}_{yx}(x,y)$, the second pair could represent the first-order partial derivatives of $f(x,y).$\n\n- For the third pair, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{xy}(x,y) & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(2{y}^{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡xy+{y}^{2})=4y\\operatorname{cos}⁡xy-2x{y}^{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡xy+2y \\\\ {f}_{yx}(x,y) & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(2\\operatorname{sin}⁡xy+2xy\\operatorname{cos}⁡xy+2xy) \\\\  & =2y\\operatorname{cos}⁡xy+2y\\operatorname{cos}⁡xy-2x{y}^{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡xy+2y \\\\  & =4y\\operatorname{cos}⁡xy-2x{y}^{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡xy+2y.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAs ${f}_{xy}(x,y)={f}_{yx}(x,y)$,  the third pair could represent the first-order partial derivatives of $f(x,y).$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is 'II and III only'."}] [{:db/id 17592186351159 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id 17592186054631 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "9b7f1d12-3335-5dd7-bf36-e07077d0c45d" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Subsets of Special Sets"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1d220af7-b57f-591a-a08b-ba4c9c9644fe" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id 17592186348704} {:answer/id #uuid "5132899a-7600-511a-a618-c5063acd3327" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id 17592186349183} {:answer/id #uuid "75567262-237d-5498-b953-727d472667e2" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id 17592186349525} {:answer/id #uuid "8d5a9ae5-0a7b-54b5-891e-fe7efa13bf62" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id 17592186349720} {:answer/id #uuid "ec5b6a83-4ce3-5742-be34-0fd6300460ae" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id 17592186350509}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "ec5b6a83-4ce3-5742-be34-0fd6300460ae" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id 17592186350509} :answer-field/id #uuid "f507049a-991f-52c9-ad38-6d7ca2a3e35a" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id 17592186351138}] :question/difficulty {:db/id 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "94ae6b8c-148f-5682-9bab-a030784afd5c" :question/math-academy-id "q-80812" :question/problem "Which of the following are subsets of ${Q}^{+}$  (the set of positive rational numbers)?\n\n- ${Q}_{0}^{+}$ (the set of non-negative rational numbers)\n- $N$\n- $Z$" :question/worked-solution "$A$ is a subset of $B$ if every element of $A$ is an element of $B.$\n\nWith that in mind, let's inspect the given choices.\n\n- ${Q}_{0}^{+}$ is not a subset of ${Q}^{+}.$ For example, $0\\in {Q}_{0}^{+}$ but $0\\notin {Q}^{+}.$\n\n- $N$ is a subset of ${Q}^{+}.$ Indeed, every natural number is also a positive rational number.\n\n- $Z$ is not a subset of ${Q}^{+}.$ For example, $-1\\in Z$ but $-1\\notin {Q}^{+}.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II only\"."}] [{:db/id 17592186351166 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id 17592186054631 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "9b7f1d12-3335-5dd7-bf36-e07077d0c45d" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Subsets of Special Sets"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "24938232-5aed-5ef7-868f-3610f1574f06" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id 17592186348761} {:answer/id #uuid "52f7e6f9-0dc1-5f37-aa2b-ce91f653f868" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id 17592186349204} {:answer/id #uuid "6fe3917b-35bb-53d3-9e39-fbdecaab7db7" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id 17592186349474} {:answer/id #uuid "815915f5-5eeb-56e7-a654-4d3780987757" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id 17592186349638} {:answer/id #uuid "a7ac2e85-7507-5564-9526-b54e6454e621" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id 17592186349939}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "a7ac2e85-7507-5564-9526-b54e6454e621" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id 17592186349939} :answer-field/id #uuid "8fb53a1e-28dc-541c-9aef-9910f92f7876" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id 17592186350951}] :question/difficulty {:db/id 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "f854e867-6b9f-52a2-9a74-9314a61b1db2" :question/math-academy-id "q-80829" :question/problem "Which of the following are subsets of ${R}^{-}$ (the set of negative real numbers)?\n\n- ${Z}_{0}^{-}$ (the set of non-positive integers)\n- $N$\n- ${Q}^{-}$  (the set of negative rational numbers)" :question/worked-solution "$A$ is a subset of $B$ if every element of $A$ is an element of $B.$\n\nWith that in mind, let's inspect the given choices.\n\n- ${Z}_{0}^{-}$ is *not* a subset of ${R}^{-}.$ For example, $0\\in {Z}_{0}^{-}$ but $0\\notin {R}^{-}.$\n\n- $N$ is not  a subset of ${R}^{-}.$ For example, $1\\in N$ but $1\\notin {R}^{-}.$\n\n- ${Q}^{-}$ is a subset of ${R}^{-}.$ Indeed, every negative rational number is also a negative real number.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"III only\"."}] [{:db/id 17592186351223 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id 17592186133781 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "ed5c8af7-e617-5e8c-be01-45a26eb17d4d" :knowledge-point/title "Determining Whether Sets are Orthonormal"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "29720cd0-05b4-5b72-aa7f-f9e9adfe346c" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$V$ and $W$ only" :db/id 17592186348808} {:answer/id #uuid "572eb01c-3fd8-52d3-92f3-8f8417886a96" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$U$ and $V$ only" :db/id 17592186349247} {:answer/id #uuid "aef735ba-fa87-5c65-bc31-399cb2154f9c" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$U$ and $W$ only" :db/id 17592186349993} {:answer/id #uuid "bf771832-5ba4-5289-94b3-720cfc8a578c" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$W$ only" :db/id 17592186350148} {:answer/id #uuid "f0abb535-abdc-5adc-b02e-f70943d024cd" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$V$ only" :db/id 17592186350553}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "29720cd0-05b4-5b72-aa7f-f9e9adfe346c" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$V$ and $W$ only" :db/id 17592186348808} :answer-field/id #uuid "a0d977f1-8559-526b-8a29-72ca4192caf7" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id 17592186350980}] :question/difficulty {:db/id 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "25a9c5a2-c2b4-580a-a454-817fa98a8c5d" :question/math-academy-id "q-81109" :question/problem "Which of the following sets are orthonormal?\n\nI. $U=\\{[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -5 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ -2 \\\\ 4\\end{aligned}]\\}$\n\nII. $V=\\{[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{13}} \\\\ \\frac{3}{\\sqrt{13}}\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{3}{\\sqrt{13}} \\\\ \\frac{2}{\\sqrt{13}}\\end{aligned}]\\}$\n\nIII. $W=\\{[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{6}} \\\\ \\frac{2}{\\sqrt{6}} \\\\ \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{6}}\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}} \\\\ -\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}} \\\\ \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}}\\end{aligned}]\\}$" :question/worked-solution "Let's denote the vectors of the given sets as \n$\\{{u}_{1},{u}_{2},{u}_{3}\\},$ \n$\\{{v}_{1},{v}_{2}\\},$ and \n$\\{{w}_{1},{w}_{2}\\},$ respectively. \n\nThe sets $U,V,$ and $W$ are orthonormal if the following conditions on each set hold:\n\n- $\\{\\begin{aligned}{u}_{1}\\cdot {u}_{2}=0 \\\\ {u}_{1}\\cdot {u}_{3}=0 \\\\ {u}_{2}\\cdot {u}_{3}=0\\end{aligned}\\,\\text{and}\\,\\{\\begin{aligned}{u}_{1}\\cdot {u}_{1}=1 \\\\ {u}_{2}\\cdot {u}_{2}=1 \\\\ {u}_{3}\\cdot {u}_{3}=1.\\end{aligned}$\n\n- ${v}_{1}\\cdot {v}_{2}=0\\,\\text{and}\\,\\{\\begin{aligned}{v}_{1}\\cdot {v}_{1}=1 \\\\ {v}_{2}\\cdot {v}_{2}=1.\\end{aligned}$\n\n- $\\{\\begin{aligned}{w}_{1}\\cdot {w}_{2}=0 \\\\ {w}_{1}\\cdot {w}_{3}=0 \\\\ {w}_{2}\\cdot {w}_{3}=0\\end{aligned}\\,\\text{and}\\,\\{\\begin{aligned}{w}_{1}\\cdot {w}_{1}=1 \\\\ {w}_{2}\\cdot {w}_{2}=1 \\\\ {w}_{3}\\cdot {w}_{3}=1.\\end{aligned}$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our sets in turn.\n\n- For the set $U,$ notice that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{u}_{1}\\cdot {u}_{1} & =[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -5 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}]\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -5 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}]=1+25+4=30\\ne 1 & \\times \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, $U$ is not an orthonormal set.\n\n- For the set $V,$ we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{v}_{1}\\cdot {v}_{2} & =[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{13}} \\\\ \\frac{3}{\\sqrt{13}}\\end{aligned}]\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{3}{\\sqrt{13}} \\\\ \\frac{2}{\\sqrt{13}}\\end{aligned}]=-\\frac{6}{13}+\\frac{6}{13}=0 & ✓ \\\\ {v}_{1}\\cdot {v}_{1} & =[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{13}} \\\\ \\frac{3}{\\sqrt{13}}\\end{aligned}]\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{13}} \\\\ \\frac{3}{\\sqrt{13}}\\end{aligned}]=\\frac{4}{13}+\\frac{9}{13}=1 & ✓ \\\\ {v}_{2}\\cdot {v}_{2} & =[\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{3}{\\sqrt{13}} \\\\ \\frac{2}{\\sqrt{13}}\\end{aligned}]\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{3}{\\sqrt{13}} \\\\ \\frac{2}{\\sqrt{13}}\\end{aligned}]=\\frac{9}{13}+\\frac{4}{13}=1 & ✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the set $V$ is orthonormal.\n\n- For the set $W,$ we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{w}_{1}\\cdot {w}_{2} & =[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{6}} \\\\ \\frac{2}{\\sqrt{6}} \\\\ \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{6}}\\end{aligned}]\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}} \\\\ -\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}} \\\\ \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}}\\end{aligned}]=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{18}}-\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{18}}+\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{18}}=0 & ✓ \\\\ {w}_{1}\\cdot {w}_{1} & =[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{6}} \\\\ \\frac{2}{\\sqrt{6}} \\\\ \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{6}}\\end{aligned}]\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{6}} \\\\ \\frac{2}{\\sqrt{6}} \\\\ \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{6}}\\end{aligned}]=\\frac{1}{6}+\\frac{4}{6}+\\frac{1}{6}=1 & ✓ \\\\ {w}_{2}\\cdot {w}_{2} & =[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}} \\\\ -\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}} \\\\ \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}}\\end{aligned}]\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}} \\\\ -\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}} \\\\ \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}}\\end{aligned}]=\\frac{1}{3}+\\frac{1}{3}+\\frac{1}{3}=1 & ✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the set $W$ is orthonormal.\n\nTherefore, we conclude that the correct answer is \"$V$ and $W$ only\"."}] [{:db/id 17592186351277 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id 17592186087750 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "7c9849b9-397b-5fb9-bb4b-01f3d013c5c2" :knowledge-point/title "Computing the Image of a Polygon Under the Action of a Linear Transformation"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0d7d9c1c-bf88-5f0b-ab2d-c5c9c4d612ff" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id 17592186348564} {:answer/id #uuid "5ec5fc3a-9023-535e-b535-de60051b88bc" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, II, and III" :db/id 17592186349316} {:answer/id #uuid "b20acc1a-e442-5157-b08f-5307d6e01a82" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id 17592186350020} {:answer/id #uuid "c1854549-a028-5302-a0de-a79e09d61628" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id 17592186350162} {:answer/id #uuid "d0f5c90e-f539-567a-a1fa-662727c04a7a" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id 17592186350283}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "b20acc1a-e442-5157-b08f-5307d6e01a82" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id 17592186350020} :answer-field/id #uuid "81b918ce-115c-50b8-9bbe-372163bf339d" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id 17592186350930}] :question/difficulty {:db/id 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "e67ff0be-cbbc-585a-82ab-87b00ade6bf8" :question/math-academy-id "q-8125" :question/problem "Consider the linear transformation $T$ with matrix representation $T,$ given by\n\n$$\nT=[\\begin{aligned}7 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -1\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nA triangle $S$ has vertices at $(-3,0),(3,0),$ and $(0,-3).$ Which of the following are coordinates of a vertex of ${S}^{′}$, the image of $S$ under the action of $T?$\n\n- $(21,0)$\n- $(3,0)$\n- $(-21,0)$" :question/worked-solution "To find the image of $S$ under the action of $T$, we first create a matrix $X$ containing all of the vertices of $S:$\n\n$$\nX=[\\begin{aligned}-3 & 3 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & -3\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nNow, we compute the image of $X$ under $T$ by calculating the matrix product $TX:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}TX & =[\\begin{aligned}7 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}-3 & 3 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & -3\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-21 & 21 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 3\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the coordinates of the respective vertices of ${S}^{′}$ are $(-21,0),(21,0),(0,3).$ \n\nSo, the correct answer is \"I and III only.\""}] [{:db/id 17592186351308 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id 17592186129321 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "359bc41e-1b6d-5177-9642-4f8697bc5ba0" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Rotation-Scaling Matrices"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1bb11024-8113-54af-b88b-ff7eb0e8d487" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id 17592186348683} {:answer/id #uuid "5bf3e0c5-e53c-54dd-858b-eeb6b7db18ea" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id 17592186349294} {:answer/id #uuid "60de92e4-81a6-526a-9488-4b70a6131316" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id 17592186349330} {:answer/id #uuid "674584d7-b562-55a7-a6e5-4684fdf59be1" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id 17592186349392} {:answer/id #uuid "9e3499b8-5683-5685-98b0-832b134ee579" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id 17592186349858}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "674584d7-b562-55a7-a6e5-4684fdf59be1" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id 17592186349392} :answer-field/id #uuid "7f10cfb6-5b91-5f36-8bf1-0d08c0816132" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id 17592186350926}] :question/difficulty {:db/id 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "92058ab0-e5fa-580e-91cc-9a3c064bb64d" :question/math-academy-id "q-81453" :question/problem "Which of the following are rotation-scaling matrices?\n\n- $A=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 \\\\ 2 & 1\\end{aligned}]$\n- $B=[\\begin{aligned}-2 & -5 \\\\ 5 & -2\\end{aligned}]$\n- $C=[\\begin{aligned}8 & 4 \\\\ -4 & -8\\end{aligned}]$" :question/worked-solution "A rotation-scaling matrix has the form \n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}a & -b \\\\ b & a\\end{aligned}],\n$$\n\n where $a$ and $b$ are real numbers that are not equal to zero simultaneously. Notice that the entries on the main diagonal are the same, while the antidiagonal entries have opposite signs.\n\nWith that in mind let's check each of the given matrices:\n\n- $A=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 \\\\ 2 & 1\\end{aligned}]$ is *not* a rotation-scaling matrix since the entries on the antidiagonal do not have opposite signs.\n\n- $B=[\\begin{aligned}-2 & -5 \\\\ 5 & -2\\end{aligned}]$ is a rotation-scaling matrix.\n\n- $C=[\\begin{aligned}8 & 4 \\\\ -4 & -8\\end{aligned}]$ is *not* a rotation-scaling matrix since the entries on the main diagonal are not the same.\n\nIn conclusion, the correct answer is \"II only.\""}] [{:db/id 17592186351309 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id 17592186129321 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "359bc41e-1b6d-5177-9642-4f8697bc5ba0" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Rotation-Scaling Matrices"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "005375ba-6420-5c41-9abd-3f2cab65879d" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id 17592186348457} {:answer/id #uuid "26fe961f-5722-5343-aefd-1431298c90b5" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id 17592186348783} {:answer/id #uuid "3c8d0ffa-7ca5-5f99-9ce7-626d55bb20ec" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id 17592186348997} {:answer/id #uuid "3d64d2eb-b1c4-5bb7-8eb9-6191f3312f0f" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id 17592186349003} {:answer/id #uuid "45e4bbc6-c997-5ab6-85d3-40285d433e08" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id 17592186349084}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "3c8d0ffa-7ca5-5f99-9ce7-626d55bb20ec" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id 17592186348997} :answer-field/id #uuid "533abd35-178d-563a-8d51-571d90c0d9f9" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id 17592186350849}] :question/difficulty {:db/id 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "e1ff6dbb-4ce0-5c14-8e12-348294b11875" :question/math-academy-id "q-81455" :question/problem "Which of the following are rotation-scaling matrices?\n\n- $A=[\\begin{aligned}-2 & 4 \\\\ -4 & 2\\end{aligned}]$\n- $B=[\\begin{aligned}2 & -\\frac{3}{4} \\\\ \\frac{3}{4} & 2\\end{aligned}]$\n- $C=[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{2} & 5 \\\\ -5 & \\frac{1}{2}\\end{aligned}]$" :question/worked-solution "A rotation-scaling matrix has the form \n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}a & -b \\\\ b & a\\end{aligned}],\n$$\n\n where $a$ and $b$ are real numbers that are not equal to zero simultaneously. Notice that the entries on the main diagonal are the same, while the antidiagonal entries have opposite signs.\n\nWith that in mind let's check each of the given matrices:\n\n- $A=[\\begin{aligned}-2 & 4 \\\\ -4 & 2\\end{aligned}]$ is *not* a rotation-scaling matrix since the entries on the main diagonal are not the same.\n\n- $B=[\\begin{aligned}2 & -\\frac{3}{4} \\\\ \\frac{3}{4} & 2\\end{aligned}]$ is a rotation-scaling matrix.\n\n- $C=[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{2} & 5 \\\\ -5 & \\frac{1}{2}\\end{aligned}]$ is a rotation-scaling matrix.\n\nIn conclusion, the correct answer is \"II and III only.\""}] [{:db/id 17592186351372 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id 17592186092224 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "20f4ef93-3e7b-58ec-992e-3a62b12b74e2" :knowledge-point/title "Using a Matrix That Represents a Rotation of 180 Degrees"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "17672d7b-1484-5a23-8f4a-1d0c8fe3f938" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id 17592186348645} {:answer/id #uuid "382f3226-220a-530e-82f8-7d3559509fc1" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id 17592186348955} {:answer/id #uuid "50edb2a8-35bd-5f50-ae59-221642a50ecd" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, II, and III" :db/id 17592186349181} {:answer/id #uuid "be022f7d-c15c-5e75-b6e4-ba243b9fec9c" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id 17592186350135} {:answer/id #uuid "fd0b2c51-7d75-5adf-9b9f-5f7dd3853c7d" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id 17592186350676}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "50edb2a8-35bd-5f50-ae59-221642a50ecd" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, II, and III" :db/id 17592186349181} :answer-field/id #uuid "73dc4853-74e7-55b1-96a6-789bd3846186" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id 17592186350907}] :question/difficulty {:db/id 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "77695064-c91d-5069-813d-3934cbdfcb63" :question/math-academy-id "q-81815" :question/problem "A triangle $S$ has vertices at $(4,1),(0,-3),(-1,3).$  Which of the following are coordinates of the vertices of ${S}^{′},$ the image of $S$ under the **clockwise** rotation ${R}_{-{180}^{∘}}$ by an angle of ${180}^{∘}$ about the origin?\n\n- $(1,-3)$\n- $(-4,-1)$\n- $(0,3)$" :question/worked-solution "The clockwise rotation ${R}_{-{180}^{∘}}$ by an angle of ${180}^{∘}$ is the same as counterclockwise rotation by the same angle. It's given by the matrix\n\n$$\nR=[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -1\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nTo find the image of $S$ under the action of ${R}_{-{180}^{∘}},$ we first create a matrix $X$ containing all of the vertices of $S:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}X & =[\\begin{aligned}4 & 0 & -1 \\\\ 1 & -3 & 3\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we compute the image of $X$ under ${R}_{-{180}^{∘}}$ by calculating the matrix product $RX:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}RX & =[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}4 & 0 & -1 \\\\ 1 & -3 & 3\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-4 & 0 & 1 \\\\ -1 & 3 & -3\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the coordinates of the respective vertices of ${S}^{′}$ are $(-4,-1),(0,3),(1,-3).$\n\nSo, the correct answer is \"I, II and III.\""}] [{:db/id 17592186351373 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id 17592186092224 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "20f4ef93-3e7b-58ec-992e-3a62b12b74e2" :knowledge-point/title "Using a Matrix That Represents a Rotation of 180 Degrees"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "23f6ec9b-64d4-5b66-b112-483123b77704" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id 17592186348758} {:answer/id #uuid "80305c3f-3cb3-5936-b5f7-f4fc8aedef7f" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id 17592186349621} {:answer/id #uuid "bc433bb3-6840-5b05-9a62-3e359a09e5c8" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id 17592186350120} {:answer/id #uuid "d2b124a2-4652-5b2e-8138-bc4fc05dcfbd" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id 17592186350297} {:answer/id #uuid "e014cf02-c5e1-5777-b832-4760fedc5219" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id 17592186350413}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "e014cf02-c5e1-5777-b832-4760fedc5219" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id 17592186350413} :answer-field/id #uuid "496c35da-9916-52cf-81c8-276ad715edd4" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id 17592186350831}] :question/difficulty {:db/id 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "231d9a8a-12f5-5ef7-842a-1be3e946bcb6" :question/math-academy-id "q-81816" :question/problem "A triangle $S$ has vertices at $(-3,0),(1,5),(2,-2).$  Which of the following are coordinates of the vertices of ${S}^{′},$ the image of $S$ under the **clockwise** rotation ${R}_{-{180}^{∘}}$ by an angle of ${180}^{∘}$ about the origin?\n\n- $(-2,2)$\n- $(0,3)$\n- $(-1,-5)$" :question/worked-solution "The clockwise rotation ${R}_{-{180}^{∘}}$ by an angle of ${180}^{∘}$ is the same as counterclockwise rotation by the same angle. It's given by the matrix\n\n$$\nR=[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -1\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nTo find the image of $S$ under the action of ${R}_{-{180}^{∘}},$ we first create a matrix $X$ containing all of the vertices of $S:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}X & =[\\begin{aligned}-3 & 1 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 5 & -2\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we compute the image of $X$ under ${R}_{-{180}^{∘}}$ by calculating the matrix product $RX:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}RX & =[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}-3 & 1 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 5 & -2\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}3 & -1 & -2 \\\\ 0 & -5 & 2\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the coordinates of the respective vertices of ${S}^{′}$ are $(3,0),(-1,-5),(-2,2).$\n\nSo, the correct answer is \"I and III only.\""}] [{:db/id 17592186351374 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id 17592186092224 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "20f4ef93-3e7b-58ec-992e-3a62b12b74e2" :knowledge-point/title "Using a Matrix That Represents a Rotation of 180 Degrees"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "679188a7-8de5-5326-9adb-756653c6721c" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id 17592186349400} {:answer/id #uuid "6909ea72-faa8-535d-9b38-8f1c80e97976" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id 17592186349414} {:answer/id #uuid "8f2b9d0b-aee0-5ddb-ab6f-4a943f07fb63" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id 17592186349735} {:answer/id #uuid "b7c1f16e-2893-5078-a617-c1dea4e3fc85" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id 17592186350073} {:answer/id #uuid "fec842f6-b4a4-5d26-8b17-cfcb78a2d728" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id 17592186350688}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "679188a7-8de5-5326-9adb-756653c6721c" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id 17592186349400} :answer-field/id #uuid "a65574ef-7b58-5bb2-954b-0dafdad6334f" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id 17592186350988}] :question/difficulty {:db/id 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "cedf0fcc-98d4-596a-ad79-42e7fd62dfba" :question/math-academy-id "q-81818" :question/problem "A triangle $S$ has vertices at $(-5,1),(0,-4),(3,5).$  Which of the following are coordinates of the vertices of ${S}^{′},$ the image of $S$ under the counterclockwise rotation ${R}_{\\pi }$ by an angle of $\\pi $ radians about the origin?\n\n- $(4,0)$\n- $(-3,-5)$\n- $(-1,5)$" :question/worked-solution "The counterclockwise rotation ${R}_{\\pi }$ by an angle of $\\pi $ radians is given by the matrix\n\n$$\nR=[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -1\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nTo find the image of $S$ under the action of ${R}_{\\pi },$ we first create a matrix $X$ containing all of the vertices of $S:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}X & =[\\begin{aligned}-5 & 0 & 3 \\\\ 1 & -4 & 5\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we compute the image of $X$ under ${R}_{\\pi }$ by calculating the matrix product $RX:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}RX & =[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}-5 & 0 & 3 \\\\ 1 & -4 & 5\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}5 & 0 & -3 \\\\ -1 & 4 & -5\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the coordinates of the respective vertices of ${S}^{′}$ are $(5,-1),(0,4),(-3,-5).$\n\nSo, the correct answer is \"II only.\""}] [{:db/id 17592186351375 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id 17592186092224 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "20f4ef93-3e7b-58ec-992e-3a62b12b74e2" :knowledge-point/title "Using a Matrix That Represents a Rotation of 180 Degrees"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "25878e3e-4660-56b1-aebc-5cf486c84d7d" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id 17592186348769} {:answer/id #uuid "4e92eff1-2f22-58c8-909d-3a69618c4e67" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id 17592186349159} {:answer/id #uuid "85db19be-aa9b-5ed6-999a-6d25c79e13bb" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id 17592186349669} {:answer/id #uuid "f179df73-8f35-56c5-8bc7-5ae38d792a66" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id 17592186350557} {:answer/id #uuid "faf2b2ad-b924-585c-afc9-e1870c44b16c" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id 17592186350657}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "faf2b2ad-b924-585c-afc9-e1870c44b16c" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id 17592186350657} :answer-field/id #uuid "31b5282d-1b98-5d48-8d34-e38fc8f72d36" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id 17592186350793}] :question/difficulty {:db/id 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "eca92846-b208-5714-9d9f-4947d539dbcc" :question/math-academy-id "q-81819" :question/problem "A triangle $S$ has vertices at $(4,4),(-3,-3),(-1,7).$ Which of the following are coordinates of the vertices of ${S}^{′},$ the image of $S$ under the counterclockwise rotation ${R}_{{180}^{∘}}$ by an angle of ${180}^{∘}$ about the origin?\n\n- $(3,-3)$\n- $(-4,-4)$\n- $(1,-7)$" :question/worked-solution "The counterclockwise rotation ${R}_{{180}^{∘}}$ by an angle of ${180}^{∘}$ is given by the matrix\n\n$$\nR=[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -1\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nTo find the image of $S$ under the action of ${R}_{{180}^{∘}},$ we first create a matrix $X$ containing all of the vertices of $S:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}X & =[\\begin{aligned}4 & -3 & -1 \\\\ 4 & -3 & 7\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we compute the image of $X$ under ${R}_{{180}^{∘}}$ by calculating the matrix product $RX:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}RX & =[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}4 & -3 & -1 \\\\ 4 & -3 & 7\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-4 & 3 & 1 \\\\ -4 & 3 & -7\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the coordinates of the respective vertices of ${S}^{′}$ are $(-4,-4),(3,3),(1,-7).$\n\nSo, the correct answer is \"II and III only.\""}] [{:db/id 17592186351421 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id 17592186133843 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "ee1838df-f1d6-5bc4-908c-b96e2ad577c9" :knowledge-point/title "Determining Whether Sets are Orthogonal"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0e08d1ad-f5c0-5097-9890-f0ab89b66086" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$U$ only" :db/id 17592186348566} {:answer/id #uuid "31280c8e-36d5-5edb-8935-400e163c6254" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$U$ and $W$ only" :db/id 17592186348882} {:answer/id #uuid "5b0ed777-5ab0-5b13-827c-7d063fe7e6c4" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$V$ only" :db/id 17592186349284} {:answer/id #uuid "d973a507-2ddd-5e50-833f-ee66a5642171" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$W$ only" :db/id 17592186350352} {:answer/id #uuid "ed5a03b2-59e7-58c6-9098-8c0824620a6f" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$V$ and $W$ only" :db/id 17592186350521}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "d973a507-2ddd-5e50-833f-ee66a5642171" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$W$ only" :db/id 17592186350352} :answer-field/id #uuid "f6188e16-8fdf-508e-9b0f-8d7f586a31a2" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id 17592186351142}] :question/difficulty {:db/id 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "0e6a1e4f-2315-5270-b1fa-4652e5933f97" :question/math-academy-id "q-82396" :question/problem "Which of the following sets are orthogonal?\n\n- $U={[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ -3 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}-5 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}-8 \\\\ -1 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}]}$\n- $V={[\\begin{aligned}-3 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}6 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]}$\n- $W={[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]}$" :question/worked-solution "Let's denote the vectors of the given sets as \n${{u}_{1},{u}_{2},{u}_{3}},$\n${{v}_{1},{v}_{2}},$ and \n${{w}_{1},{w}_{2},{w}_{3}},$ respectively. \n\nThe sets $U,V,$ and $W$ are orthogonal if the following conditions on each set hold:\n\n- ${u}_{1}\\cdot {u}_{2}=0,\\,{u}_{1}\\cdot {u}_{3}=0,\\,{u}_{2}\\cdot {u}_{3}=0.$\n\n- ${v}_{1}\\cdot {v}_{2}=0.$\n\n- ${w}_{1}\\cdot {w}_{2}=0,\\,{w}_{1}\\cdot {w}_{3}=0,\\,{w}_{2}\\cdot {w}_{3}=0.$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our sets in turn.\n\n- For the set $U,$ notice that \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{u}_{1}\\cdot {u}_{2} & =[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ -3 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}-5 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]=-10-3+0=-13\\ne 0 & \\times \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, $U$ is not an orthogonal set.\n\n- For the set $V,$ notice that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{v}_{1}\\cdot {v}_{2} & =[\\begin{aligned}-3 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}]\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}6 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]=-18-2=-20\\ne 0 & \\times \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, $V$ is not an orthogonal set.\n\n- For the set $W,$ we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{w}_{1}\\cdot {w}_{2} & =[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}]=2-1-1=0 & ✓ \\\\ {w}_{1}\\cdot {w}_{3} & =[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]=0-1+1=0 & ✓ \\\\ {w}_{2}\\cdot {w}_{3} & =[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}]\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]=0+1-1=0 & ✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the set $W$ is orthogonal.\n\nTherefore, we conclude that the correct answer is \"$W$ only\"."}] [{:db/id 17592186351422 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id 17592186133843 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "ee1838df-f1d6-5bc4-908c-b96e2ad577c9" :knowledge-point/title "Determining Whether Sets are Orthogonal"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1808b334-76b3-52df-a1a6-8b27cf9bde32" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$U$ and $V$ only" :db/id 17592186348649} {:answer/id #uuid "1f42a2b4-971f-5282-bafa-57f6edbf33b4" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$V$ only" :db/id 17592186348719} {:answer/id #uuid "95671ee1-ea34-5b9e-b055-16b7e130dd09" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$U$ and $W$ only" :db/id 17592186349788} {:answer/id #uuid "9f0bd696-9fc4-5142-916d-9086d9ae01bb" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$U$ only" :db/id 17592186349865} {:answer/id #uuid "ce07f604-e928-5e49-bbf0-cb8821612bd7" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$V$ and $W$ only" :db/id 17592186350259}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "1808b334-76b3-52df-a1a6-8b27cf9bde32" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$U$ and $V$ only" :db/id 17592186348649} :answer-field/id #uuid "248c40ff-0114-5468-a84e-a4a88929f614" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id 17592186350774}] :question/difficulty {:db/id 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "c7632fcb-d145-5958-9540-44c860cfa586" :question/math-academy-id "q-82397" :question/problem "Which of the following sets are orthogonal?\n\n- $U={[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 3 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]}$\n- $V={[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -1 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]}$\n- $W={[\\begin{aligned}5 \\\\ -3\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}-3 \\\\ -8\\end{aligned}]}$" :question/worked-solution "Let's denote the vectors of the given sets as \n${{u}_{1},{u}_{2},{u}_{3}},$ \n${{v}_{1},{v}_{2}},$ and \n${{w}_{1},{w}_{2}},$ respectively. \n\nThe sets $U,V,$ and $W$ are orthogonal if the following conditions on each set hold:\n\n- ${u}_{1}\\cdot {u}_{2}=0,\\,{u}_{1}\\cdot {u}_{3}=0,\\,{u}_{2}\\cdot {u}_{3}=0.$\n\n- ${v}_{1}\\cdot {v}_{2}=0.$\n\n- ${w}_{1}\\cdot {w}_{2}=0.$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our sets in turn.\n\n- For the set $U,$ we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{u}_{1}\\cdot {u}_{2} & =[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 3 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}]=-1+3-1-1=0 & ✓ \\\\ {u}_{1}\\cdot {u}_{3} & =[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 3 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]=1+0-2+1=0 & ✓ \\\\ {u}_{2}\\cdot {u}_{3} & =[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}]\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]=-1+0+2-1=0 & ✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the set $U$ is orthogonal.\n\n- For the set $V,$ we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{v}_{1}\\cdot {v}_{2} & =[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -1 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}]\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]=2-1-1=0 & ✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the set $V$ is orthogonal.\n\n- For the set $W,$ notice that \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{w}_{1}\\cdot {w}_{2} & =[\\begin{aligned}5 \\\\ -3\\end{aligned}]\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}-3 \\\\ -8\\end{aligned}]=-15+24=9\\ne 0 & \\times \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, $W$ is not an orthogonal set.\n\nTherefore, we conclude that the correct answer is \"$U$ and $V$ only\"."}] [{:db/id 17592186351423 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id 17592186133781 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "ed5c8af7-e617-5e8c-be01-45a26eb17d4d" :knowledge-point/title "Determining Whether Sets are Orthonormal"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "495e79d0-5695-5b1d-b180-8255ddd180d8" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$U$ only" :db/id 17592186349112} {:answer/id #uuid "4f8f838d-c58e-52c5-9a16-9f6273240074" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$V$ only" :db/id 17592186349165} {:answer/id #uuid "4fd9369b-ea61-5a1d-ac7f-55fd79648d4a" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$U$ and $W$ only" :db/id 17592186349169} {:answer/id #uuid "9477547c-7d95-50de-b89c-0ff5612d9aad" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$W$ only" :db/id 17592186349774} {:answer/id #uuid "b50a8836-0fef-5adc-a47b-d13e1dab0baa" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$V$ and $W$ only" :db/id 17592186350049}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "9477547c-7d95-50de-b89c-0ff5612d9aad" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$W$ only" :db/id 17592186349774} :answer-field/id #uuid "6e054f01-0c8d-5ba5-b957-74b1a6c94ad6" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id 17592186350892}] :question/difficulty {:db/id 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "2c1e164f-769d-52d4-a09c-f8474e7b2368" :question/math-academy-id "q-82401" :question/problem "Which of the following sets are orthonormal?\n\n- $U={[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]}$\n- $V={[\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}} \\\\ \\frac{2}{\\sqrt{5}}\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]}$\n- $W={[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]}$" :question/worked-solution "Let's denote the vectors of the given sets as \n${{u}_{1},{u}_{2}},$\n${{v}_{1},{v}_{2}},$ and \n${{w}_{1},{w}_{2}}$, respectively. \n\nThe sets $U,V,$ and $W$ are orthonormal if the following conditions on each set hold:\n\n- ${u}_{1}\\cdot {u}_{2}=0\\,\\text{and}\\,{\\begin{aligned}{u}_{1}\\cdot {u}_{1}=1 \\\\ {u}_{2}\\cdot {u}_{2}=1.\\end{aligned}$\n\n- ${v}_{1}\\cdot {v}_{2}=0\\,\\text{and}\\,{\\begin{aligned}{v}_{1}\\cdot {v}_{1}=1 \\\\ {v}_{2}\\cdot {v}_{2}=1.\\end{aligned}$\n\n- ${w}_{1}\\cdot {w}_{2}=0\\,\\text{and}\\,{\\begin{aligned}{w}_{1}\\cdot {w}_{1}=1 \\\\ {w}_{2}\\cdot {w}_{2}=1.\\end{aligned}$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our sets in turn.\n\n- For the set $U,$ notice that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{u}_{1}\\cdot {u}_{1} & =[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]=1+1=2\\ne 1 & \\times \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, $U$ is not an orthonormal set.\n\n- For the set $V,$ notice that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{v}_{1}\\cdot {v}_{2} & =[\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}} \\\\ \\frac{2}{\\sqrt{5}}\\end{aligned}]\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]=0+\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{5}}=\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{5}}\\ne 0 & \\times \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, $V$ is not an orthonormal set.\n\n- For the set $W,$ we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{w}_{1}\\cdot {w}_{2} & =[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]=0+0=0 & ✓ \\\\ {w}_{1}\\cdot {w}_{1} & =[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]=1+0=1 & ✓ \\\\ {w}_{2}\\cdot {w}_{2} & =[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]=0+1=1 & ✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the set $W$ is orthonormal.\n\nTherefore, we conclude that the correct answer is \"$W$ only\"."}] [{:db/id 17592186351471 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id 17592186087746 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "d8c04b23-e341-57e9-a072-985e4b536e5a" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Linear Transformations"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "61be30a1-e0f8-5e75-b806-749de2961190" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, II, and III" :db/id 17592186349341} {:answer/id #uuid "84316694-dae1-5f54-aebd-e653a190107b" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id 17592186349653} {:answer/id #uuid "94cdc7c9-8342-50ab-9bea-d6611c359cb8" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "None" :db/id 17592186349780} {:answer/id #uuid "a559cc25-0dd3-5186-9ef2-495f6b774679" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id 17592186349920} {:answer/id #uuid "cbbaf352-55b2-5f1b-9c0b-327d64fe6e4d" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id 17592186350238}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "61be30a1-e0f8-5e75-b806-749de2961190" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, II, and III" :db/id 17592186349341} :answer-field/id #uuid "1c5a073c-52a4-505b-b138-c4213c01bfe1" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id 17592186350760}] :question/difficulty {:db/id 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "d21c33a8-a8c0-5b77-a079-4c787c111b5f" :question/math-academy-id "q-8296" :question/problem "Let \n$T:[\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y\\end{aligned}]⟶[\\begin{aligned}5x+2y \\\\ 3y\\end{aligned}].$\nWhich of the following statements are true?\n\n- $T(kv)=kT(v)$ for any real $2\\times 1$ column vector $v$ and real scalar $k$\n- $T({v}_{1}+{v}_{2})=T({v}_{1})+T({v}_{2})$ for any real $2\\times 1$ column vectors ${v}_{1}$ and ${v}_{2}$\n- $T$ is a linear transformation" :question/worked-solution "A linear transformation $T$ satisfies two properties:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & T[\\begin{aligned}kx \\\\ ky\\end{aligned}]=kT[\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y\\end{aligned}], \\\\  & T[\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}+{x}_{2} \\\\ {y}_{1}+{y}_{2}\\end{aligned}]=T[\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} \\\\ {y}_{1}\\end{aligned}]+T[\\begin{aligned}{x}_{2} \\\\ {y}_{2}\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Checking the first condition, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}T[\\begin{aligned}kx \\\\ ky\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}5(kx)+2(ky) \\\\ 3(ky)\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}k(5x+2y) \\\\ k(3y)\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =k[\\begin{aligned}5x+2y \\\\ 3y\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =kT[\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y\\end{aligned}].\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Checking the second condition, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}T[\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}+{x}_{2} \\\\ {y}_{1}+{y}_{2}\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}5({x}_{1}+{x}_{2})+2({y}_{1}+{y}_{2}) \\\\ 3({y}_{1}+{y}_{2})\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}5{x}_{1}+2{y}_{1} \\\\ 3{y}_{1}\\end{aligned}]+[\\begin{aligned}5{x}_{2}+2{y}_{2} \\\\ 3{y}_{2}\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =T[\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} \\\\ {y}_{1}\\end{aligned}]+T[\\begin{aligned}{x}_{2} \\\\ {y}_{2}\\end{aligned}].\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $\\text{T}$ is a linear transformation.\n\nAs a result, all three statements are true."}] [{:db/id 17592186351472 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id 17592186087746 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "d8c04b23-e341-57e9-a072-985e4b536e5a" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Linear Transformations"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1c8885d8-6682-5fa2-8910-913ea7fa4354" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id 17592186348693} {:answer/id #uuid "1eb9e24d-e09f-5514-99f5-7f780feb6c5c" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, II, and III" :db/id 17592186348715} {:answer/id #uuid "3e386808-f929-5075-847b-8e26ef770089" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id 17592186349014} {:answer/id #uuid "78bbafdc-71b8-57e8-be89-9a17e0e6c1a9" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id 17592186349563} {:answer/id #uuid "da3a295e-ce40-59ab-96ab-0c122163f211" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "None" :db/id 17592186350360}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "1eb9e24d-e09f-5514-99f5-7f780feb6c5c" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, II, and III" :db/id 17592186348715} :answer-field/id #uuid "43c3161f-b901-5adc-b467-8ed60dcbe261" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id 17592186350821}] :question/difficulty {:db/id 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "5104f251-39bc-5c29-bbcf-96e73f6b8fe5" :question/math-academy-id "q-8297" :question/problem "Let \n$T:[\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y\\end{aligned}]⟶[\\begin{aligned}3y \\\\ 2x\\end{aligned}].$\nWhich of the following statements are true?\n\n- $T(kv)=kT(v)$ for any real $2\\times 1$ column vector $v$ and real scalar $k$\n- $T({v}_{1}+{v}_{2})=T({v}_{1})+T({v}_{2})$ for any real $2\\times 1$ column vectors ${v}_{1}$ and ${v}_{2}$\n- $T$ is a linear transformation" :question/worked-solution "A linear transformation $T$ satisfies two properties:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & T[\\begin{aligned}kx \\\\ ky\\end{aligned}]=kT[\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y\\end{aligned}], \\\\  & T[\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}+{x}_{2} \\\\ {y}_{1}+{y}_{2}\\end{aligned}]=T[\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} \\\\ {y}_{1}\\end{aligned}]+T[\\begin{aligned}{x}_{2} \\\\ {y}_{2}\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Checking the first condition, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}T[\\begin{aligned}kx \\\\ ky\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}3ky \\\\ 2kx\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =k[\\begin{aligned}3y \\\\ 2x\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =kT[\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y\\end{aligned}].\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Checking the second condition, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}T[\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}+{x}_{2} \\\\ {y}_{1}+{y}_{2}\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}3({y}_{1}+{y}_{2}) \\\\ 2({x}_{1}+{x}_{2})\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}3{y}_{1} \\\\ 2{x}_{1}\\end{aligned}]+[\\begin{aligned}3{y}_{2} \\\\ 2{x}_{2}\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =T[\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} \\\\ {y}_{1}\\end{aligned}]+T[\\begin{aligned}{x}_{2} \\\\ {y}_{2}\\end{aligned}].\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $\\text{T}$ is a linear transformation.\n\nAs a result, all three statements are true."}] [{:db/id 17592186351535 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id 17592186133843 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "ee1838df-f1d6-5bc4-908c-b96e2ad577c9" :knowledge-point/title "Determining Whether Sets are Orthogonal"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "19628589-c585-50da-a08f-d16156b30654" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$U$ only" :db/id 17592186348658} {:answer/id #uuid "32242d4f-c08f-5634-bb7c-a044ed166cca" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$W$ only" :db/id 17592186348892} {:answer/id #uuid "5b245e85-64c4-5b15-b9e5-cf3890016d6c" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$V$ and $W$ only" :db/id 17592186349286} {:answer/id #uuid "60abd1fa-0da0-5751-abb2-866a646578fb" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$U$ and $V$ only" :db/id 17592186349327} {:answer/id #uuid "cfd94aba-1787-5be4-942a-24258edda1da" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$U$ and $W$ only" :db/id 17592186350274}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "cfd94aba-1787-5be4-942a-24258edda1da" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$U$ and $W$ only" :db/id 17592186350274} :answer-field/id #uuid "c6e9a62b-01e6-5967-8440-2d30c852b68d" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id 17592186351052}] :question/difficulty {:db/id 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "3593d781-0056-56f1-8b48-1ee74f8fd0fe" :question/math-academy-id "q-83719" :question/problem "Which of the following sets are orthogonal?\n\n- $U={[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]}$\n- $V={[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ 0 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}4 \\\\ -2 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}-6 \\\\ 0 \\\\ -6\\end{aligned}]}$\n- $W={[\\begin{aligned}8 \\\\ -4\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}-5 \\\\ -10\\end{aligned}]}$" :question/worked-solution "Let's denote the vectors of the given sets as \n${{u}_{1},{u}_{2},{u}_{3}},$ \n${{v}_{1},{v}_{2},{v}_{3}},$ and \n${{w}_{1},{w}_{2}},$ respectively. \n\nThe sets $U,V,$ and $W$ are orthogonal if the following conditions on each set hold:\n\n- ${u}_{1}\\cdot {u}_{2}=0,\\,{u}_{1}\\cdot {u}_{3}=0,\\,{u}_{2}\\cdot {u}_{3}=0.$\n\n- ${v}_{1}\\cdot {v}_{2}=0,\\,{v}_{1}\\cdot {v}_{3}=0,\\,{v}_{2}\\cdot {v}_{3}=0.$\n\n- ${w}_{1}\\cdot {w}_{2}=0.$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our sets in turn.\n\n- For the set $U,$ we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{u}_{1}\\cdot {u}_{2} & =[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]=-1+0+1=0 & ✓ \\\\ {u}_{1}\\cdot {u}_{3} & =[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]=0+0+0=0 & ✓ \\\\ {u}_{2}\\cdot {u}_{3} & =[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]=0+0+0=0 & ✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the set $U$ is orthogonal.\n\n- For the set $V,$ we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{v}_{1}\\cdot {v}_{2} & =[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ 0 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}]\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}4 \\\\ -2 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}]=12+0+1=13\\ne 0 & \\times \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, $V$ is not an orthogonal set.\n\n- For the set $W,$ notice that \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{w}_{1}\\cdot {w}_{2} & =[\\begin{aligned}8 \\\\ -4\\end{aligned}]\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}-5 \\\\ -10\\end{aligned}]=-40+40=0 & ✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the set $W$ is orthogonal.\n\nTherefore, we conclude that the correct answer is \"$U$ and $W$ only\"."}] [{:db/id 17592186351542 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id 17592186133781 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "ed5c8af7-e617-5e8c-be01-45a26eb17d4d" :knowledge-point/title "Determining Whether Sets are Orthonormal"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "3a4f28f2-d974-5a65-8213-04795e7de605" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$U$ only" :db/id 17592186348973} {:answer/id #uuid "5e34ff4d-8f7c-5ec9-8897-24713cfde0e2" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$W$ only" :db/id 17592186349311} {:answer/id #uuid "6b9e1ec6-8e0b-5da1-9fd6-8d626d78ba4c" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$U$ and $V$ only" :db/id 17592186349438} {:answer/id #uuid "cd3681bc-7477-528f-821a-19436df269a3" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$U$ and $W$ only" :db/id 17592186350250} {:answer/id #uuid "cdab980a-d6f5-5611-ade3-bc4a1413c279" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$V$ and $W$ only" :db/id 17592186350257}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "cd3681bc-7477-528f-821a-19436df269a3" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$U$ and $W$ only" :db/id 17592186350250} :answer-field/id #uuid "cded3021-7d49-58db-a464-fd687f0c58fc" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id 17592186351066}] :question/difficulty {:db/id 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "757ea74d-034b-5cf5-b116-1f66ebbe72c0" :question/math-academy-id "q-83745" :question/problem "Which of the following sets are orthonormal?\n\n- $U={[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{13}} \\\\ \\frac{3}{\\sqrt{13}}\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{3}{\\sqrt{13}} \\\\ \\frac{2}{\\sqrt{13}}\\end{aligned}]}$\n- $V={[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 1 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1 \\\\ -4\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}6 \\\\ -1 \\\\ -7\\end{aligned}]}$\n- $W={[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{5}} \\\\ \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}}\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}} \\\\ -\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{5}}\\end{aligned}]}$" :question/worked-solution "Let's denote the vectors of the given sets as \n${{u}_{1},{u}_{2}},$ \n${{v}_{1},{v}_{2},{v}_{3}},$ and \n${{w}_{1},{w}_{2}},$ respectively. \n\nThe sets $U,V,$ and $W$ are orthonormal if the following conditions on each set hold:\n\n- ${u}_{1}\\cdot {u}_{2}=0\\,\\text{and}\\,{\\begin{aligned}{u}_{1}\\cdot {u}_{1}=1 \\\\ {u}_{2}\\cdot {u}_{2}=1.\\end{aligned}$\n\n- ${\\begin{aligned}{v}_{1}\\cdot {v}_{2}=0 \\\\ {v}_{1}\\cdot {v}_{3}=0 \\\\ {v}_{2}\\cdot {v}_{3}=0\\end{aligned}\\,\\text{and}\\,{\\begin{aligned}{v}_{1}\\cdot {v}_{1}=1 \\\\ {v}_{2}\\cdot {v}_{2}=1 \\\\ {v}_{3}\\cdot {v}_{3}=1.\\end{aligned}$\n\n- ${w}_{1}\\cdot {w}_{2}=0\\,\\text{and}\\,{\\begin{aligned}{w}_{1}\\cdot {w}_{1}=1 \\\\ {w}_{2}\\cdot {w}_{2}=1.\\end{aligned}$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our sets in turn.\n\n- For the set $U,$ we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{u}_{1}\\cdot {u}_{2} & =[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{13}} \\\\ \\frac{3}{\\sqrt{13}}\\end{aligned}]\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{3}{\\sqrt{13}} \\\\ \\frac{2}{\\sqrt{13}}\\end{aligned}]=-\\frac{6}{13}+\\frac{6}{13}=0 & ✓ \\\\ {u}_{1}\\cdot {u}_{1} & =[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{13}} \\\\ \\frac{3}{\\sqrt{13}}\\end{aligned}]\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{13}} \\\\ \\frac{3}{\\sqrt{13}}\\end{aligned}]=\\frac{4}{13}+\\frac{9}{13}=1 & ✓ \\\\ {u}_{2}\\cdot {u}_{2} & =[\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{3}{\\sqrt{13}} \\\\ \\frac{2}{\\sqrt{13}}\\end{aligned}]\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{3}{\\sqrt{13}} \\\\ \\frac{2}{\\sqrt{13}}\\end{aligned}]=\\frac{9}{13}+\\frac{4}{13}=1 & ✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the set $U$ is orthonormal.\n\n- For the set $V,$ notice that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{v}_{1}\\cdot {v}_{2} & =[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 1 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}]\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1 \\\\ -4\\end{aligned}]=0+1+8=9\\ne 0 & \\times \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, $V$ is not an orthonormal set.\n\n- For the set $W,$ we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{w}_{1}\\cdot {w}_{2} & =[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{5}} \\\\ \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}}\\end{aligned}]\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}} \\\\ -\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{5}}\\end{aligned}]=\\frac{2}{5}-\\frac{2}{5}=0 & ✓ \\\\ {w}_{1}\\cdot {w}_{1} & =[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{5}} \\\\ \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}}\\end{aligned}]\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{5}} \\\\ \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}}\\end{aligned}]=\\frac{4}{5}+\\frac{1}{5}=1 & ✓ \\\\ {w}_{2}\\cdot {w}_{2} & =[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}} \\\\ -\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{5}}\\end{aligned}]\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}} \\\\ -\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{5}}\\end{aligned}]=\\frac{1}{5}+\\frac{4}{5}=1 & ✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, $W$ is an orthonormal set.\n\nTherefore, we conclude that the correct answer is \"$U$ and $W$ only\"."}] [{:db/id 17592186351543 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id 17592186105817 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "2f7256c7-afea-5c33-a859-623b0531e88c" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Discrete Random Variables"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "120fab5a-7a2e-524f-959b-d51035c63e57" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id 17592186348601} {:answer/id #uuid "4eac6fc5-516a-54d0-a870-566797eef57b" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, II, and III" :db/id 17592186349160} {:answer/id #uuid "bf2aab15-3795-57cb-96d1-6a1357e369ec" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id 17592186350143} {:answer/id #uuid "ee203bcc-11df-5642-a2fb-37f65be8304b" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id 17592186350527} {:answer/id #uuid "fda5d663-19cc-5d86-a753-5c963c0408d7" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id 17592186350679}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "120fab5a-7a2e-524f-959b-d51035c63e57" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id 17592186348601} :answer-field/id #uuid "de3d86c1-9453-5879-b8eb-16a995edd2f6" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id 17592186351096}] :question/difficulty {:db/id 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "97ab1308-2ae6-5488-8b7b-f352f2f86ff6" :question/math-academy-id "q-83746" :question/problem "Which of the following are discrete random variables?\n\n- The number of hearts in a standard deck of cards\n- The number of diamonds obtained when $7$ cards are randomly selected from a standard deck of cards\n- The number of times a card is selected from a standard deck of cards until a King appears" :question/worked-solution "A random variable is a variable whose value is determined by the outcome of a trial or experiment.\n\nA *discrete* random variable is a random variable\n\n- that has finitely many possible values, or\n\n- whose possible values can be listed in a sequence: ${x}_{1},{x}_{2},{x}_{3},…$\n\nRemember that a real interval (e.g. $(a,b)$ or $[a,b]$) cannot be listed in a sequence.\n\nWith that in mind, let's consider each of the given statements.\n\n- Statement I does *not* describe a discrete random variable. The number of hearts in a particular complete deck of cards is not the outcome of a trial or experiment.\n\n- Statement II describes a discrete random variable. Its value is determined by the result of an experiment, and its possible values can be listed in a sequence: $0,1,2,3,4,5,6,7.$\n\n- Statement III describes a discrete random variable. Its value is determined by the result of an experiment, and we can count its possible values in a sequence: $1,2,3,…$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II and III only.\""}] [{:db/id 17592186351546 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id 17592186105817 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "2f7256c7-afea-5c33-a859-623b0531e88c" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Discrete Random Variables"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "4aec78c4-7503-5290-ab1e-e331abe9d6da" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id 17592186349132} {:answer/id #uuid "693eaeb1-6a33-509d-b224-d974178cc9bf" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id 17592186349421} {:answer/id #uuid "6c3da218-06de-5858-a402-9028bb376e02" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id 17592186349445} {:answer/id #uuid "907e3e43-5694-5205-ace7-54016bcd6661" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id 17592186349744} {:answer/id #uuid "d93b18f2-af63-503c-95a8-606c11f0e71b" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id 17592186350351}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "d93b18f2-af63-503c-95a8-606c11f0e71b" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id 17592186350351} :answer-field/id #uuid "ae8581a5-51ed-50d3-b3df-f1f3ea274f70" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id 17592186351007}] :question/difficulty {:db/id 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "423a76f9-5b29-5c2a-b8cc-b13643f5126d" :question/math-academy-id "q-83758" :question/problem "Which of the following are discrete random variables?\n\n- The average height of students in a particular class\n- The number of blue sugar-coated chocolates in a randomly selected bag of candy\n- The number of free-throws Sally makes in a carnival game where she attempts $3$ free-throws" :question/worked-solution "A random variable is a variable whose value is determined by the outcome of a trial or experiment.\n\nA *discrete* random variable is a random variable\n\n- that has finitely many possible values, or\n\n- whose possible values can be listed in a sequence: ${x}_{1},{x}_{2},{x}_{3},…$\n\nRemember that a real interval (e.g. $(a,b)$ or $[a,b]$) cannot be listed in a sequence.\n\nWith that in mind, let's consider each of the given statements.\n\n- Statement I does *not* describe a discrete random variable. Although its value is determined by the result of an experiment, the possible values are real numbers that cannot be listed in a sequence.\n\n- Statement II describes a discrete random variable. Its value is determined by the result of an experiment, and it has finitely many possible values: $0,1,2,3,…$\n\n- Statement III describes a discrete random variable. Its value is determined by the result of an experiment, and we can list its possible values in a sequence: $0,1,2,3.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II and III only.\""}] [{:db/id 17592186351547 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id 17592186105817 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "2f7256c7-afea-5c33-a859-623b0531e88c" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Discrete Random Variables"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0c70da7a-f007-5390-a37a-d774dcdfe2f9" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id 17592186348556} {:answer/id #uuid "3895f578-77b1-5d93-9849-a14e63e92d39" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id 17592186348958} {:answer/id #uuid "6e1ab1a4-be93-53ac-9254-3efcc42eeebc" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, II, and III" :db/id 17592186349457} {:ans