answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id 17592186357999} :answer-field/id #uuid "456a44fb-b78f-52f0-9413-11ab6fa7aefb" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id 17592186360104}] :question/difficulty {:db/id 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "748a6989-6b95-540d-a99c-327fc633c873" :question/math-academy-id "q-96827" :question/problem "Which of the following is the correct interpretation of $g:[0,2]\\to {0,2}?$\n\n- The domain of $g$ is ${0,2}$\n- The codomain of $g$ is ${0,2}$\n- The range of $g$ is $[0,2]$" :question/worked-solution "The notation \n\n$$\ng:[0,2]\\to {0,2}\n$$\n\nmeans that the function $g$ maps the set $[0,2]$ to the set ${0,2}.$ In this case,\n\n- the domain of $g$ is $[0,2],$ and\n\n- the codomain of $g$ is ${0,2}.$ Note that this is *not* necessarily the range.\n\nAs a result, statements I and III are false, while statement II is true.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II only.\""}] [{:db/id 17592186360719 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id 17592186172034 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "1240e42a-92a5-51cc-a80e-3fe1ef0b16f7" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Codomains and Ranges"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0a38fba7-1e20-5451-aee0-408535719f37" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id 17592186357839} {:answer/id #uuid "384e706b-aaff-56a8-9492-8afb6a575dd2" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id 17592186358250} {:answer/id #uuid "5d9660ae-b6a1-5efd-8076-b367d90fa32c" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id 17592186358569} {:answer/id #uuid "84d85faf-24b6-5701-81e3-a44768dd64b2" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id 17592186358920} {:answer/id #uuid "f1063b5c-3a9d-58ec-a84b-33db62b2393f" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id 17592186359898}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "84d85faf-24b6-5701-81e3-a44768dd64b2" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id 17592186358920} :answer-field/id #uuid "64a2dcb0-fbbb-518b-bd86-648aad97805e" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id 17592186360153}] :question/difficulty {:db/id 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "9d79f788-f655-5287-8607-5b096289e0bd" :question/math-academy-id "q-96830" :question/problem "![](images/74/740d578e554cd61bc733079c32c063d41ab4d9dcc775d36f7080ee8e5aa5a622.png)\n\nConsider the function $f:X\\to Y$ given by the mapping diagram shown above. Which of the following statements are true?\n\n- The domain of $f$ is ${5,10,15}$\n- The codomain of $f$ is ${5,10,12,13,14,15}$\n- The range of $f$ is ${12,13,14}$" :question/worked-solution "The notation $f:X\\to Y$ means that the function $f$ maps the set $X$ to the set $Y.$ In this case,\n\n- the domain of $f$ is $X,$\n\n- the codomain of $f$ is $Y.$\n\nThe range of $f$ is the subset of the codomain that contains all values $y\\in Y$ that are mapped to by at least one element $x\\in X.$\n\nWith that in mind, let's inspect our diagram:\n\n- the domain of $f$ is $X={5,10,15},$\n\n- the codomain of $f$ is $Y={12,13,14},$\n\n- the range of $f$ is ${12,13},$ the subset of elements of $Y$ that have at least one incoming arrow.\n\nAs a result, statements II and III are false, while statement I is true.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer \"I only.\""}] [{:db/id 17592186360720 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id 17592186105893 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "ca93cd65-65e3-521e-a1e3-2721f45fb6a6" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Coordinates of an Image Formed by a Singular Transformation"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0cfe9812-86f1-5c5b-9fd2-5a6147ed8a40" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id 17592186357859} {:answer/id #uuid "4a8fdc73-f630-52c5-90b5-cff027f2bf0b" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id 17592186358389} {:answer/id #uuid "9669aab1-ba94-53d4-b9e7-518f8d8bb0eb" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id 17592186359067} {:answer/id #uuid "c49835a5-6cb5-5405-a466-b16bbb77b948" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id 17592186359479} {:answer/id #uuid "eeb47e53-1a29-5602-9773-81346d526d56" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id 17592186359880}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "c49835a5-6cb5-5405-a466-b16bbb77b948" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id 17592186359479} :answer-field/id #uuid "d2840d95-e615-5d9f-a975-078cc51f7264" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id 17592186360355}] :question/difficulty {:db/id 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "e1d4a0e7-8ceb-5003-89cd-d540839fd74d" :question/math-academy-id "q-96855" :question/problem "Consider the linear transformation $T$ with matrix representation $T,$ given by\n\n$$\nT=[\\begin{aligned}-4 & 2 \\\\ -8 & 4\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nA triangle $S$ has vertices at $(1,-1)$, $(1,0)$ and $(-2,1).$ Which of the following are coordinates of the vertices of ${S}^{′},$ the image of $S$ under the action of $T?$ \n\n- $(10,20)$\n- $(4,-8)$\n- $(-6,6)$" :question/worked-solution "To find the image of $S$ under the action of $T$, we first create a matrix $X$ containing all of the vertices of $S:$\n\n$$\nX=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 & -2 \\\\ -1 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nNow, we compute the image of $X$ under $T$ by calculating the matrix product $TX:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}TX & =[\\begin{aligned}-4 & 2 \\\\ -8 & 4\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 & -2 \\\\ -1 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-6 & -4 & 10 \\\\ -12 & -8 & 20\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the coordinates of the respective vertices of ${S}^{′}$ are $(-6,-12)$, $(-4,-8)$ and $(10,20).$ \n\nSo, the correct answer is \"I  only.\""}] [{:db/id 17592186360721 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id 17592186105893 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "ca93cd65-65e3-521e-a1e3-2721f45fb6a6" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Coordinates of an Image Formed by a Singular Transformation"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "3495151c-6f9c-5673-88de-f1c56e1af0e0" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id 17592186358221} {:answer/id #uuid "5751455e-b08a-59b1-989d-3eba444bfcf9" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id 17592186358509} {:answer/id #uuid "ad0bf57c-af5a-58c7-aa89-1d4cb5090cdc" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id 17592186359247} {:answer/id #uuid "b7d45304-6eb4-5c6d-b2cb-2ed7cead1fe1" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id 17592186359343} {:answer/id #uuid "d49d906c-2287-5d38-a378-e7c13932bd96" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id 17592186359609}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "b7d45304-6eb4-5c6d-b2cb-2ed7cead1fe1" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id 17592186359343} :answer-field/id #uuid "80db2f55-379d-536b-89f3-0c339f244b93" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id 17592186360210}] :question/difficulty {:db/id 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "0b46e610-7522-5441-8d91-b874b0e467ac" :question/math-academy-id "q-96856" :question/problem "Consider the linear transformation $T$ with matrix representation $T,$ given by\n\n$$\nT=[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 2 \\\\ 2 & -4\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nA triangle $S$ has vertices at $(0,-1),(1,1),$ and $(3,2).$ Which of the following are coordinates of the vertices of ${S}^{′},$ the image of $S$ under the action of $T?$\n\n- $(-1,4)$\n- $(-2,4)$\n- $(1,-3)$" :question/worked-solution "To find the image of $S$ under the action of $T$, we first create a matrix $X$ containing all of the vertices of $S:$\n\n$$\nX=[\\begin{aligned}0 & 1 & 3 \\\\ -1 & 1 & 2\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nNow, we compute the image of $X$ under $T$ by calculating the matrix product $TX:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}TX & =[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 2 \\\\ 2 & -4\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}0 & 1 & 3 \\\\ -1 & 1 & 2\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-2 & 1 & 1 \\\\ 4 & -2 & -2\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the coordinates of the respective vertices of ${S}^{′}$ are  $(-2,4)$, and $(1,-2).$ \n\nSo, the correct answer is \" II only.\""}] [{:db/id 17592186360722 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id 17592186105893 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "ca93cd65-65e3-521e-a1e3-2721f45fb6a6" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Coordinates of an Image Formed by a Singular Transformation"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "6b22130e-d538-51cd-afa7-dd10f5401224" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id 17592186358688} {:answer/id #uuid "a973aeee-5466-598f-b41a-c22c9961762a" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id 17592186359210} {:answer/id #uuid "abdf9aea-6545-5bd0-9dec-4d467e2cb7cc" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id 17592186359229} {:answer/id #uuid "c7316c85-95b4-51ee-bfe4-c004c5b0d590" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id 17592186359505} {:answer/id #uuid "e5d46810-2201-51e7-ade6-257ff2e94041" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id 17592186359786}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "c7316c85-95b4-51ee-bfe4-c004c5b0d590" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id 17592186359505} :answer-field/id #uuid "43f8b74e-fb61-58b8-a354-602b2da35b35" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id 17592186360097}] :question/difficulty {:db/id 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "ae6d946d-9a9f-5816-82c2-7048c4392f45" :question/math-academy-id "q-96857" :question/problem "Consider the linear transformation $T$ with matrix representation $T,$ given by\n\n$$\nT=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 3 \\\\ 3 & 9\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nA triangle $S$ has vertices at $(0,-1),(-2,1),$ and $(3,0).$ Which of the following are coordinates of the vertices of ${S}^{′},$ the image of $S$ under the action of $T?$ \n\n- $(1,3)$\n- $(0,-3)$\n- $(2,3)$" :question/worked-solution "To find the image of $S$ under the action of $T$, we first create a matrix $X$ containing all of the vertices of $S:$\n\n$$\nX=[\\begin{aligned}0 & -2 & 3 \\\\ -1 & 1 & 0\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nNow, we compute the image of $X$ under $T$ by calculating the matrix product $TX:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}TX & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 3 \\\\ 3 & 9\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}0 & -2 & 3 \\\\ -1 & 1 & 0\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-3 & 1 & 3 \\\\ -9 & 3 & 9\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the coordinates of the respective vertices of ${S}^{′}$ are $(-3,-9),$  $(1,3)$, and $(3,9).$ \n\nSo, the correct answer is \" I only.\""}] [{:db/id 17592186360779 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id 17592186172036 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "1343e4de-56ee-563c-bdc8-f8c0cdc54f0d" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying True Statements Regarding Slope Fields"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "8df25c87-e636-5dde-b751-e80b8dc57e3d" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id 17592186358989} {:answer/id #uuid "90274536-3643-5fef-a31d-ddbe3d4dedb1" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id 17592186359009} {:answer/id #uuid "9f1657b5-6491-5a2e-a622-f00650458340" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id 17592186359139} {:answer/id #uuid "b4e65af8-2544-591a-a04c-29a0ec7cd421" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id 17592186359327} {:answer/id #uuid "e718a960-bff2-5a96-b844-4c13a75e05fa" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "None of those listed" :db/id 17592186359798}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "8df25c87-e636-5dde-b751-e80b8dc57e3d" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id 17592186358989} :answer-field/id #uuid "6a986fe2-482f-5a4b-893a-3449b83a4970" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id 17592186360167}] :question/difficulty {:db/id 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "cae22cc3-a67e-53bb-bb36-eebf3af162ea" :question/math-academy-id "q-97288" :question/problem "Consider the differential equation $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=y+1$ and its slope field. Which of the following statements are true?\n\n- At every point along the line $y=-2,$ the slope field has a positive slope\n- At every point along the line $y=-1,$ the slope field is horizontal\n- At every point along the line $y=1,$ the slope field has a negative slope" :question/worked-solution "Notice that we are given an equation of the form\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=f(y).\n$$\n\nThis is an autonomous equation because the right-hand side is a function of $y$ only. This means that, for a fixed value of $y,$ the slope does not vary with $x.$ \n\nNow, let's check each statement:\n\n- Statement I is false. Consider a point of the form $(x,-2)$ where $x$ is arbitrary. We have\n\n$$\n{\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}|}_{(x,-2)}=-2+1=-1<0,\n$$\n\nand this implies that the slope at the point $(x,-2)$ is negative for any $x.$ Moreover, the slope at the point $(x,-2)$ is equal to $-1$ for any $x.$\n\n- Statement II is true. Consider a point of the form $(x,-1)$ where $x$ is arbitrary. We have\n\n$$\n{\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}|}_{(x,-1)}=-1+1=0,\n$$\n\nand this implies that the slope at the point $(x,-1)$ is horizontal for any $x.$\n\n- Statement III is false. Consider a point of the form $(x,1)$ where $x$ is arbitrary. We have\n\n$$\n{\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}|}_{(x,1)}=1+1=2>0,\n$$\n\nand this implies that the slope at the point $(x,1)$ is positive for any $x.$ Moreover, the slope at the point $(x,1)$ is equal to $2$ for any $x.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II only.\"\n\nWe can use this information to begin sketching the slope field, as shown below.\n\n![](images/ca/caa69f6116eba6462c4fe18f2e535d83db350f0797cf1fcd895ac6df9a4d6fd7.png)"}] [{:db/id 17592186362290 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id 17592186172049 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "337518a9-39a1-57e3-8686-74a135f7c3d7" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Oriented Curves"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "12952cd4-7772-56dc-91d8-15bc8530be70" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III and IV only" :db/id 17592186360911} {:answer/id #uuid "2038019b-1885-5eec-9088-19e1d3928ef8" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "IV only" :db/id 17592186360973} {:answer/id #uuid "2e5ac34d-1889-5353-8d4f-53b2d888139a" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id 17592186361043} {:answer/id #uuid "47758d4f-db43-5767-94a8-6bad79571164" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id 17592186361148} {:answer/id #uuid "c5af9d7e-7ae6-54a3-b951-68572e9e5ccd" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, II and IV only" :db/id 17592186361731}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "2e5ac34d-1889-5353-8d4f-53b2d888139a" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id 17592186361043} :answer-field/id #uuid "9160105b-00f4-5747-a450-ab936f60ad0c" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id 17592186362113}] :question/difficulty {:db/id 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "e0e004a8-576b-5fb6-94f2-3e66cfd74da8" :question/math-academy-id "q-98338" :question/problem "![](images/d1/d1b6a9fa72404e765796d75fc7ba00a391317ef2670e2f65f7c43db32207e8d2.png)\n\nWhich of the curves above is simple, closed, and negatively oriented?" :question/worked-solution "A simple closed curve is positively oriented if, when traveling along it, one always has the curve interior to the left and the curve exterior to the right. Otherwise, the curve is negatively oriented. \n\nA curve is positively oriented if it is traversed counterclockwise and is negatively oriented if it is traversed clockwise.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each of the curves:\n\n- Curve I is not closed.\n\n- Curve II is simple, closed, negatively oriented. The arrows indicate that the curve is traversed clockwise.\n\n- Curve III is a simple and closed curve but it is not negatively oriented. The arrows indicate that the curve is traversed counterclockwise, so it is positively oriented.\n\n- Curve IV is not simple.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II only.\""}] [{:db/id 17592186362303 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id 17592186147492 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "6e71c0c9-7dd1-5f09-a55c-96b5bed1ee9a" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying a Differential Equation Corresponding to a Phase Line"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "11372d09-67d9-541a-9fc3-f4d3ea012f48" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id 17592186360899} {:answer/id #uuid "21644acb-f019-5b89-bfed-a10b1e66c095" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "None of the listed" :db/id 17592186360979} {:answer/id #uuid "54c407de-2051-50b6-b4c4-b6d4d49eb166" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id 17592186361214} {:answer/id #uuid "c0be473a-9841-5281-b3ad-622f0e51737f" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id 17592186361701} {:answer/id #uuid "e765d40f-d905-59a7-8880-dd026959d10e" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id 17592186361871}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "54c407de-2051-50b6-b4c4-b6d4d49eb166" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id 17592186361214} :answer-field/id #uuid "9211f8d4-4b04-526a-80d6-74182399c28a" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id 17592186362115}] :question/difficulty {:db/id 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "4cdb362d-f774-5da6-bc15-c04006a3144a" :question/math-academy-id "q-98632" :question/problem "![](images/ca/ca296c3a8a2989fd5e5ca647ad4f7d0f4890aa3caf8d9e0a1fce98498d2d7d64.png)\n\nConsider the phase line shown above. Which of the following differential equations could have this phase line?\n\n- $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=(y+1)^{2}$\n- $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=(y-2)^{2}(y+1)$\n- $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}={y}^{2}-y-2$" :question/worked-solution "First, notice that all three differential equations are of the form\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=f(y).\n$$\n\nThe phase line gives us the following information:\n\n- The equilibrium solutions are $y=-1$ and $y=2.$\n\n- $f(y)$ is positive for $y\\in (-\\infty ,-1)\\cup (2,\\infty ).$\n\n- $f(y)$ is negative for $y\\in (-1,2).$\n\nWith this in mind, let's study each of the given equations.\n\n- We start by considering ${y}^{′}=(y+1)^{2}.$\n\nIn this case, $f(y)=(y+1)^{2},$ and the equilibrium solution is $y=-1$ only.\n\nTherefore, equation I does not have the given phase line.\n\n- Next, we consider ${y}^{′}=(y-2)^{2}(y+1).$\n\nFirst, $f(y)=(y-2)^{2}(y+1),$ so $f(y)=0$ if $y=-1,2.$ So, the equilibrium solutions correspond to those shown in the phase line.\n\nHowever, we note that $f(y)<0$ for $y\\in (-\\infty ,-1).$ This does *not* correspond to the arrows shown on the phase line.\n\nTherefore, equation II does not have the given phase line.\n\n- Finally, we consider ${y}^{′}={y}^{2}-y-2.$\n\nFirst, we see that $f(y)=(y+1)(y-2),$ so $f(y)=0$ if $y=-1,2.$ So, the equilibrium solutions correspond to those shown in the phase line.\n\nNow, we can construct a sign table for $f(y)$ as follows: \n\n|  | $(-\\infty ,-1)$ | $(-1,2)$ | $(2,\\infty )$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $y+1$ | $-$ | $+$ | $+$ |\n| $y-2$ | $-$ | $-$ | $+$ |\n| $f(y)$ | $+$ | $-$ | $+$ |\n\nSo, we have that $f(y)$ is negative for  $y\\in (-1,2),$ and $f(y)$ is positive for $y\\in (-\\infty ,-1)\\cup (2,\\infty ).$ This corresponds to the arrows shown on the phase line.\n\nTherefore, equation III has the given phase line.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"III only.\""}] [{:db/id 17592186362304 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id 17592186147492 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "6e71c0c9-7dd1-5f09-a55c-96b5bed1ee9a" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying a Differential Equation Corresponding to a Phase Line"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "3aa6c7e0-5267-5b8c-abf2-5d0df309dc2a" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id 17592186361103} {:answer/id #uuid "5d74c0da-e225-5209-830d-3e80c0fe6f58" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id 17592186361258} {:answer/id #uuid "830dcc1b-2b2e-5389-b0f1-16feba0089e3" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "None of the listed" :db/id 17592186361419} {:answer/id #uuid "a6615e8f-f741-5aca-a503-23a2a8928607" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id 17592186361582} {:answer/id #uuid "b969e418-a210-5467-8aff-175c3f8f7677" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id 17592186361663}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "5d74c0da-e225-5209-830d-3e80c0fe6f58" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id 17592186361258} :answer-field/id #uuid "a39d4165-9cb7-5227-961c-7c53619ef114" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id 17592186362133}] :question/difficulty {:db/id 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "98ee9b06-5212-535a-b3fd-5d1e48bb48f8" :question/math-academy-id "q-98636" :question/problem "![](images/b5/b5afe944331145c5253b2f1100f642b8a91a538295f3c84877eeb86870a04915.png)\n\nConsider the phase line shown above. Which of the following differential equations could have this phase line?\n\n- $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=y({y}^{2}-4)$\n- $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=y(4-{y}^{2})$\n- $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}={y}^{2}-4$" :question/worked-solution "First, notice that all three differential equations are of the form\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=f(y).\n$$\n\nThe phase line gives us the following information:\n\n- The equilibrium solutions are $y=-2,$ $y=0$ and $y=2.$\n\n- $f(y)$ is positive for $y\\in (-2,0)\\cup (2,\\infty ).$\n\n- $f(y)$ is negative for $y\\in (-\\infty ,-2)\\cup (0,2).$\n\nWith this in mind, let's study each of the given equations.\n\n- We start by considering ${y}^{′}=y({y}^{2}-4).$\n\nFirst, we see that $f(y)=y(y+2)(y-2),$ so $f(y)=0$ if $y=-2,0,2.$ So, the equilibrium solutions correspond to those shown in the phase line.\n\nNow, we can construct a sign table for $f(y)$ as follows: \n\n|  | $(-\\infty ,-2)$ | $(-2,0)$ | $(0,2)$ | $(2,\\infty )$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $y+2$ | $-$ | $+$ | $+$ | $+$ |\n| $y$ | $-$ | $-$ | $+$ | $+$ |\n| $y-2$ | $-$ | $-$ | $-$ | $+$ |\n| $f(y)$ | $-$ | $+$ | $-$ | $+$ |\n\nSo, we have that $f(y)$ is negative for $y\\in (-\\infty ,-2)\\cup (0,2),$ and $f(y)$ is positive for $y\\in (-2,0)\\cup (2,\\infty ).$ This corresponds to the arrows shown on the phase line.\n\nTherefore, equation I has the given phase line.\n\n- Next, we consider ${y}^{′}=y(4-{y}^{2}).$\n\nFirst, $f(y)=y(4-{y}^{2}),$ so $f(y)=0$ if $y=-2,0,2.$ So, the equilibrium solutions correspond to those shown in the phase line.\n\nHowever, we note that $f(y)>0$ for $y\\in (-\\infty ,-2).$ This does *not* correspond to the arrows shown on the phase line.\n\nTherefore, equation II does not have the given phase line.\n\n- Finally, we consider ${y}^{′}={y}^{2}-4.$\n\nIn this case, $f(y)={y}^{2}-4,$ and the equilibrium solutions are $y=\\pm 2$ only.\n\nTherefore, equation III does not have the given phase line.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only.\""}] [{:db/id 17592186362305 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id 17592186147492 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "6e71c0c9-7dd1-5f09-a55c-96b5bed1ee9a" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying a Differential Equation Corresponding to a Phase Line"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "29f574a9-2f08-5294-9f5c-67e219070410" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id 17592186361025} {:answer/id #uuid "3eb87ec8-c295-54ce-86c3-beaa2460079a" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id 17592186361121} {:answer/id #uuid "4e822101-3318-538a-8e55-7c2654b3e80d" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id 17592186361180} {:answer/id #uuid "95c23d54-74fb-5579-9050-5c4e2afeabcd" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id 17592186361502} {:answer/id #uuid "f7ce208c-37aa-5358-a431-5805208424b9" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "None of the listed" :db/id 17592186361946}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "95c23d54-74fb-5579-9050-5c4e2afeabcd" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id 17592186361502} :answer-field/id #uuid "b8e431df-ff4c-55d8-8720-8b0557dc17c8" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id 17592186362150}] :question/difficulty {:db/id 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "77dc20d1-343d-5ade-92ad-7f182610538c" :question/math-academy-id "q-98643" :question/problem "![](images/96/96740fdcc0dc6aa22fa097ba714669fae4ef79543aafad72b3fc3db7722f83d1.png)\n\nConsider the phase line shown above. Which of the following differential equations could have this phase line?\n\n- $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=-y(y-2)$\n- $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=-y(y-2)^{2}$\n- $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}={y}^{2}+2y$" :question/worked-solution "First, notice that all three differential equations are of the form\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=f(y).\n$$\n\nThe phase line gives us the following information:\n\n- The equilibrium solutions are $y=0$ and $y=2.$\n\n- $f(y)$ is positive for $y\\in (-\\infty ,0).$\n\n- $f(y)$ is negative for $y\\in (0,2)\\cup (2,\\infty ).$\n\nWith this in mind, let's study each of the given equations.\n\n- We start by considering ${y}^{′}=-y(y-2).$\n\nFirst, $f(y)=-y(y-2),$ so $f(y)=0$ if $y=0,2.$ So, the equilibrium solutions correspond to those shown in the phase line.\n\nHowever, we note that $f(y)>0$ for $y\\in (0,2).$ This does *not* correspond to the arrows shown on the phase line.\n\nTherefore, equation I does not have the given phase line.\n\n- Next, we consider ${y}^{′}=-y(y-2)^{2}.$\n\nFirst, we see that $f(y)=-y(y-2)^{2},$ so $f(y)=0$ if $y=0,2.$ So, the equilibrium solutions correspond to those shown in the phase line.\n\nNow, we can construct a sign table for $f(y)$ as follows: \n\n|  | $(-\\infty ,0)$ | $(0,2)$ | $(2,\\infty )$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $-y$ | $+$ | $-$ | $-$ |\n| $(y-2)^{2}$ | $+$ | $+$ | $+$ |\n| $f(y)$ | $+$ | $-$ | $-$ |\n\n \nSo, we have that $f(y)$ is positive for  $y\\in (-\\infty ,0),$ and $f(y)$ is negative for $y\\in (0,2)\\cup (2,\\infty ).$ This corresponds to the arrows shown on the phase line.\n\nTherefore, equation II has the given phase line.\n\n- Finally, we consider ${y}^{′}={y}^{2}+2y.$\n\nIn this case, $f(y)=y(y+2),$ and the equilibrium solution are $y=-2,0.$ \n\nTherefore, equation III does not have the given phase line.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II only.\""}] [{:db/id 17592186362306 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id 17592186147492 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "6e71c0c9-7dd1-5f09-a55c-96b5bed1ee9a" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying a Differential Equation Corresponding to a Phase Line"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2610a7cf-db25-56cc-a4de-3950f6b93f3b" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id 17592186361000} {:answer/id #uuid "ac17eb13-4abf-5033-ad9f-27fd7afee6f7" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id 17592186361602} {:answer/id #uuid "c54fbc98-7ef2-5a4b-9aca-02280ca85909" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id 17592186361728} {:answer/id #uuid "dd5fbbcd-aead-5d18-8c6e-26eb82ee8f58" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id 17592186361824} {:answer/id #uuid "f15fd121-a9a5-583b-a3cd-b6da8b635009" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "None of the listed" :db/id 17592186361912}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "c54fbc98-7ef2-5a4b-9aca-02280ca85909" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id 17592186361728} :answer-field/id #uuid "e96588a0-1001-5470-b659-7ae9d6ec24bd" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id 17592186362189}] :question/difficulty {:db/id 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "23af5cf8-2a07-56b8-9952-fd82e54a4b1d" :question/math-academy-id "q-98646" :question/problem "![](images/54/54cfcbaa5ce20c1881f35e83bb0ec28fa1399cc5100ee745941ff7bb52ac7705.png)\n\nConsider the phase line shown above. Which of the following differential equations could have this phase line?\n\n- $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=-(y+2)^{2}(y-1)$\n- $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=(y+2)(y-1)$\n- $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=-(y+2)(y-1)^{2}$" :question/worked-solution "First, notice that all three differential equations are of the form\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=f(y).\n$$\n\nThe phase line gives us the following information:\n\n- The equilibrium solutions are $y=-2$ and $y=1.$\n\n- $f(y)$ is positive for $y\\in (-\\infty ,-2).$\n\n- $f(y)$ is negative for $y\\in (-2,1)\\cup (1,\\infty ).$\n\nWith this in mind, let's study each of the given equations.\n\n- We start by considering ${y}^{′}=-(y+2)^{2}(y-1).$\n\nFirst, $f(y)=-(y+2)^{2}(y-1),$ so $f(y)=0$ if $y=-2,1.$ So, the equilibrium solutions correspond to those shown in the phase line.\n\nHowever, we note that $f(y)>0$ for $y\\in (-\\infty ,-2)\\cup (-2,1).$ This does *not* correspond to the arrows shown on the phase line.\n\nTherefore, equation I does not have the given phase line.\n\n- Next, we consider ${y}^{′}=(y+2)(y-1).$\n\nFirst, $f(y)=(y+2)(y-1),$ so $f(y)=0$ if $y=-2,1.$ So, the equilibrium solutions correspond to those shown in the phase line.\n\nHowever, we note that $f(y)>0$ for $y\\in (-\\infty ,-2)\\cup (1,\\infty ).$ This does *not* correspond to the arrows shown on the phase line.\n\nTherefore, equation II does not have the given phase line.\n\n- Finally, we consider ${y}^{′}=-(y+2)(y-1)^{2}.$\n\nFirst, we see that $f(y)=-(y+2)(y-1)^{2},$ so $f(y)=0$ if $y=-2,1.$ So, the equilibrium solutions correspond to those shown in the phase line.\n\nNow, we can construct a sign table for $f(y)$ as follows: \n\n|  | $(-\\infty ,-2)$ | $(-2,1)$ | $(1,\\infty )$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $-(y+2)$ | $+$ | $-$ | $-$ |\n| $(y-1)^{2}$ | $+$ | $+$ | $+$ |\n| $f(y)$ | $+$ | $-$ | $-$ |\n\nSo, we have that $f(y)$ is negative for $y\\in (-2,1)\\cup (1,\\infty ),$ and $f(y)$ is positive for $y\\in (-\\infty ,-2).$ This corresponds to the arrows shown on the phase line.\n\nTherefore, equation III has the given phase line.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"III only.\""}] [{:db/id 17592186362310 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id 17592186172051 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "d1af47ea-6253-5d58-9b94-31e52de27106" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Simply-Connected Regions"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "6c330c16-86a4-5567-97a4-35d7be078109" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id 17592186361318} {:answer/id #uuid "9d0b8f47-f841-5b4b-82f8-d1e9e9169702" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III and IV only" :db/id 17592186361538} {:answer/id #uuid "b5ed1871-6019-50c4-b60d-84897d16cb50" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, II, and III only" :db/id 17592186361646} {:answer/id #uuid "be0acb67-df35-5a8d-aed9-550e66100c58" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, III, and IV only" :db/id 17592186361685} {:answer/id #uuid "c528bc6f-a3a3-5b70-81d3-16a2214884c9" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id 17592186361727}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "b5ed1871-6019-50c4-b60d-84897d16cb50" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, II, and III only" :db/id 17592186361646} :answer-field/id #uuid "cec89a36-df3b-5198-9e47-dea407d04a08" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id 17592186362170}] :question/difficulty {:db/id 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "0317a51a-f6d5-582a-aa69-b3f89cc6775d" :question/math-academy-id "q-98690" :question/problem "![](images/0d/0db1568fab302ddbd9f16dbdf9c276eeb62cbac37346b077c1639fe68c2396ac.png)\n\nWhich of the regions shown above are simply connected?" :question/worked-solution "A connected region $D$ is simply connected if every simple closed curve in $D$ can be contracted to a point.\n\n![](images/7d/7d6aea1a62fb290702630ef95ec86f4119e7c8fdd74b8148b62b66e5ac871b58.png)\n\nA connected *plane* region $D$ is simply connected if every simple closed curve in $D$ encloses only points in $D.$ In other words, the region does not contain any holes. Plane regions containing holes are not simply connected, since some simple closed curves cannot be contracted to a point:\n\n![](images/c3/c3c3e7a60ff0dd917cf6c0b293dae7510e457a0455629add1c54cadac65248b7.png)\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each of the regions:  \n\n- Region I is simply connected. This is a connected region that does not contain holes.\n\n- Region II is simply connected. This is a connected region that does not contain holes.\n\n- Region III is simply connected. This is a connected region that does not contain holes.\n\n- Region IV is not simply connected. Instead, this is a connected region that contains a hole.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I, II, and III only.\""}] [{:db/id 17592186362313 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id 17592186147494 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "680a42fd-9b70-5fb2-916c-d10b71b11d82" :knowledge-point/title "Classifying Equilibrium Points Using Phase Lines"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "3b34748f-857d-5cfa-b351-1a43a20c2692" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id 17592186361107} {:answer/id #uuid "61cb4512-f982-534e-a91e-870ab396ea10" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id 17592186361275} {:answer/id #uuid "84029e23-38de-5dd2-96bf-7030fa4379e8" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id 17592186361425} {:answer/id #uuid "c9562f76-31e9-5fb8-8d05-14359ba8542f" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id 17592186361746} {:answer/id #uuid "e5e5787c-1281-5971-b262-071aa37d5e7e" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id 17592186361864}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "c9562f76-31e9-5fb8-8d05-14359ba8542f" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id 17592186361746} :answer-field/id #uuid "1a69467f-1203-587a-9ced-02e01b856019" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id 17592186362004}] :question/difficulty {:db/id 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "dd0fe6f6-6555-578d-880a-04a4380b3213" :question/math-academy-id "q-98700" :question/problem "![](images/4c/4c4ef140c85eb91bd01688cc2a447f13a21c2a51964c6d101887d15045cee95d.png)\n\nA particular autonomous differential equation has the phase line shown above. Which of the following statements are true?\n\nI. $y=-2$ is a sink\n\nII. $y=-2$ is a source\n\nIII. $y=2$ is a node" :question/worked-solution "We can classify the equilibrium solutions of an autonomous ODE ${y}^{′}=f(y)$ by examining its phase line.\n\n- An equilibrium solution is a *sink* if the arrows directly above and below it on the phase line both point *toward* the equilibrium solution.\n\n- An equilibrium solution is a *source* if the arrows directly above and below it on the phase line both point *away* from the equilibrium solution.\n\n- An equilibrium solution is a *node* if the arrows directly above and below it on the phase line point in the same direction.\n\nLet's check each statement:\n\n- Statement I is true, while statement II is false. Notice that the arrow just above the point $y=-2$ points downward, and the arrow just below it points upward. In other words, both arrows point *toward* the point $y=-2.$This means that $y=-2$ is a sink.\n\n- Statement III is true. Notice that the arrow just above the point $y=2$ points downward, and the arrow just below it points downward too. In other words, both arrows point in the *same* direction on either side of $y=2.$ This means that $y=2$ is a node.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and III only\"."}] [{:db/id 17592186362314 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id 17592186147494 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "680a42fd-9b70-5fb2-916c-d10b71b11d82" :knowledge-point/title "Classifying Equilibrium Points Using Phase Lines"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2a74f383-33f8-523e-afc2-28283d62a481" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id 17592186361027} {:answer/id #uuid "352459c2-e87d-555e-a46f-057af18fd807" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id 17592186361071} {:answer/id #uuid "5762f318-0805-508d-8470-1dccc0ea509e" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id 17592186361234} {:answer/id #uuid "9bb104b9-04bc-5112-9260-37c3ccb3756c" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id 17592186361531} {:answer/id #uuid "f342669d-8a5c-5231-b7f4-b9f8c5b1083e" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id 17592186361923}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "5762f318-0805-508d-8470-1dccc0ea509e" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id 17592186361234} :answer-field/id #uuid "efc08f17-6874-5b94-858f-d25d69b2fc3e" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id 17592186362197}] :question/difficulty {:db/id 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "b3fbe51b-8dce-56ec-a759-a07e5a4e34b0" :question/math-academy-id "q-98701" :question/problem "![](images/74/74790c4dbcb2d9cebee802dd155b5fa0a94e442f9bbc59c6277eb0d6fca3d60a.png)\n\nA particular autonomous differential equation has the phase line shown above. Which of the following statements are true?\n\n- $y=-2$ is a source\n- $y=-5$ is a node\n- $y=-5$ is a sink" :question/worked-solution "We can classify the equilibrium solutions of an autonomous ODE ${y}^{′}=f(y)$ by examining its phase line.\n\n- An equilibrium solution is a *sink* if the arrows directly above and below it on the phase line both point *toward* the equilibrium solution.\n\n- An equilibrium solution is a *source* if the arrows directly above and below it on the phase line both point *away* from the equilibrium solution.\n\n- An equilibrium solution is a *node* if the arrows directly above and below it on the phase line point in the same direction.\n\nLet's check each statement:\n\n- Statement I is true. Notice that the arrow just above the point $y=-2$ points upward, and the arrow just below it points downward. In other words, both arrows point *away* from  $y=-2.$ This means that $y=-2$ is a source.\n\n- Statement II is false, while statement III is true. Notice that the arrow just above the point $y=-5$ points downward, and the arrow just below it points upward. In other words, both arrows point *toward* the point $y=-5.$ This means that $y=-5$ is a sink.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and III only\"."}] [{:db/id 17592186362315 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id 17592186147494 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "680a42fd-9b70-5fb2-916c-d10b71b11d82" :knowledge-point/title "Classifying Equilibrium Points Using Phase Lines"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0c3eda40-2242-5e0a-9555-00e5bf887560" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id 17592186360884} {:answer/id #uuid "593d718c-c9be-5e69-88ba-9ec9c5d045bf" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id 17592186361242} {:answer/id #uuid "5d324b6e-bc85-5092-b5ff-97103e5e694b" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id 17592186361254} {:answer/id #uuid "6e99537e-a0e9-5876-b0bb-411dc460d8ec" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id 17592186361328} {:answer/id #uuid "8e618e93-96aa-5ed8-a025-fb58a1e0ab02" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id 17592186361469}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "0c3eda40-2242-5e0a-9555-00e5bf887560" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id 17592186360884} :answer-field/id #uuid "8e1df10d-2460-548d-98f6-05718a45ad84" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id 17592186362108}] :question/difficulty {:db/id 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "bf8ec5e0-4855-5481-a220-299eb2c89a02" :question/math-academy-id "q-98702" :question/problem "![](images/68/68eb28d31145fe38d481cb2273bc35edc80bfccf1b80b2e9a75b4136eab45629.png)\n\nA particular autonomous differential equation has the phase line shown above. Which of the following statements are true?\n\n- $y=4$ is a source\n- $y=4$ is a node\n- $y=1$ is a sink" :question/worked-solution "We can classify the equilibrium solutions of an autonomous ODE ${y}^{′}=f(y)$ by examining its phase line.\n\n- An equilibrium solution is a *sink* if the arrows directly above and below it on the phase line both point *toward* the equilibrium solution.\n\n- An equilibrium solution is a *source* if the arrows directly above and below it on the phase line both point *away* from the equilibrium solution.\n\n- An equilibrium solution is a *node* if the arrows directly above and below it on the phase line point in the same direction.\n\nLet's check each statement:\n\n- Statement I is true, while statement II is false. Notice that the arrow just above the point $y=4$ points upward, and the arrow just below it points downward. In other words, both arrows point *away* from  $y=4.$ This means that $y=4$ is a source.\n\n- Statement III is false. Notice that the arrow just above the point $y=1$ points downward, and the arrow just below it points downward too. In other words, both arrows point in the *same* direction on either side oft $y=1.$This means that $y=1$ is a node.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only\"."}] [{:db/id 17592186362318 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id 17592186147495 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "4535b48e-a33f-5f62-95d3-a5c0991c1f6d" :knowledge-point/title "Classifying Equilibrium Points Using a Graph"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "10267759-948c-5e21-b819-eea0a52b9c53" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id 17592186360894} {:answer/id #uuid "21c0f117-930c-5f72-bd6c-e01d53d016cd" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id 17592186360984} {:answer/id #uuid "8db2a840-a6b6-51d4-8f6c-92bd0dad5406" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id 17592186361466} {:answer/id #uuid "b5c1cb60-43c1-5cc0-a644-c5f45b9d687b" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id 17592186361644} {:answer/id #uuid "c000b11d-6918-5ea4-9dcf-dd56873b8480" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id 17592186361695}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "c000b11d-6918-5ea4-9dcf-dd56873b8480" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id 17592186361695} :answer-field/id #uuid "7e224e7e-dfd1-5c42-9dff-859b4fa1ddef" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id 17592186362086}] :question/difficulty {:db/id 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "e1d8d74b-55cf-51de-803f-0cecc60a4fb1" :question/math-academy-id "q-98714" :question/problem "![](images/6b/6b9b15bd3c8cda84156d5461fb9f80f892a1dba26151cc37d381757f9f659fa1.png)\n\nConsider the differential equation $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=f(y)$ where the graph of $f(y)$ is shown above. Which of the following statements are true?\n\nI. $y=-3$ is a node.\n\nII. $y=2$ is a sink.\n\nIII. $y=2$ is a source." :question/worked-solution "We can classify the equilibrium solutions of an autonomous ODE ${y}^{′}=f(y)$ by examining its phase line.\n\n- An equilibrium solution is a *sink* if the arrows directly above and below it on the phase line both point *toward* the equilibrium solution.\n\n- An equilibrium solution is a *source* if the arrows directly above and below it on the phase line both point *away* from the equilibrium solution.\n\n- An equilibrium solution is a *node* if the arrows directly above and below it on the phase line point in the same direction.\n\nThe phase line that corresponds to the given graph is as follows:\n\n![](images/36/362ffe8871efa8dc597708b039fa766c3603777ba12460e84d9af1fc39800288.png)\n\nTherefore, we have:\n\n- Statement I is true. From the phase line, we see that $y=-3$ is a node.\n\n- Statement II is true, while statement III is false. From the phase line, we see that $y=2$ is a sink.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and II only\"."}] [{:db/id 17592186362319 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id 17592186147495 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "4535b48e-a33f-5f62-95d3-a5c0991c1f6d" :knowledge-point/title "Classifying Equilibrium Points Using a Graph"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "159d1b6f-bc88-5e81-930b-1feb13f58e60" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id 17592186360922} {:answer/id #uuid "3b043f4e-61a3-5d51-8cc8-0b48259e3f97" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id 17592186361106} {:answer/id #uuid "9cd47953-1f4c-5a18-8e96-037941d6ed94" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id 17592186361535} {:answer/id #uuid "b102e3ce-72af-58f5-a986-ba1591632b13" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/iden