From the phase line, we see that $y=2$ is a source.\n\n- Statement III is true. From the phase line, we see that $y=-1$ is a sink.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"III only\"."}] [{:db/id 17592186362322 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id 17592186147495 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "4535b48e-a33f-5f62-95d3-a5c0991c1f6d" :knowledge-point/title "Classifying Equilibrium Points Using a Graph"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "15c32191-765d-58f7-8e7d-323f392118cb" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id 17592186360924} {:answer/id #uuid "46e7e4ee-6254-5172-8b9f-397f96825893" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id 17592186361145} {:answer/id #uuid "a2bc5784-c1de-50bd-9e9e-2bf05ff09080" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id 17592186361564} {:answer/id #uuid "e0f5b8c2-31d6-5107-96f4-ef046c9625b7" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id 17592186361842} {:answer/id #uuid "fa87ecf3-4de2-5513-813c-b3aef6e4035f" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id 17592186361957}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "46e7e4ee-6254-5172-8b9f-397f96825893" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id 17592186361145} :answer-field/id #uuid "43aa3a0a-d08a-5e61-897e-a608c4281c42" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id 17592186362044}] :question/difficulty {:db/id 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "99cb8df0-a236-544f-967a-a702cdee4d6f" :question/math-academy-id "q-98752" :question/problem "![](images/31/3114d1c94b63a2506a66e701346bc7367b901659b78a2cf7c191648c96a716dc.png)\n\nConsider the differential equation $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=f(y)$ where the graph of $f(y)$ is shown above. Which of the following statements are true?\n\nI. $y=3$ is a node.\n\nII. $y=-1$ is a sink.\n\nIII. $y=-1$ is a source." :question/worked-solution "We can classify the equilibrium solutions of an autonomous ODE ${y}^{′}=f(y)$ by examining its phase line.\n\n- An equilibrium solution is a *sink* if the arrows directly above and below it on the phase line both point *toward* the equilibrium solution.\n\n- An equilibrium solution is a *source* if the arrows directly above and below it on the phase line both point *away* from the equilibrium solution.\n\n- An equilibrium solution is a *node* if the arrows directly above and below it on the phase line point in the same direction.\n\nThe phase line that corresponds to the given graph is as follows:\n\n![](images/f7/f72f89ecad74cc708b5950763002757d9d012d3859c7a5bd5eb19ae60fc54e55.png)\n\nTherefore, we have:\n\n- Statement I is false. From the phase line, we see that $y=3$ is a sink.\n\n- Statement II is false while statement III is true. From the phase line, we see that $y=-1$ is a source.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"III only\"."}] [{:db/id 17592186362380 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id 17592186133871 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "67683e7d-bf4f-5fdf-8fe5-df996c30e836" :knowledge-point/title "Determining Whether a Set of Vectors Is Orthogonal With Respect to a Given Inner Product"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "28b3f016-77e0-5fed-9910-f1af71be4795" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id 17592186361014} {:answer/id #uuid "923c31c5-f24c-56e5-be8f-60366c4447f7" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, II, and III" :db/id 17592186361483} {:answer/id #uuid "b6732784-8893-5766-95ac-fa9db15d2b07" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id 17592186361648} {:answer/id #uuid "ea92373e-d3c1-5fff-8902-f0e7baa4aecc" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id 17592186361881} {:answer/id #uuid "ee81316b-dcb2-53d4-888b-baee2761b9c9" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "None of the statements are true" :db/id 17592186361897}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "28b3f016-77e0-5fed-9910-f1af71be4795" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id 17592186361014} :answer-field/id #uuid "ac4be4ad-6b8f-545c-96a1-edc53b2f47ce" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id 17592186362140}] :question/difficulty {:db/id 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "029fdb6a-d6ee-5a1f-bdd5-c75825b67c96" :question/math-academy-id "q-99635" :question/problem "$$\n{v}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}],\\,{v}_{2}=[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}],\\,{v}_{3}=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 2 \\\\ -3\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nConsider the vectors shown above. Given that the inner product in the vector space ${R}^{3}$ is defined as \n\n$$\n⟨x,y⟩={x}_{1}{y}_{1}+2{x}_{2}{y}_{2}+{x}_{3}{y}_{3},\n$$\n\nwhich of the following statements are true?\n\n- ${v}_{1}⊥{v}_{3}$ with respect to the given inner product\n- ${v}_{2}⊥{v}_{3}$ with respect to the given inner product\n- $S={{v}_{1},{v}_{2},{v}_{3}}$ is an orthogonal set with respect to the given inner product" :question/worked-solution "Recall that a set of vectors in an inner product space is orthogonal if all vectors in the set are mutually orthogonal with respect to the given inner product. \n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our statements in turn.\n\n- Statement I is true. Indeed, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}⟨{v}_{1},{v}_{3}⟩ & =(-1)(1)+2(1)(2)+(1)(-3) \\\\  & =(-1)+4-3 \\\\  & =0,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich means that ${v}_{1}⊥{v}_{3}$ with respect to the given inner product.\n\n- Statement I is false since\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}⟨{v}_{2},{v}_{3}⟩ & =(2)(1)+2(-1)(2)+(2)(-3) \\\\  & =2-4-6 \\\\  & =-8\\ne 0,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich means that ${v}_{2}⊥̸{v}_{3}$ with respect to the given inner product.\n\n- Statement III is false. Since ${v}_{2}⊥̸{v}_{3},$ the set $S$ is *not* orthogonal.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only.\""}] [{:db/id 17592186362382 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id 17592186133871 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "67683e7d-bf4f-5fdf-8fe5-df996c30e836" :knowledge-point/title "Determining Whether a Set of Vectors Is Orthogonal With Respect to a Given Inner Product"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2f958a7a-8342-5818-894d-1a1679ed3273" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id 17592186361049} {:answer/id #uuid "3c73dd4f-8bbf-52b2-b7cb-80638367ef82" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id 17592186361115} {:answer/id #uuid "7c8dac09-050a-51b6-8b60-aeeec8412286" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id 17592186361388} {:answer/id #uuid "e4748f74-6269-5282-9135-0525fbf8b22a" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id 17592186361859} {:answer/id #uuid "f5d08f73-6553-5647-88fe-d36c62dfa018" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "None of the statements are true" :db/id 17592186361939}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "3c73dd4f-8bbf-52b2-b7cb-80638367ef82" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id 17592186361115} :answer-field/id #uuid "4c915201-703a-540d-b017-3aa7678e7376" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id 17592186362049}] :question/difficulty {:db/id 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "689e4d4d-2277-5c62-9cd6-9887b81429d8" :question/math-academy-id "q-99656" :question/problem "$$\n{v}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}],\\,{v}_{2}=[\\begin{aligned}4 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}],\\,{v}_{3}=[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nConsider the vectors shown above. Given that the inner product in the vector space ${R}^{3}$ is defined as \n\n$$\n⟨x,y⟩={x}_{1}{y}_{1}+2{x}_{2}{y}_{2}+2{x}_{3}{y}_{3},\n$$\n\nwhich of the following statements are true?\n\n- ${v}_{1}⊥{v}_{2}$ with respect to the given inner product\n- ${v}_{2}⊥̸{v}_{3}$ with respect to the given inner product\n- $S={{v}_{1},{v}_{2},{v}_{3}}$ is an orthogonal set with respect to the given inner product" :question/worked-solution "Recall that a set of vectors in an inner product space is orthogonal if all vectors in the set are mutually orthogonal with respect to the given inner product. \n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our statements in turn.\n\n- Statement I is true. Indeed, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}⟨{v}_{1},{v}_{2}⟩ & =(1)(4)+2(1)(-1)+2(-1)(1) \\\\  & =4-2-2 \\\\  & =0,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich means that ${v}_{1}⊥{v}_{2}$ with respect to the given inner product.\n\n- Statement II is false since \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}⟨{v}_{2},{v}_{3}⟩ & =(4)(0)+2(-1)(1)+2(1)(1) \\\\  & =0-2+2 \\\\  & =0,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich means that ${v}_{2}⊥{v}_{3}$ with respect to the given inner product.\n\n- Statement III is true. Indeed, computing the inner product $⟨{v}_{1},{v}_{3}⟩$, we have \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}⟨{v}_{1},{v}_{3}⟩ & =(1)(0)+2(1)(1)+2(-1)(1) \\\\  & =0+2-2 \\\\  & =0,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich means that ${v}_{1}⊥{v}_{3}$ with respect to the given inner product.\nAs a result, the set $S$ is orthogonal.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and III only.\""}] [{:db/id 17592186362390 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id 17592186133872 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "382a6db7-2708-5711-885e-6f3f3037e8df" :knowledge-point/title "Determining Whether a Set of Functions is Orthogonal With Respect to a Given Inner Product"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "285c955f-80b8-5793-be98-42fb085c48d7" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, II, and III" :db/id 17592186361012} {:answer/id #uuid "51f76ae3-fc53-5ceb-942e-b4248393a927" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id 17592186361197} {:answer/id #uuid "75e99244-27f6-5825-9c3d-c44576dcf7f1" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id 17592186361358} {:answer/id #uuid "955c6050-dc9a-5175-b717-2342b379fb03" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id 17592186361498} {:answer/id #uuid "c37db751-9ad5-5430-ab75-5489d9f259e1" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "None of the statements are true" :db/id 17592186361714}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "75e99244-27f6-5825-9c3d-c44576dcf7f1" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id 17592186361358} :answer-field/id #uuid "8e95ec87-7b1b-553d-bd1d-6b2645d08b87" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id 17592186362111}] :question/difficulty {:db/id 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "06ea44bd-2468-51be-aabc-e31db5c657e5" :question/math-academy-id "q-99815" :question/problem "Consider the following set of functions:\n\n$$\n{f}_{1}(t)=t,\\,{f}_{2}(t)={t}^{2},\\,{f}_{3}(t)=\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\n$$\n\nGiven that the inner product in the vector space $C[-\\pi ,\\pi ]$ of continuous functions over $[-\\pi ,\\pi ]$ is defined as \n\n$$\n⟨f(t),g(t)⟩={\\int }_{-\\pi }^{\\pi }f(t)g(t)\\,\\text{d}t,\n$$\n\nwhich of the following statements are true?\n\n- ${f}_{1}(t)⊥{f}_{2}(t)$ with respect to the given inner product\n- ${f}_{1}(t)⊥{f}_{3}(t)$ with respect to the given inner product\n- $S={{f}_{1}(t),{f}_{2}(t),{f}_{3}(t)}$ is an orthogonal set with respect to the given inner product\n\n*Hint: $⟨{f}_{2},{f}_{3}⟩=0.$*" :question/worked-solution "Recall that a set of vectors in an inner product space is called orthogonal if all vectors in the set are mutually orthogonal with respect to the given inner product. \n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our statements in turn.\n\n- Statement I is true. We have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}⟨{f}_{1}(t),{f}_{2}(t)⟩ & =⟨t,{t}^{2}⟩ \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-\\pi }^{\\pi }t\\cdot {t}^{2}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-\\pi }^{\\pi }{t}^{3}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =\\frac{{t}^{4}}{4}{|}_{-\\pi }^{\\pi } \\\\  & =\\frac{{\\pi }^{4}}{4}-(\\frac{(-\\pi )^{4}}{4}) \\\\  & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis means that ${f}_{1}(t)⊥{f}_{2}(t)$ with respect to the given inner product.\n\n- Statement II is false. We have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}⟨{f}_{1}(t),{f}_{3}(t)⟩=⟨t,\\operatorname{sin}⁡t⟩={\\int }_{-\\pi }^{\\pi }t\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,\\text{d}t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet's use integration by parts. We denote\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u=t & ⟹ & {u}^{′}=1, \\\\ {v}^{′}=\\operatorname{sin}⁡t & ⟹ & v=\\int {v}^{′}\\,\\text{d}t=\\int \\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,\\text{d}t=-\\operatorname{cos}⁡t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, applying the integration by parts formula, we obtain \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-\\pi }^{\\pi }t\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,\\text{d}t & =uv{|}_{-\\pi }^{\\pi }-{\\int }_{-\\pi }^{\\pi }{u}^{′}v\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =(-t\\operatorname{cos}⁡t){|}_{-\\pi }^{\\pi }-{\\int }_{-\\pi }^{\\pi }(-\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =(-\\pi \\cdot \\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\pi )-(-(-\\pi )\\cdot \\operatorname{cos}⁡(-\\pi )))+\\operatorname{sin}⁡t{|}_{-\\pi }^{\\pi } \\\\  & =\\pi +\\pi +(\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\pi )-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(-\\pi )) \\\\  & =2\\pi \\ne 0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis means that ${f}_{1}(t)⊥̸{f}_{3}(t)$ with respect to the given inner product.\n\n- Statement III is false. Since ${f}_{1}(t)⊥̸{f}_{3}(t),$ the set $S$ is *not* orthogonal.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only.\""}] [{:db/id 17592186362391 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id 17592186133872 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "382a6db7-2708-5711-885e-6f3f3037e8df" :knowledge-point/title "Determining Whether a Set of Functions is Orthogonal With Respect to a Given Inner Product"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "6cc59c81-637e-52e7-92da-29524f62b7e3" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id 17592186361322} {:answer/id #uuid "802fb970-ace7-5f4e-97e6-b37c8d31083d" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id 17592186361407} {:answer/id #uuid "899a8db7-6c1c-587d-911a-d033dfff4c88" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "None of the statements are true" :db/id 17592186361448} {:answer/id #uuid "a5985011-8eed-525d-a132-fa5c90200b48" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id 17592186361579} {:answer/id #uuid "c084fbdf-f594-575b-a303-5e655bf5a067" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, II, and III" :db/id 17592186361697}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "802fb970-ace7-5f4e-97e6-b37c8d31083d" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id 17592186361407} :answer-field/id #uuid "45b83442-8c98-555a-a0e1-62d1cef63835" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id 17592186362045}] :question/difficulty {:db/id 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "5c3132dd-8104-5475-a04a-6ba3130fe419" :question/math-academy-id "q-99822" :question/problem "Consider the following set of functions:\n\n$$\n{f}_{1}(t)=\\operatorname{cos}⁡t,\\,{f}_{2}(t)={sin}^{2}⁡t,\\,{f}_{3}(t)=1\n$$\n\nGiven that the inner product in the vector space $C[0,\\pi ]$ of continuous functions over $[0,\\pi ]$ is defined as \n\n$$\n⟨f(t),g(t)⟩={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }f(t)g(t)\\,\\text{d}t,\n$$\n\nwhich of the following statements are true?\n\nI. ${f}_{1}(t)⊥{f}_{3}(t)$ with respect to the given inner product\n\nII. ${f}_{2}(t)⊥{f}_{3}(t)$ with respect to the given inner product\n\nIII. $S=\\{{f}_{1}(t),{f}_{2}(t),{f}_{3}(t)\\}$ is an orthogonal set with respect to the given inner product\n\n*Hint: $⟨{f}_{1},{f}_{2}⟩=0.$*" :question/worked-solution "Recall that a set of vectors in an inner product space is called orthogonal if all vectors in the set are mutually orthogonal with respect to the given inner product. \n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our statements in turn.\n\n- Statement I is true. We have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}⟨{f}_{1}(t),{f}_{3}(t)⟩ & =⟨\\operatorname{cos}⁡t,1⟩ \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =\\operatorname{sin}⁡t{|}_{0}^{\\pi } \\\\  & =\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\pi -\\operatorname{sin}⁡0 \\\\  & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis means that ${f}_{1}(t)⊥{f}_{3}(t)$ with respect to the given inner product.\n\n- Statement II is false. We have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}⟨{f}_{2}(t),{f}_{3}(t)⟩=⟨{sin}^{2}⁡t,1⟩={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }{sin}^{2}⁡t\\,\\text{d}t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can evaluate this integral using the identity ${sin}^{2}⁡\\alpha =\\frac{1-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2\\alpha )}{2}$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }{sin}^{2}⁡t\\,\\text{d}t & =\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }(1-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t))\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}[t-\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t)}{2}]{|}_{0}^{\\pi } \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}[\\pi -\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2\\pi )}{2}-(0-\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡0}{2})] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}[\\pi -0-(0-0)] \\\\  & =\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\ne 0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis means that ${f}_{2}(t)⊥̸{f}_{3}(t)$ with respect to the given inner product.\n\n- Statement III is false. Since ${f}_{2}(t)⊥̸{f}_{3}(t),$ the set $S$ is *not* orthogonal.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only.\""}] [{:db/id 17592186362392 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id 17592186133872 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "382a6db7-2708-5711-885e-6f3f3037e8df" :knowledge-point/title "Determining Whether a Set of Functions is Orthogonal With Respect to a Given Inner Product"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0c4a1f08-b0f1-5a1b-acc0-21bb784ad758" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id 17592186360885} {:answer/id #uuid "4bb8d109-8a0a-5cd9-b031-5643137e401f" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, II, and III" :db/id 17592186361165} {:answer/id #uuid "6ec89f85-3d50-5b28-9284-a6ca3b103f85" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "None of the statements are true" :db/id 17592186361329} {:answer/id #uuid "7aa7dda3-d138-5e2f-93cb-6aa10e847b8a" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id 17592186361379} {:answer/id #uuid "d3c106d9-8717-568d-b8c9-93966eb2cbe4" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id 17592186361785}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "4bb8d109-8a0a-5cd9-b031-5643137e401f" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, II, and III" :db/id 17592186361165} :answer-field/id #uuid "bd0740c3-55af-55b0-9072-37fabbd9d3dd" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id 17592186362153}] :question/difficulty {:db/id 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "30fad2b1-33b1-5011-baca-2c53469ec04d" :question/math-academy-id "q-99827" :question/problem "Consider the following set of functions:\n\n$$\n{f}_{1}(t)=1,\\,{f}_{2}(t)=\\operatorname{cos}⁡t,\\,{f}_{3}(t)=\\operatorname{cos}⁡2t\n$$\n\nGiven that the inner product in the vector space $C[-\\pi ,\\pi ]$ of continuous functions over $[-\\pi ,\\pi ]$ is defined as \n\n$$\n⟨f(t),g(t)⟩={\\int }_{-\\pi }^{\\pi }f(t)g(t)\\,\\text{d}t,\n$$\n\nwhich of the following statements are true?\n\nI. ${f}_{1}(t)⊥{f}_{2}(t)$ with respect to the given inner product\n\nII. ${f}_{1}(t)⊥{f}_{3}(t)$ with respect to the given inner product\n\nIII. $S=\\{{f}_{1}(t),{f}_{2}(t),{f}_{3}(t)\\}$ is an orthogonal set with respect to the given inner product\n\n*Hint: $⟨{f}_{2},{f}_{3}⟩=0$*" :question/worked-solution "Recall that a set of vectors in an inner product space is called orthogonal if all vectors in the set are mutually orthogonal with respect to the given inner product. \n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our statements in turn.\n\n- Statement I is true. We have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}⟨{f}_{1}(t),{f}_{2}(t)⟩ & =⟨1,\\operatorname{cos}⁡t⟩ \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-\\pi }^{\\pi }\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =\\operatorname{sin}⁡t{|}_{-\\pi }^{\\pi } \\\\  & =\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\pi -\\operatorname{sin}⁡(-\\pi ) \\\\  & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis means that ${f}_{1}(t)⊥{f}_{2}(t)$ with respect to the given inner product.\n\n- Statement II is true. We have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}⟨{f}_{1}(t),{f}_{3}(t)⟩ & =⟨1,\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t)⟩ \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-\\pi }^{\\pi }\\operatorname{cos}⁡2t\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t)}{2}{|}_{-\\pi }^{\\pi } \\\\  & =\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2\\pi )}{2}-\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡(-2\\pi )}{2} \\\\  & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis means that ${f}_{1}(t)⊥{f}_{3}(t)$ with respect to the given inner product.\n\n- Statement III is true. Since ${f}_{1}(t)⊥{f}_{2}(t),$ ${f}_{1}(t)⊥{f}_{3}(t),$ and ${f}_{2}(t)⊥{f}_{3}(t),$ the set $S$ is orthogonal.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I, II, and III.\""}] [{:db/id 17592186362393 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id 17592186133872 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "382a6db7-2708-5711-885e-6f3f3037e8df" :knowledge-point/title "Determining Whether a Set of Functions is Orthogonal With Respect to a Given Inner Product"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "21aa3b60-7035-52b5-ae28-6a977fededb5" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id 17592186360981} {:answer/id #uuid "58c19846-4823-596f-a585-42d6d6d0e029" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id 17592186361239} {:answer/id #uuid "6335a30d-dbe5-5961-82f9-99190d2da6ce" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id 17592186361285} {:answer/id #uuid "9912ae7b-51ba-5ed4-bb12-f35d5c8f549d" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, II, and III" :db/id 17592186361519} {:answer/id #uuid "9ee38031-cb20-5a55-be0b-b36494c72614" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "None of the statements are true" :db/id 17592186361549}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "9912ae7b-51ba-5ed4-bb12-f35d5c8f549d" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, II, and III" :db/id 17592186361519} :answer-field/id #uuid "18c70965-ac2f-536b-80d9-b8024b87c810" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id 17592186362002}] :question/difficulty {:db/id 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "4c64aee5-b8e4-5802-9d88-5d5ccb488483" :question/math-academy-id "q-99839" :question/problem "Consider the following set of functions:\n\n$$\n{f}_{1}(t)=\\operatorname{sin}⁡t,\\,{f}_{2}(t)=\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t),\\,{f}_{3}(t)=\\operatorname{sin}⁡(5t)\n$$\n\nGiven that the inner product in the vector space $C[0,\\pi /2]$ of continuous functions over $[0,\\pi /2]$ is defined as \n\n$$\n⟨f(t),g(t)⟩={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}f(t)g(t)\\,\\text{d}t,\n$$\n\nwhich of the following statements are true?\n\nI. ${f}_{1}(t)⊥{f}_{2}(t)$ with respect to the given inner product\n\nII. ${f}_{1}(t)⊥{f}_{3}(t)$ with respect to the given inner product\n\nIII. $S=\\{{f}_{1}(t),{f}_{2}(t),{f}_{3}(t)\\}$ is an orthogonal set with respect to the given inner product\n\n*Hint: $⟨{f}_{2},{f}_{3}⟩=0.$*" :question/worked-solution "Recall that a set of vectors in an inner product space is called orthogonal if all vectors in the set are mutually orthogonal with respect to the given inner product. \n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our statements in turn.\n\n- Statement I is true. We have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}⟨{f}_{1}(t),{f}_{2}(t)⟩=⟨\\operatorname{sin}⁡t,\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t)⟩={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t)\\,\\text{d}t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can evaluate this integral using the identity $\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\alpha \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\beta =\\frac{1}{2}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\alpha -\\beta )-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\alpha +\\beta ))$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t)\\,\\text{d}t & =\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡(t-3t)-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(t+3t))\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡(-2t)-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4t))\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}(-\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡(-2t)}{2}-\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4t)}{4}){|}_{0}^{\\pi /2} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}(-\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡(-\\pi )}{2}-\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2\\pi )}{4})-\\frac{1}{2}(-\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡0}{2}-\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡0}{4}) \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot 0+\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot 0 \\\\  & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis means that ${f}_{1}(t)⊥{f}_{2}(t)$ with respect to the given inner product.\n\n- Statement II is true. We have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}⟨{f}_{1}(t),{f}_{3}(t)⟩=⟨\\operatorname{sin}⁡t,\\operatorname{sin}⁡(5t)⟩={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\operatorname{sin}⁡(5t)\\,\\text{d}t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can evaluate this integral using the identity $\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\alpha \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\beta =\\frac{1}{2}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\alpha -\\beta )-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\alpha +\\beta ))$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,\\operatorname{sin}⁡(5t)\\,\\text{d}t & =\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡(t-5t)-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(t+5t))\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡(-4t)-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(6t))\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}(-\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡(-4t)}{4}-\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡(6t)}{6}){|}_{0}^{\\pi /2} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}(-\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡(-2\\pi )}{4}-\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3\\pi )}{6})-\\frac{1}{2}(-\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡0}{4}-\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡0}{6}) \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot 0+\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot 0 \\\\  & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis means that ${f}_{1}(t)⊥{f}_{3}(t)$ with respect to the given inner product.\n\n- Statement III is true. Since ${f}_{1}(t)⊥{f}_{2}(t),$ ${f}_{1}(t)⊥{f}_{3}(t),$ and ${f}_{2}(t)⊥{f}_{3}(t),$ the set $S$ is orthogonal.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I, II, and III.\""}] [{:db/id 17592186362394 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id 17592186133872 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "382a6db7-2708-5711-885e-6f3f3037e8df" :knowledge-point/title "Determining Whether a Set of Functions is Orthogonal With Respect to a Given Inner Product"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0b2afcc6-423f-56e3-9376-3f6d5d86a10b" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, II, and III" :db/id 17592186360879} {:answer/id #uuid "1ab956b4-1ee5-50bb-813d-db356da427fc" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "None of the statements are true" :db/id 17592186360943} {:answer/id #uuid "3894fd16-9f41-540a-9bfe-664b848e325b" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id 17592186361095} {:answer/id #uuid "561b7b00-721a-5ba0-84d3-1e037016c7d3" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id 17592186361225} {:answer/id #uuid "74fb95c8-2498-5319-a7fb-0e6c9083d7cd" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id 17592186361352}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "0b2afcc6-423f-56e3-9376-3f6d5d86a10b" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, II, and III" :db/id 17592186360879} :answer-field/id #uuid "8cfa4db9-4fb3-5f43-aab3-4522533819bd" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id 17592186362104}] :question/difficulty {:db/id 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "8889f645-9334-518f-83dc-7064dd4f1d1d" :question/math-academy-id "q-99848" :question/problem "Consider the following set of functions:\n\n$$\n{f}_{1}(t)=t,\\,{f}_{2}(t)={t}^{2},\\,{f}_{3}(t)=t-\\frac{5}{12}{t}^{3}\n$$\n\nGiven that the inner product in the vector space $C[-2,2]$ of continuous functions over $[-2,2]$ is defined as \n\n$$\n⟨f(t),g(t)⟩={\\int }_{-2}^{2}f(t)g(t)\\,\\text{d}t,\n$$\n\nwhich of the following statements are true?\n\n- ${f}_{1}(t)⊥{f}_{2}(t)$ with respect to the given inner product\n- ${f}_{1}(t)⊥{f}_{3}(t)$ with respect to the given inner product\n- $S={{f}_{1}(t),{f}_{2}(t),{f}_{3}(t)}$ is an orthogonal set with respect to the given inner product\n\n*Hint: $⟨{f}_{2},{f}_{3}⟩=0.$*" :question/worked-solution "Recall that a set of vectors in an inner product space is called orthogonal if all vectors in the set are mutually orthogonal with respect to the given inner product. \n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our statements in turn.\n\n- Statement I is true. We have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}⟨{f}_{1}(t),{f}_{2}(t)⟩ & =⟨t,{t}^{2}⟩ \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-2}^{2}t\\cdot {t}^{2}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-2}^{2}{t}^{3}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =\\frac{{t}^{4}}{4}{|}_{-2}^{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{{2}^{4}}{4}-(\\frac{(-2)^{4}}{4}) \\\\  & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis means that ${f}_{1}(t)⊥{f}_{2}(t)$ with respect to the given inner product.\n\n- Statement II is true. We have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}⟨{f}_{1}(t),{f}_{3}(t)⟩ & =⟨t,t-\\frac{5}{12}{t}^{3}⟩ \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-2}^{2}t\\cdot (t-\\frac{5}{12}{t}^{3})\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-2}^{2}({t}^{2}-\\frac{5}{12}{t}^{4})\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =(\\frac{{t}^{3}}{3}-\\frac{1}{12}{t}^{5}){|}_{-2}^{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{{2}^{3}}{3}-\\frac{1}{12}{2}^{5}-(\\frac{(-2)^{3}}{3}-\\frac{1}{12}(-2)^{5}) \\\\  & =\\frac{16}{3}-\\frac{16}{3} \\\\  & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis means that ${f}_{1}(t)⊥{f}_{3}(t)$ with respect to the given inner product.\n\n- Statement III is true. Since ${f}_{1}(t)⊥{f}_{2}(t),$ ${f}_{1}(t)⊥{f}_{3}(t),$ and ${f}_{2}(t)⊥{f}_{3}(t),$ the set $S$ is orthogonal.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I, II, and III.\""}] [{:db/id 17592186362395 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id 17592186133871 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "67683e7d-bf4f-5fdf-8fe5-df996c30e836" :knowledge-point/title "Determining Whether a Set of Vectors Is Orthogonal With Respect to a Given Inner Product"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "059cde48-5bc6-5673-868b-ea8d54a2ceaa" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, II, and III" :db/id 17592186360852} {:answer/id #uuid "0648bfc8-3cf5-525d-952e-35798fa034ab" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "None of the statements are true" :db/id 17592186360856} {:answer/id #uuid "071c6897-6b9c-507f-afc9-a42fa5595c82" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id 17592186360861} {:answer/id #uuid "23b3ad14-ea1f-5ed1-8153-b4af930d27f7" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id 17592186360989} {:answer/id #uuid "f9448014-3f39-5aa8-ad45-ffec33f870a9" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id 17592186361954}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "059cde48-5bc6-5673-868b-ea8d54a2ceaa" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, II, and III" :db/id 17592186360852} :answer-field/id #uuid "165bbc62-0650-50d6-9912-f876af3735c8" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id 17592186362000}] :question/difficulty {:db/id 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "021a928d-5bbc-5ce6-b2a2-f571107e0c32" :question/math-academy-id "q-99875" :question/problem "$$\n{v}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}],\\,{v}_{2}=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 3 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}],\\,{v}_{3}=[\\begin{aligned}4 \\\\ 0 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nConsider the vectors shown above. Given that the inner product in the vector space ${R}^{3}$ is defined as \n\n$$\n⟨x,y⟩={x}_{1}{y}_{1}+3{x}_{2}{y}_{2}+2{x}_{3}{y}_{3},\n$$\n\nwhich of the following statements are true?\n\n- ${v}_{1}⊥{v}_{2}$ with respect to the given inner product\n- ${v}_{2}⊥{v}_{3}$ with respect to the given inner product\n- $S={{v}_{1},{v}_{2},{v}_{3}}$ is an orthogonal set with respect to the given inner product" :question/worked-solution "Recall that a set of vectors in an inner product space is orthogonal if all vectors in the set are mutually orthogonal with respect to the given inner product. \n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our statements in turn.\n\n- Statement I is true. Indeed, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}⟨{v}_{1},{v}_{2}⟩ & =(1)(1)+3(-1)(3)+2(2)(2) \\\\  & =1-9+8 \\\\  & =0,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich means that ${v}_{1}⊥{v}_{2}$ with respect to the given inner product.\n\n- Statement II is true. Indeed, we have \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}⟨{v}_{2},{v}_{3}⟩ & =(1)(4)+3(3)(0)+2(2)(-1) \\\\  & =4+0-4 \\\\  & =0,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich means that ${v}_{2}⊥{v}_{3}$ with respect to the given inner product.\n\n- Statement III is true. Indeed, computing the inner product $⟨{v}_{1},{v}_{3}⟩$, we have \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}⟨{v}_{1},{v}_{3}⟩ & =(1)(4)+3(-1)(0)+2(2)(-1) \\\\  & =4+0-4 \\\\  & =0,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich means that ${v}_{1}⊥{v}_{3}$ with respect to the given inner product.\nAs a result, the set $S$ is orthogonal.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I, II, and III.\""}] [{:db/id 17592186362396 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id 17592186133871 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "67683e7d-bf4f-5fdf-8fe5-df996c30e836" :knowledge-point/title "Determining Whether a Set of Vectors Is Orthogonal With Respect to a Given Inner Product"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1e459610-671a-55f5-926f-d836642ee1e7" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id 17592186360962} {:answer/id #uuid "42a56c57-c027-5da1-a918-c982043c4b74" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id 17592186361130} {:answer/id #uuid "92d764c8-5589-5c86-afcb-1260cce729d0" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id 17592186361488} {:answer/id #uuid "a80ac5e3-0988-5dff-9337-81973915b4a3" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id 17592186361588} {:answer/id #uuid "e52bbe28-55bf-50c5-88e9-b24d3a5bf912" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "None of the statements is true" :db/id 17592186361860}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "92d764c8-5589-5c86-afcb-1260cce729d0" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id 17592186361488} :answer-field/id #uuid "155818f2-c6ce-5084-8af3-263d5097e9fc" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id 17592186361999}] :question/difficulty {:db/id 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "8a41208f-2a14-503d-a1ad-b859e476f0c0" :question/math-academy-id "q-99878" :question/problem "$$\n{v}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}],\\,{v}_{2}=[\\begin{aligned}5 \\\\ -2 \\\\ -4\\end{aligned}],\\,{v}_{3}=[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 2 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nConsider the vectors shown above. Given that the inner product in the vector space ${R}^{3}$ is defined as \n\n$$\n⟨x,y⟩=2{x}_{1}{y}_{1}+{x}_{2}{y}_{2}+{x}_{3}{y}_{3},\n$$\n\nwhich of the following statements are true?\n\n- ${v}_{1}⊥{v}_{2}$ with respect to the given inner product\n- ${v}_{1}⊥̸{v}_{3}$ with respect to the given inner product\n- $S={{v}_{1},{v}_{2},{v}_{3}}$ is an orthogonal set with respect to the given inner product" :question/worked-solution "Recall that a set of vectors in an inner product space is orthogonal if all vectors in the set are mutually orthogonal with respect to the given inner product. \n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our statements in turn.\n\n- Statement I is true. Indeed, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}⟨{v}_{1},{v}_{2}⟩ & =2(1)(5)+(1)(-2)+(2)(-4) \\\\  & =10-2-8 \\\\  & =0,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich means that ${v}_{1}⊥{v}_{2}$ with respect to the given inner product.\n\n- Statement II is false since \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}⟨{v}_{1},{v}_{3}⟩ & =2(1)(0)+(1)(2)+(2)(-1) \\\\  & =0+2-2 \\\\  & =0,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich means that ${v}_{1}⊥{v}_{3}$ with respect to the given inner product.\n\n- Statement III is true. Indeed, computing the inner product $⟨{v}_{2},{v}_{3}⟩$, we have \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}⟨{v}_{2},{v}_{3}⟩ & =2(5)(0)+(-2)(2)+(-4)(-1) \\\\  & =0-4+4 \\\\  & =0,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich means that ${v}_{2}⊥{v}_{3}$ with respect to the given inner product.\nAs a result, the set $S$ is orthogonal.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and III only.\""}] [{:db/id 17592186362397 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id 17592186133872 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "382a6db7-2708-5711-885e-6f3f3037e8df" :knowledge-point/title "Determining Whether a Set of Functions is Orthogonal With Respect to a Given Inner Product"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "09d64f7c-60c5-5475-9188-10f0ad37b369" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "None of the statements are true" :db/id 17592186360874} {:answer/id #uuid "49ff6a2a-f8f1-536c-9010-d3253c2c863c" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id 17592186361155} {:answer/id #uuid "553acf95-4d47-5e87-b0d5-91b5d4c18302" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, II, and III" :db/id 17592186361219} {:answer/id #uuid "bb691514-1033-5141-87e5-6d94196c1e3d" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id 17592186361672} {:answer/id #uuid "bdf8a54d-2d6f-5788-8852-403dfb668e5b" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id 17592186361684}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "bdf8a54d-2d6f-5788-8852-403dfb668e5b" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id 17592186361684} :answer-field/id #uuid "3e284079-743c-5982-b9eb-0f2f9cb7833b" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id 17592186362038}] :question/difficulty {:db/id 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "d313e454-e9a2-5532-b1b1-37c4d7cbbe80" :question/math-academy-id "q-99951" :question/problem "Consider the following set of functions:\n\n$$\n{f}_{1}(t)={e}^{t},\\,{f}_{2}(t)=t{e}^{-t}-{e}^{-t},\\,{f}_{3}(t)={e}^{-t}\n$$\n\nGiven that the inner product in the vector space $C[0,2]$ of continuous functions over $[0,2]$ is defined as \n\n$$\n⟨f(t),g(t)⟩={\\int }_{0}^{2}f(t)g(t)\\,\\text{d}t,\n$$\n\nwhich of the following statements are true?\n\n- ${f}_{1}(t)⊥{f}_{2}(t)$ with respect to the given inner product\n- ${f}_{1}(t)⊥{f}_{3}(t)$ with respect to the given inner product\n- $S={{f}_{1}(t),{f}_{2}(t),{f}_{3}(t)}$ is an orthogonal set with respect to the given inner product" :question/worked-solution "Recall that a set of vectors in an inner product space is called orthogonal if all vectors in the set are mutually orthogonal with respect to the given inner product. \n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our statements in turn.\n\n- Statement I is true. We have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}⟨{f}_{1}(t),{f}_{2}(t)⟩ & =⟨{e}^{t},t{e}^{-t}-{e}^{-t}⟩ \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}{e}^{t}(t{e}^{-t}-{e}^{-t})\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}(t-1)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =(\\frac{{t}^{2}}{2}-t){|}_{0}^{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{{2}^{2}}{2}-2-(0-0) \\\\  & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis means that ${f}_{1}(t)⊥{f}_{2}(t)$ with respect to the given inner product.\n\n- Statement II is false. We have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}⟨{f}_{1}(t),{f}_{3}(t)⟩ & =⟨{e}^{t},{e}^{-t}⟩ \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}{e}^{t}\\cdot {e}^{-t}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}\\text{d}t \\\\  & =t{|}_{0}^{2} \\\\  & =2-0 \\\\  & =2\\ne 0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis means that ${f}_{1}(t)⊥̸{f}_{3}(t)$ with respect to the given inner product.\n\n- Statement III is false. Since ${f}_{1}(t)⊥̸{f}_{3}(t),$ the set $S$ is *not* orthogonal.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only.\""}] [{:db/id 17592186362675 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id 17592186152224 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "a0abad78-e492-5d09-a04d-bce54605c78c" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Generalized Eigenvectors of a 2x2 Matrix"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "8b0fb635-b2b2-5e34-ac62-08f8f2e8ae69" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "None of the statements are true" :db/id 17592186362677} {:answer/id #uuid "cb13e4e3-d7df-540c-a097-57428d520b7b" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id 17592186362678} {:answer/id #uuid "3bc61d98-b92d-5a97-ae66-d8c9383a525f" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id 17592186362679} {:answer/id #uuid "8d450859-7640-5bcf-b3b8-9063923d9bb9" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id 17592186362680} {:answer/id #uuid "0a3d8b10-13ac-534a-8c6c-8075fbd6d6b9" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id 17592186362681}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "0a3d8b10-13ac-534a-8c6c-8075fbd6d6b9" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id 17592186362681} :answer-field/id #uuid "50e44f68-8e03-5772-829b-5a09815b3ae9" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id 17592186362676}] :question/difficulty {:db/id 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "92a6911e-532d-5f89-96e6-761d0ceb55f3" :question/math-academy-id "q-109487" :question/problem "$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}-1 & -3 \\\\ 3 & 5\\end{aligned}],\\,v=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nConsider the matrix $A$ and vector $v$ shown above. Which of the following statements are true?\n\n- $v$ is a generalized eigenvector of rank $1$ corresponding to the eigenvalue $\\lambda =2$ of $A$\n- $v$ is a generalized eigenvector of rank $2$ corresponding to the eigenvalue $\\lambda =2$ of $A$\n- $v$ is an eigenvector corresponding to the eigenvalue $\\lambda =2$ of $A$" :question/worked-solution "A *generalized eigenvector* ${v}_{2}$ of rank $2$ corresponding to the eigenvalue $\\lambda $ of a matrix $A$ is a nonzero vector such that\n\n$$\n{v}_{2}\\overset{A-\\lambda I\\,}{\\to }{v}_{1}\\overset{A-\\lambda I\\,}{\\to }0,\n$$\n\nwhere ${v}_{1}\\ne 0.$ Recall that an *eigenvector* is simply a generalized eigenvector of rank $1.$\n\nWith this in mind, let's examine our statements.\n\nFirst, we compute $A-2I:$\n\n$$\nA-2I=[\\begin{aligned}-1 & -3 \\\\ 3 & 5\\end{aligned}]-2[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}-3 & -3 \\\\ 3 & 3\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\n- Next, we find the image of $v$ under the action of $A-2I:$\n\n$$\n(A-2I)v=[\\begin{aligned}-3 & -3 \\\\ 3 & 3\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]={{[\\begin{aligned}-6 \\\\ 6\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{w}\\ne 0\n$$\n\n- Since we got a nonzero vector in the last step, we now compute the image of $w$ under the action of $A-2I:$\n\n$$\n(A-2I)w=[\\begin{aligned}-3 & -3 \\\\ 3 & 3\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}-6 \\\\ 6\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]=0\n$$\n\nSo, we obtain the following chain of maps:\n\n$$\n{{[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{v}\\overset{A-2I\\,}{\\to }{{[\\begin{aligned}-6 \\\\ 6\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{w}\\overset{A-2I\\,}{\\to }{{[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{0}\n$$\n\nHere, we see that $A-2I$ maps $v$ to $0$ in $2$ steps.\n\nTherefore:\n\n$\\,$ *$v$ is a generalized eigenvector of rank $2$ corresponding to the eigenvalue $\\lambda =2$ of $A.$*\n\nSo, the correct answer is \"II only.\""}] [{:db/id 17592186362682 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id 17592186152226 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "76e60bc4-ae61-5c39-ad80-14e0cdab14da" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Generalized Eigenvectors of a 3x3 Matrix"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2f322b92-e727-56f8-b965-168eeb7ec238" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "None of the statements are true" :db/id 17592186362684} {:answer/id #uuid "fea449ab-857f-5e97-a2dd-9a5decfbfe3b" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id 17592186362685} {:answer/id #uuid "e5e9c614-fb32-5fc3-a8e1-80768c7a6164" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id 17592186362686} {:answer/id #uuid "fd8fd250-9f94-565b-afb8-d6406e6e4d2c" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id 17592186362687} {:answer/id #uuid "e4559c11-53ee-582b-ac61-017f9bf594ba" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id 17592186362688}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "2f322b92-e727-56f8-b965-168eeb7ec238" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "None of the statements are true" :db/id 17592186362684} :answer-field/id #uuid "dc073027-8d63-5f72-bb4e-ddd774541c01" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id 17592186362683}] :question/difficulty {:db/id 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "83c9089c-246d-51fb-b493-4538acc22376" :question/math-academy-id "q-109574" :question/problem "$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}-3 & 2 & -2 \\\\ -1 & 1 & -2 \\\\ 1 & 0 & -1\\end{aligned}],\\,v=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nConsider the matrix $A$ and vector $v$ shown above. Which of the following statements are true?\n\nI. $v$ is an eigenvector corresponding to the eigenvalue $\\lambda =-1$ of $A$\n\nII. $v$ is a generalized eigenvector of rank $1$ corresponding to the eigenvalue $\\lambda =-1$ of $A$\n\nIII. $v$ is a generalized eigenvector of rank $2$ corresponding to the eigenvalue $\\lambda =-1$ of $A$" :question/worked-solution "A *generalized eigenvector* ${v}_{n}$ of rank $n$ corresponding to the eigenvalue $\\lambda $ of a matrix $A$ is a nonzero vector such that\n\n$$\n{v}_{n}\\overset{A-\\lambda I\\,}{\\to }{v}_{n-1}\\overset{A-\\lambda I\\,}{\\to }…\\overset{A-\\lambda I\\,}{\\to }{v}_{1}\\overset{A-\\lambda I\\,}{\\to }0,\n$$\n\nwhere ${v}_{n-1},{v}_{n-2},…,{v}_{1}\\ne 0.$ An *eigenvector* is simply a generalized eigenvector of rank $1.$\n\nFirst, we compute $A+I:$\n\n$$\nA+I=[\\begin{aligned}-3 & 2 & -2 \\\\ -1 & 1 & -2 \\\\ 1 & 0 & -1\\end{aligned}]+[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}-2 & 2 & -2 \\\\ -1 & 2 & -2 \\\\ 1 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\n- Next, we find the image of $v$ under the action of $A+I:$\n\n$$\n(A+I)v=[\\begin{aligned}-2 & 2 & -2 \\\\ -1 & 2 & -2 \\\\ 1 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]={{[\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{w}\\ne 0\n$$\n\n- Since we got a nonzero vector, we now compute the image of $w$ under the action of $A+I:$\n\n$$\n(A+I)w=[\\begin{aligned}-2 & 2 & -2 \\\\ -1 & 2 & -2 \\\\ 1 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]={{[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ -2 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{x}\\ne 0\n$$\n\n- Again, since we got a nonzero vector in the last step, we now compute the image of $x$ under the action of $A+I:$\n\n$$\n(A+I)x=[\\begin{aligned}-2 & 2 & -2 \\\\ -1 & 2 & -2 \\\\ 1 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ -2 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]=0\n$$\n\nSo, we obtain the following chain of maps:\n\n$$\n{{[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{v}\\overset{A+I\\,}{\\to }{{[\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{w}\\overset{A+I\\,}{\\to }{{[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ -2 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{x}\\overset{A+I\\,}{\\to }{{[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{0}\n$$\n\nHere, we see that $A+I$ maps $v$ to $0$ in $3$ steps.\n\nTherefore:\n\n$\\,$ *$v$ is a generalized eigenvector of rank $3$ corresponding to the eigenvalue $\\lambda =-1$ of $A.$*\n\nSo, the correct answer is \"None of the statements are true.\""}] [{:db/id 17592186362710 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id 17592186133885 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "5ada5d20-89d8-5bc9-9b10-172356be0eb4" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying an Orthogonal Fundamental Subspaces of a Matrix"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "501cfa61-1bae-5493-9927-44e080eedcb6" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id 17592186362712} {:answer/id #uuid "e79a9ced-86df-5aba-ba5d-8b38b1a30f4e" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id 17592186362713} {:answer/id #uuid "d78089f5-2aad-5e43-b5a0-cc7b67658993" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id 17592186362714} {:answer/id #uuid "1fbd0e7e-1a3f-539f-916d-f095722f68be" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id 17592186362715} {:answer/id #uuid "149e462a-2fa3-515b-a582-18f8ee2ca359" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id 17592186362716}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "e79a9ced-86df-5aba-ba5d-8b38b1a30f4e" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id 17592186362713} :answer-field/id #uuid "fe489986-82fe-5320-9907-156feba7cc97" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id 17592186362711}] :question/difficulty {:db/id 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "7b12af04-ad27-575e-b0d1-aaf05c9b69e1" :question/math-academy-id "q-109774" :question/problem "Which of the following subspaces must be orthogonal to $\\text{Null}({A}^{T})$ for every matrix $A?$\n\n- $\\text{Col}(A)$\n- $\\text{Null}(A)$\n- $\\text{Col}({A}^{T})$" :question/worked-solution "Recall that $\\text{Null}({A}^{T})$ is the left null space of $A.$ This consists of vectors that, when pre-multiplied by ${A}^{T},$ result in the zero-vector $0.$\n\nWhen pre-multiplying a vector by ${A}^{T},$ we actually multiply each row of ${A}^{T}$, that is, each column of $A$, by that vector. So, $\\text{Null}({A}^{T})$ must be orthogonal to the column space of $A,$ which is $\\text{Col}(A).$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only.\"\n\n**Note:** In general, the four fundamental subspaces of a matrix $A$ can be schematically visualized, as shown below.\n\n![](images/de/de22dd5b352e1d5be48f4b6ed3b2a4657ae623c1bd288d3464559758ea334af7.png)"}] [{:db/id 17592186362717 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id 17592186133885 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "5ada5d20-89d8-5bc9-9b10-172356be0eb4" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying an Orthogonal Fundamental Subspaces of a Matrix"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "09b69c48-96d4-5148-9d23-458c6d5a1c1e" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id 17592186362719} {:answer/id #uuid "87c311c4-ea4d-5db0-b386-be941d6c05b0" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id 17592186362720} {:answer/id #uuid "b8ed516f-6983-54ab-b9b1-1326006eaed0" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id 17592186362721} {:answer/id #uuid "5ac3b5cc-540d-5a99-9e0f-4c7bfca468b1" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id 17592186362722} {:answer/id #uuid "13649877-0942-5032-9fb2-4c1bcd640b3d" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id 17592186362723}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "b8ed516f-6983-54ab-b9b1-1326006eaed0" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id 17592186362721} :answer-field/id #uuid "66be61d8-973a-58fe-b28b-59ce4ae34aa6" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id 17592186362718}] :question/difficulty {:db/id 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "90aa20cc-7bd4-5b9c-96fe-e3f55743e36c" :question/math-academy-id "q-109781" :question/problem "Which of the following subspaces must be orthogonal to $\\text{Col}({B}^{T})$ for every matrix $B?$\n\n- $\\text{Col}(B)$\n- $\\text{Null}({B}^{T})$\n- $\\text{Null}(B)$" :question/worked-solution "Recall that $\\text{Col}({B}^{T})$ is the row space of $B.$ This consists of all linear combinations of the rows of $B.$\n\nIf a vector is orthogonal to every linear combination of the rows of $B,$ it must be orthogonal to each row of $B.$\nBut notice that when pre-multiplying a vector by $B,$ we actually multiply each row of $B$ by that vector.\nSo, $\\text{Col}({B}^{T})$ must be orthogonal to the null space of $B,$ which is $\\text{Null}(B).$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"III only.\"\n\n**Note:** In general, the four fundamental subspaces of a matrix $A$ can be schematically visualized, as shown below.\n\n![](images/18/18713ed3d103ca0a02d74cede1e6448012e0aa28a2df804d41cfb277589a0158.png)"}] [{:db/id 17592186362757 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id 17592186133885 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "5ada5d20-89d8-5bc9-9b10-172356be0eb4" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying an Orthogonal Fundamental Subspaces of a Matrix"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "15ee909a-2213-5908-aab7-710d877e6fa8" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id 17592186362759} {:answer/id #uuid "96ddce82-4639-57d8-8962-e06b2b5e382d" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id 17592186362760} {:answer/id #uuid "a3fb9876-21aa-52b8-b681-8d87695f06f3" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id 17592186362761} {:answer/id #uuid "3d071a53-691d-52f1-b584-49005877a293" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id 17592186362762} {:answer/id #uuid "d35126b9-8b88-5dc5-b360-043c9494a374" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id 17592186362763}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "96ddce82-4639-57d8-8962-e06b2b5e382d" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id 17592186362760} :answer-field/id #uuid "db714c4b-ae52-586c-bdd6-a74df8706792" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id 17592186362758}] :question/difficulty {:db/id 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "4c3520bf-5736-51de-a6b7-f6ed37ab8f1a" :question/math-academy-id "q-109852" :question/problem "Which of the following subspaces must be orthogonal to $\\text{Col}(B)$ for every matrix $B?$\n\n- $\\text{Null}({B}^{T})$\n- $\\text{Col}({B}^{T})$\n- $\\text{Null}(B)$" :question/worked-solution "Recall that $\\text{Col}(B)$ consists of all linear combinations of the columns of $B$, that is, the rows of ${B}^{T}.$\n\nIf a vector is orthogonal to every linear combination of the rows of ${B}^{T},$ it must be orthogonal to each row of ${B}^{T}.$\nBut notice that when pre-multiplying a vector by ${B}^{T},$ we actually multiply each row of ${B}^{T}$ by that vector.\nSo, $\\text{Col}(B)$ must be orthogonal to the left null space of $B,$ which is $\\text{Null}({B}^{T}).$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only.\"\n\n**Note:** In general, the four fundamental subspaces of a matrix $B$ can be schematically visualized, as shown below.\n\n![](images/18/18713ed3d103ca0a02d74cede1e6448012e0aa28a2df804d41cfb277589a0158.png)"}] [{:db/id 17592186362921 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id 17592186166859 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "fba5f68e-15da-5804-b6d9-d72ce6acae59" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying an Equation That Generates a Slope Field by Identifying Asymptotic Behavior"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "8df671d2-4023-5ffb-978c-af5e652520d9" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id 17592186362923} {:answer/id #uuid "4ce9e6cf-20b2-5801-af21-b21093705e6e" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id 17592186362924} {:answer/id #uuid "8c6b3ad8-6dcd-51f6-84be-d6f2634bb5d7" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id 17592186362925} {:answer/id #uuid "d456ec5c-a33c-5318-aafd-9d83a91bd984" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id 17592186362926} {:answer/id #uuid "7d7b6129-0e59-52a8-b7c6-c3d6dc1780a3" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id 17592186362927}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "d456ec5c-a33c-5318-aafd-9d83a91bd984" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id 17592186362926} :answer-field/id #uuid "4dbe725d-2cb3-596a-950a-580b1e6c3920" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id 17592186362922}] :question/difficulty {:db/id 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "81af22bb-892f-5bfc-b62b-3a9ae21c3aa9" :question/math-academy-id "q-116029" :question/problem "![](images/2b/2b25629c102be82d96b7ca94d5ab5ce499d327837ed53c6b4adb69ee214db81d.png)\n\nWhich of the following equations could have generated the slope field shown above?\n\n- ${y}^{′}=\\frac{{y}^{2}}{{y}^{2}-1}$\n- ${y}^{′}=-\\frac{1}{y-1}$\n- ${y}^{′}=\\frac{1}{{y}^{2}-1}$" :question/worked-solution "![](images/1b/1bf59ce83d7a82899587095217c080a5942f28a9c36c6971ec07ae5e8988e7fc.png)\n\n \n\nNotice the following properties of the given slope field:\n\n- The slopes of the slope field become vertical as $y\\to \\pm 1.$\n\n- The slopes of the slope field become horizontal as $y\\to \\pm \\infty .$\n\n- The slopes of the slope field are positive for $y\\in (-\\infty ,-1)\\cup (1,\\infty )$ and negative for $y\\in (-1,1).$\n\nWith that in mind, let's consider each equation.\n\n- The equation ${y}^{′}=\\frac{{y}^{2}}{{y}^{2}-1}$ does *not* give the required behavior. Notice that\n\n$$\n\\frac{{y}^{2}}{{y}^{2}-1}=\\frac{1}{(1-\\frac{1}{y})}\\to 1\\,\\text{as}\\,y\\to \\pm \\infty .\n$$\n\nTherefore, the slopes of the slope field for this differential equation do not become horizontal as $y\\to \\pm \\infty .$\n\n- The equation ${y}^{′}=\\frac{1}{y-1}$ does *not* give the required behavior. The slopes of the slope field for this differential equation do not become vertical as $y\\to -1.$\n\n- The equation ${y}^{′}=\\frac{1}{{y}^{2}-1}$ gives the required behavior. Notice that ${y}^{′}\\to 0$ as $y\\to \\pm \\infty ,$ and ${y}^{′}\\to \\infty $ as $y\\to \\pm 1.$ Also, the right-hand side of this equation is positive for $y\\in (-\\infty ,-1)\\cup (1,\\infty )$ and negative for $y\\in (-1,1).$"}] [{:db/id 17592186362976 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id 17592186166859 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "fba5f68e-15da-5804-b6d9-d72ce6acae59" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying an Equation That Generates a Slope Field by Identifying Asymptotic Behavior"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "5fb3ca87-7a26-5c72-bfd1-a3fd86c495c9" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id 17592186362978} {:answer/id #uuid "a3040ae7-fae0-5e85-934e-b4cff5adf52a" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id 17592186362979} {:answer/id #uuid "98551df9-5584-5920-8feb-90b89f576b94" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id 17592186362980} {:answer/id #uuid "7a62cbe7-6315-58e4-9fc5-d1c4b026eef9" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id 17592186362981} {:answer/id #uuid "2be11fb0-e5ce-52b1-99d9-f4ca4b7a5acd" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id 17592186362982}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "a3040ae7-fae0-5e85-934e-b4cff5adf52a" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id 17592186362979} :answer-field/id #uuid "f695614b-55be-556d-b550-3dc61b67861a" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id 17592186362977}] :question/difficulty {:db/id 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "1bc4fffb-9fca-5d90-ae58-a105216385f3" :question/math-academy-id "q-120187" :question/problem "![](images/da/da8d6b73685dbd2db82ad6bdaa528f316fb7819ba3f99f226f0ad9127d3a5609.png)\n\nWhich of the following equations could have generated the slope field shown above?\n\n- ${y}^{′}=-\\frac{1}{y}$\n- ${y}^{′}=\\frac{2}{{y}^{2}}$\n- ${y}^{′}=\\frac{1}{y+1}$" :question/worked-solution "![](images/9b/9b6c8c113f59cc006f26e336b85abde6337a3af2a9f5d6dccc001eee636991ac.png)\n\nNotice the following properties of the given slope field:\n\n- The slopes of the slope field become vertical as $y\\to 0.$\n\n- The slopes of the slope field become horizontal as $y\\to \\pm \\infty .$\n\n- The slopes of the slope field are positive for $y<0$ and negative for $y>0.$\n\nWith that in mind, let's consider each equation.\n\n- The equation ${y}^{′}=-\\frac{1}{y}$ gives the required behavior. Notice that ${y}^{′}\\to 0$ as $y\\to \\pm \\infty ,$ and ${y}^{′}\\to \\infty $ as $y\\to 0.$ Also, the right-hand side of this equation is positive for $y<0$ and negative for $y>0.$\n\n- The equation ${y}^{′}=\\frac{2}{{y}^{2}}$ does *not* give the required behavior. The slopes of the slope field for this differential equation are positive for $y>0.$\n\n- The equation ${y}^{′}=\\frac{1}{y+1}$ does *not* give the required behavior. Notice that\n\n$$\n\\frac{y}{y+1}\\to 0\\,\\text{as}\\,y\\to 0.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the slopes of the slope field for this differential equation do not become vertical as $y\\to 0.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only.\""}] [{:db/id 17592186363405 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id 17592186105861 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "16a6f0f7-4003-5908-8b1b-68d218fdcb8f" :knowledge-point/title "Determining Whether a Function Is a Valid PDF"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "48472de6-866a-5dcd-a9f6-02aa1ff0cdd3" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "None of the statements are true" :db/id 17592186363407} {:answer/id #uuid "04d2d6ac-d98c-5684-8ea1-3861da991c56" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, II, and III" :db/id 17592186363408} {:answer/id #uuid "62323ad1-50a6-5ba0-a1c5-61054e5c619e" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id 17592186363409} {:answer/id #uuid "afb7d291-f5c3-524d-85de-5281265ce3a8" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id 17592186363410} {:answer/id #uuid "5173d7df-b3a7-583d-9b15-3abc62346e76" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id 17592186363411}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "04d2d6ac-d98c-5684-8ea1-3861da991c56" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, II, and III" :db/id 17592186363408} :answer-field/id #uuid "fb7abbf5-d5b5-5cf3-9ed4-ede86c5ffb43" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id 17592186363406}] :question/difficulty {:db/id 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "ef54c03e-38cc-58aa-92b0-b3a91ad36505" :question/math-academy-id "q-158422" :question/problem "![](images/8f/8f955ee719a4e69f0d09c678f0a74023f137efd7b5ae36d49b98706156384173.png)\n\nConsider the function $f(x)$ whose graph is shown above.\n\n$$\nf(x)={\\begin{aligned}x+\\frac{1}{2},\\, & 0<x<1, \\\\ 0,\\, & \\text{otherwise}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWhich of the following statements are true?\n\n- $f(x)\\ge 0$ for all $x$\n- ${\\int }_{0}^{1}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x=1$\n- $f(x)$ is a valid probability density function" :question/worked-solution "For a function $f(x)$ to be a valid probability density function on a set $S,$ it must satisfy the following conditions:\n\n- $f(x)\\ge 0$ for all $x\\in S$\n\n- ${\\int }_{S}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x=1$\n\nNow, let's consider each statement in turn:\n\n- Statement I is true. From the graph, we see that $f(x)$ is nonnegative for all $x.$\n\n- Statement II is true. Indeed,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{1}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}(x+\\frac{1}{2})\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={[\\frac{{x}^{2}}{2}+\\frac{x}{2}]}_{0}^{1} \\\\  & =[\\frac{{1}^{2}}{2}+\\frac{1}{2}]-[\\frac{{0}^{2}}{2}+\\frac{0}{2}] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}+\\frac{1}{2}-0 \\\\  & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement III is true. Since statements I and II are true, $f(x)$ is a valid probability density function.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I, II, and III.\""}] [{:db/id 17592186363619 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id 17592186129287 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "b4ade459-9aba-5520-95d3-69ef3fd62d9d" :knowledge-point/title "Classifying Recurrence Relations"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "9a52b768-c3eb-5bde-97e5-a532bb77dd1e" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id 17592186363621} {:answer/id #uuid "aea978a8-7a1f-5511-b2ce-7a327be58b49" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id 17592186363622} {:answer/id #uuid "e1d62003-b1b3-5199-ab7b-bd7885ae44f6" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id 17592186363623} {:answer/id #uuid "2089dcc9-62c8-5743-b2e9-f8f2a0245131" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id 17592186363624} {:answer/id #uuid "a9356a0a-ab16-525d-843d-77e76c4b9e31" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id 17592186363625}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "aea978a8-7a1f-5511-b2ce-7a327be58b49" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id 17592186363622} :answer-field/id #uuid "3f8a348d-8f04-54bc-82e3-cb2e42565377" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id 17592186363620}] :question/difficulty {:db/id 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "875338da-f85c-5b2a-9645-5f7fb1d7affd" :question/math-academy-id "q-253466" :question/problem "Which of the following recurrence relations are first-order?\n\n- ${a}_{n}=4{a}_{n-1}+7$\n- ${b}_{n}={b}_{n-1}+3{b}_{n-3}$\n- ${c}_{n}=5{c}_{n-1}-2n$" :question/worked-solution "The order of a recurrence relation is the largest index difference between ${a}_{n}$ and the preceding terms in the relation.\n\nLet's examine each recurrence relation in turn.\n\n- The largest index difference between ${a}_{n}$ and the preceding term ${a}_{n-1}$ in the relation is $1.$ So, this recurrence relation has order $1.$\n\n- The largest index difference between ${b}_{n}$ and the preceding terms ${b}_{n-1}$ and ${b}_{n-3}$ in the relation is $3.$ So, this recurrence relation has order $3.$\n\n- The largest index difference between ${c}_{n}$ and the preceding term ${c}_{n-1}$ in the relation is $1.$ So, this recurrence relation has order $1.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and III only.\""}] [{:db/id 17592186363626 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id 17592186129287 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "b4ade459-9aba-5520-95d3-69ef3fd62d9d" :knowledge-point/title "Classifying Recurrence Relations"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "00c37177-1257-5361-8a1c-1fc4165b7598" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id 17592186363628} {:answer/id #uuid "70827be5-0f7a-5a2a-a291-73022cfe7931" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id 17592186363629} {:answer/id #uuid "d041ba80-d063-556b-a0c8-4703b661b3bf" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id 17592186363630} {:answer/id #uuid "e7462c0d-a120-5966-9bcb-c93c95bb2e8b" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id 17592186363631} {:answer/id #uuid "eb50e688-1d97-5697-8b97-e6a866a40cc7" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id 17592186363632}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "eb50e688-1d97-5697-8b97-e6a866a40cc7" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id 17592186363632} :answer-field/id #uuid "fb00d54e-bf9e-5084-92c5-709b87a491e9" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id 17592186363627}] :question/difficulty {:db/id 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "18b3d391-1187-5c01-902c-37defcaf71f5" :question/math-academy-id "q-253483" :question/problem "Which of the following recurrence relations are linear (with constant coefficients)?\n\n- ${a}_{n}=-n{a}_{n-1}+4{a}_{n-2}$\n- ${b}_{n}=5{b}_{n-1}-2n$\n- ${c}_{n}={c}_{n-1}^{2}-3{c}_{n-2}$" :question/worked-solution "A recurrence relation is **linear (with constant coefficients)** if it expresses ${a}_{n}$ as a sum of previous terms ${a}_{n-1},…,{a}_{n-k}$ with constant coefficients, together with possibly a final term that depends only on $n:$\n\n$$\n{a}_{n}={s}_{1}{a}_{n-1}+{s}_{2}{a}_{n-2}+…+{s}_{k}{a}_{n-k}+g(n)\n$$\n\nwhere ${s}_{1},{s}_{2},…,{s}_{k}$ are constants and $g(n)$ is a function, often called a **forcing function**, that does not involve ${a}_{n}$ or its preceding terms. \n\nLet's examine each recurrence relation in turn.\n\n- ${a}_{n}$ is not linear. The coefficient of the term $-n{a}_{n-1}$ is not constant.\n\n- ${b}_{n}$ is linear. The term ${b}_{n-1}$ occurs only in the first power with constant coefficient.\n\n- ${c}_{n}$ is not linear. The term ${c}_{n-1}^{2}$ is not in the first power.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II only\"."}] [{:db/id 17592186363633 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id 17592186129287 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "b4ade459-9aba-5520-95d3-69ef3fd62d9d" :knowledge-point/title "Classifying Recurrence Relations"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "eb32dc55-377d-5645-83e9-876711297904" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id 17592186363635} {:answer/id #uuid "91bbf08d-b47b-5e5d-a78e-e5211955f623" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id 17592186363636} {:answer/id #uuid "d744fc50-e35c-5913-9fc1-a1796b3ba201" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id 17592186363637} {:answer/id #uuid "da1806d2-0921-560c-81ba-ef6862fc87c8" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id 17592186363638} {:answer/id #uuid "e97764b9-b518-5d91-aa47-a19a56d29e8a" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id 17592186363639}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "e97764b9-b518-5d91-aa47-a19a56d29e8a" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id 17592186363639} :answer-field/id #uuid "229ea531-5a5f-59ff-ab5e-ac3edbc0063c" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id 17592186363634}] :question/difficulty {:db/id 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "c27291fe-59f1-5d75-bef8-47b51d3e493f" :question/math-academy-id "q-253486" :question/problem "Which of the following linear recurrence relations are homogeneous?\n\n- ${a}_{n}={a}_{n-1}-3{a}_{n-2}+n$\n- ${b}_{n}={b}_{n-1}+5{b}_{n-2}$\n- ${c}_{n}=6{c}_{n-1}+1$" :question/worked-solution "A linear recurrence relation (with constant coefficients) is **homogeneous** if the forcing function is zero. \n\nIn other words, the linear recurrence relation\n\n$$\n{a}_{n}={s}_{1}{a}_{n-1}+{s}_{2}{a}_{n-2}+…+{s}_{k}{a}_{n-k}+g(n),\n$$\n\nis homogeneous if $g(n)=0$ for all $n,$ and ${s}_{i}$ is constant for each $i.$\n\nLet's examine each recurrence relation in turn.\n\n- ${a}_{n}$ is not homogeneous. The term $n$ of the expression doesn't contain preceding terms of the relation.\n\n- ${b}_{n}$ is homogeneous. The terms of the expression each contain a preceding term, ${b}_{n-1}$ and ${b}_{n-2}.$\n\n- ${c}_{n}$ is not homogeneous. The term $1$ of the expression doesn't contain preceding terms of the relation.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II only.\""}] [{:db/id 17592186363640 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id 17592186129287 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "b4ade459-9aba-5520-95d3-69ef3fd62d9d" :knowledge-point/title "Classifying Recurrence Relations"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "80fd47be-8132-50cb-9d8b-08c4d4394cfc" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id 17592186363642} {:answer/id #uuid "735c82e6-45c4-5706-9f50-1ab3bc928262" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id 17592186363643} {:answer/id #uuid "0e6160e3-97b0-56a4-9692-ae2dafd6d449" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id 17592186363644} {:answer/id #uuid "2beb2a54-da9a-5ab3-8b36-4c8001f183ad" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id 17592186363645} {:answer/id #uuid "18443de6-d3d5-50a5-89ca-48ad2614670f" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id 17592186363646}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "80fd47be-8132-50cb-9d8b-08c4d4394cfc" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id 17592186363642} :answer-field/id #uuid "a0885c86-fd77-5621-bbd1-06d43262486d" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id 17592186363641}] :question/difficulty {:db/id 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "8e46c1db-beb9-52c4-8f72-f2f2edfce45c" :question/math-academy-id "q-253487" :question/problem "Which of the following linear recurrence relations are homogeneous?\n\n- ${a}_{n}=\\frac{2}{3}{a}_{n-1}-\\frac{1}{2}{a}_{n-2}+2$\n- ${b}_{n}=5{b}_{n-1}-{b}_{n-2}$\n- ${c}_{n}=\\frac{1}{4}{c}_{n-1}$" :question/worked-solution "A linear recurrence relation (with constant coefficients) is **homogeneous** if the forcing function is zero. \n\nIn other words, the linear recurrence relation\n\n$$\n{a}_{n}={s}_{1}{a}_{n-1}+{s}_{2}{a}_{n-2}+…+{s}_{k}{a}_{n-k}+g(n),\n$$\n\nis homogeneous if $g(n)=0$ for all $n,$ and ${s}_{i}$ is constant for each $i.$\n\nLet's examine each recurrence relation in turn.\n\n- ${a}_{n}$ is not homogeneous. The term $2$ of the expression doesn't contain preceding terms of the relation.\n\n- ${b}_{n}$ is homogeneous. The terms of the expression each contain a preceding term, ${b}_{n-1}$ and ${b}_{n-2}.$\n\n- ${c}_{n}$ is homogeneous. The term of the expression contains a preceding term, ${c}_{n-1}.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II and III only.\""}] [{:db/id 17592186364040 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id 17592186129316 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "28939f65-9fd6-564d-a0c2-73c1038ac898" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Homogeneous and Inhomogeneous Systems"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "a783a885-59c4-5045-beb2-e5658ca14227" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id 17592186364042} {:answer/id #uuid "88e9116b-0b9b-5e63-b54e-568a9df1977a" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id 17592186364043} {:answer/id #uuid "8163399c-99cf-5a72-8428-0db4c63be287" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id 17592186364044} {:answer/id #uuid "1cf9b4a4-21cc-50f3-8c85-1ba421aa6c0b" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id 17592186364045} {:answer/id #uuid "6521e768-5e2c-5726-bfee-f504aa6755e7" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "None" :db/id 17592186364046}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "1cf9b4a4-21cc-50f3-8c85-1ba421aa6c0b" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id 17592186364045} :answer-field/id #uuid "034a22cb-0966-55b2-9f99-48d3fa6d6fe8" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id 17592186364041}] :question/difficulty {:db/id 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "b1ece1ec-19c9-5790-a759-998564647939" :question/math-academy-id "q-339659" :question/problem "Which of the following systems are homogeneous?\n\n- ${x}^{′}(t)=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 \\\\ 3 & 4\\end{aligned}]x(t)+[\\begin{aligned}{e}^{t} \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]$\n- ${x}^{′}(t)=[\\begin{aligned}2 & 3 \\\\ -1 & 1\\end{aligned}]x(t)+[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ t\\end{aligned}]$\n- ${x}^{′}(t)=[\\begin{aligned}0 & -6 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]x(t)$" :question/worked-solution "In matrix form, the system\n\n$$\n{x}^{′}(t)=A(t)x(t)+f(t)\n$$\n\nis *homogeneous* when $f≡0,$ and *inhomogeneous* when $f≢0.$\n\nWith this in mind, let's inspect each system:\n\n- System I is inhomogeneous since $f=[\\begin{aligned}{e}^{t} \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]≢0.$\n\n- System II is inhomogeneous since $f=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ t\\end{aligned}]≢0.$\n\n- System III is homogeneous since $f≡0.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"III only\"."}] [{:db/id 17592186364047 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id 17592186129316 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "28939f65-9fd6-564d-a0c2-73c1038ac898" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Homogeneous and Inhomogeneous Systems"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "64ae9f36-2ae5-5b74-995c-1a03435d7d84" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id 17592186364049} {:answer/id #uuid "c5f1679e-4ba9-5cd2-891c-6921b7d46b96" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id 17592186364050} {:answer/id #uuid "95cdb787-8d2a-5203-b629-98f7c0091c2d" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id 17592186364051} {:answer/id #uuid "9d213c59-8c57-5a0a-9578-64e2c4240cb9" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "None" :db/id 17592186364052} {:answer/id #uuid "861bd730-7fe3-5b41-a5ab-caaeb8427723" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id 17592186364053}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "c5f1679e-4ba9-5cd2-891c-6921b7d46b96" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id 17592186364050} :answer-field/id #uuid "c3900507-4225-54f1-a763-617ded408cb8" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id 17592186364048}] :question/difficulty {:db/id 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "73f3b368-2531-52b2-833e-f683b0622933" :question/math-academy-id "q-339665" :question/problem "Which of the following systems are homogeneous?\n\n- ${\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}^{′}(t)=3{x}_{1}(t)+2{x}_{2}(t) \\\\ {x}_{2}^{′}(t)=-{x}_{1}(t)+4{x}_{2}(t)\\end{aligned}$\n- ${\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}^{′}(t)=-{x}_{1}(t)+{x}_{2}(t) \\\\ {x}_{2}^{′}(t)=-2{x}_{1}(t)+5\\end{aligned}$\n- ${\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}^{′}(t)=0 \\\\ {x}_{2}^{′}(t)=7{x}_{1}(t)+3{x}_{2}(t)\\end{aligned}$" :question/worked-solution "Recall that a first-order linear system of differential equations with two dependent variables is a system of the form\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}^{′}(t)=a(t){x}_{1}(t)+b(t){x}_{2}(t)+f(t) \\\\ {x}_{2}^{′}(t)=c(t){x}_{1}(t)+d(t){x}_{2}(t)+g(t).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIf $f≡0$ and $g≡0$ (i.e., both $f$ and $g$ are zero functions), then the system is *homogeneous*. Otherwise, if at least one of $f$ or $g$ is not identically zero, the system is *inhomogeneous*.\n\nWith this in mind, let's inspect each system:\n\n- System I is homogeneous since it can be written in the standard form with $f≡0$ and $g≡0:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}a(t)=3, &  & b(t)=2, &  & f(t)=0 \\\\ c(t)=-1, &  & d(t)=4, &  & g(t)=0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- System II is inhomogeneous since it can be written in the standard form with $g≢0:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}a(t)=-1, &  & b(t)=1, &  & f(t)=0 \\\\ c(t)=-2, &  & d(t)=0, &  & g(t)=5\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- System III is homogeneous since it can be written in the standard form with $f≡0$ and $g≡0:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}a(t)=0, &  & b(t)=0, &  & f(t)=0 \\\\ c(t)=7, &  & d(t)=3, &  & g(t)=0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and III only\"."}] [{:db/id 17592186364145 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id 17592186124631 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "113a601e-220a-54a3-a64b-1e554bc3d79f" :knowledge-point/title "Сompleting a Sentence Using the Invertible Matrix Theorem"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "b5f07bf4-4a80-5c38-8806-6cbd8d26fa57" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id 17592186364147} {:answer/id #uuid "862b97ed-b399-5806-9f72-3a71bd6e9f7f" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id 17592186364148} {:answer/id #uuid "557f525c-a15e-58a6-9656-27d5203e43a4" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id 17592186364149} {:answer/id #uuid "a79d8796-f65e-5d31-bb2f-29124bc7fccf" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id 17592186364150} {:answer/id #uuid "09b175a4-227d-55cd-a19d-33c07569f7d6" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id 17592186364151}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "a79d8796-f65e-5d31-bb2f-29124bc7fccf" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id 17592186364150} :answer-field/id #uuid "ea5cc0e6-6d5a-59ee-82ed-ff12bda5ecf8" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id 17592186364146}] :question/difficulty {:db/id 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "e3fcd83b-c9bf-54f3-97b2-1811800f0c15" :question/math-academy-id "q-38889" :question/problem "Let $T:{R}^{7}\\to {R}^{7}$ be a linear transformation whose standard matrix is $T.$ Consider the following statement:\n\n*If ${v}_{1}\\ne {v}_{2}$ vectors such that $T({v}_{1})=T({v}_{2})$, then $\\text{__________}.$*\n\nWhich of the following can be placed into the blank space to make a true statement?\n\nI. $\\text{Ker}(T)=\\{0\\}$\n\nII. $T$ is invertible\n\nIII. $\\text{nullity}(T)>0$" :question/worked-solution "Since $T$ is a linear transformation from ${R}^{7}$ into ${R}^{7},$ the invertible matrix theorem states the following:\n\n $T$ is a bijection (an invertible transformation) $\\,⟺\\,\\{\\,\\begin{aligned} & \\text{Im}(T)={R}^{7} \\\\  & \\text{rank}(T)=7 \\\\  & \\text{Ker}(T)=\\{0\\} \\\\  & \\text{nullity}(T)=0\\end{aligned}$\n\nSince $T$ is a linear transformation, we have that\n\n$$\nT({v}_{1})=T({v}_{2})\\,⟹\\,T({v}_{1}-{v}_{2})=0.\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each statement in turn.\n\n- Statement I is false. Since ${v}_{1}-{v}_{2}\\in \\text{Ker}(T)$ and ${v}_{1}\\ne {v}_{2},$ we must have that $\\text{Ker}(T)\\ne \\{0\\}.$\n\n- Statement II is false. According to the invertible matrix theorem, since $\\text{Ker}(T)\\ne \\{0\\},$ the transformation $T$ and the matrix $T$ are not invertible.\n\n- Statement III is true. Since $T$ is not invertible, the invertible matrix theorem implies that $\\text{nullity}(T)\\ne 0.$ In turn, this means that $\\text{nullity}(T)>0.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"III only.\""}] [{:db/id 17592186364213 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id 17592186124631 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "113a601e-220a-54a3-a64b-1e554bc3d79f" :knowledge-point/title "Сompleting a Sentence Using the Invertible Matrix Theorem"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "c52cc7b6-9bfa-5536-9e5f-59eeb0900797" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id 17592186364215} {:answer/id #uuid "b4ecdf86-13ef-5e0a-b4f9-6f8533300313" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id 17592186364216} {:answer/id #uuid "9ed48d52-d008-59d3-a693-7857aac6b5e6" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id 17592186364217} {:answer/id #uuid "512e4185-0fa5-516c-8c01-749de4125a44" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id 17592186364218} {:answer/id #uuid "a2ba01e1-6325-586c-aa39-fa54eae6601b" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id 17592186364219}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "9ed48d52-d008-59d3-a693-7857aac6b5e6" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id 17592186364217} :answer-field/id #uuid "ad2b2b69-4210-5e1c-81b8-a09e94838b9f" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id 17592186364214}] :question/difficulty {:db/id 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "3c1b6231-ddc6-5ac3-a5c8-9d11b32158e5" :question/math-academy-id "q-47456" :question/problem "Let $T:{R}^{3}\\to {R}^{3}$ be a linear transformation whose standard matrix is $T,$ and ${{v}_{1},{v}_{2},{v}_{3}}$ is a basis of $\\text{Col}(T).$ Consider the following statement:\n\n*If ${v}_{1}-2{v}_{2}+{v}_{3}=0$, then $\\text{__________}.$*\n\nWhich of the following can be placed into the blank space to make a true statement?\n\n- $\\text{nullity}(T)\\ne 0$\n- $\\text{rank}(T)=3$\n- $T$ is a bijection" :question/worked-solution "Since $T$ is a linear transformation from ${R}^{3}$ into ${R}^{3},$ the invertible matrix theorem states the following:\n\n $T$ is a bijection (an invertible transformation) $\\,⟺\\,{\\,\\begin{aligned} & \\text{Im}(T)={R}^{3} \\\\  & \\text{rank}(T)=3 \\\\  & \\text{Ker}(T)={0} \\\\  & \\text{nullity}(T)=0\\end{aligned}$ \n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each statement in turn.\n\n- Statement I is true. Since the columns of $T$ satisfies ${v}_{1}-2{v}_{2}+{v}_{3}=0$, then they are linearly dependent, which gives us $\\text{Im}(T)=\\text{Col}(T)\\ne {R}^{3}.$ Therefore, according to the Invertible Matrix Theorem, we must have $\\text{nullity}(T)\\ne 0.$\n\n- Statement II is false. Since $\\text{Im}(T)\\ne {R}^{3}$, then $\\text{rank}(T)=\\text{rank}(T)\\ne 3.$\n\n- Statement III is false. According to the invertible matrix theorem, since $\\text{Im}(T)\\ne {R}^{3},$ the transformation $T$ can't be a bijection.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only.\""}] [{:db/id 17592186364723 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id 17592186152220 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "aee17e3a-031d-5c0e-9488-17410b3a2372" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying True Statements About Dimensions of Vector Spaces"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1e105f0b-e1ce-57ea-93e5-f9c2602d9ed1" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id 17592186364725} {:answer/id #uuid "db73441a-015d-5b78-9270-03e1141027fd" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id 17592186364726} {:answer/id #uuid "7795e9b1-b217-5dc6-b733-6518146b83fb" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id 17592186364727} {:answer/id #uuid "a00c46a1-8ee4-5ff8-a095-c8d32431591c" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id 17592186364728} {:answer/id #uuid "cbd9c344-617e-577a-869e-ac013d60022d" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id 17592186364729}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "db73441a-015d-5b78-9270-03e1141027fd" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id 17592186364726} :answer-field/id #uuid "deab58b3-dd37-5659-a248-9f3e15b35602" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id 17592186364724}] :question/difficulty {:db/id 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "6a2340ea-4d69-565f-a0a6-1a2327573430" :question/math-academy-id "q-106904" :question/problem "Which of the following vector spaces has a dimension of $11?$\n\n- ${R}_{10}[t]$, the vector space of all polynomials in the variable $t$ of degree at most $10$\n- ${\\text{M}}_{2\\times 6}(R),$ the vector space of all $2\\times 6$ matrices over $R$\n- ${R}^{10}$" :question/worked-solution "Let's examine each of the vector spaces in turn.\n\n- Recall that $\\text{dim}({R}_{n}[t])=n+1.$ Therefore, \n\n$$\n\\text{dim}({R}_{10}[t])=10+1=11.\\,✓\n$$\n- Recall that $\\text{dim}({\\text{M}}_{n\\times m}(R))=n\\cdot m.$ Therefore, \n\n$$\n\\text{dim}({\\text{M}}_{2\\times 6}(R))=2\\cdot 6=12\\ne 11.\\,\\times \n$$\n- Recall that $\\text{dim}({R}^{n})=n.$ Therefore, \n\n$$\n\\text{dim}({R}^{10})=10\\ne 11.\\,\\times \n$$\n\nHence, the correct answer is \"I only.\""}] [{:db/id 17592186364730 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id 17592186152221 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "e609aabf-7e7e-5997-b8dc-c66e7a14a36f" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Finite and Infinite-Dimensional Vector Spaces"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "60dd5158-d283-5453-8d5f-8930bf63cd91" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id 17592186364732} {:answer/id #uuid "b5a04cf5-0c3a-5245-8c4b-860f3ac9416c" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id 17592186364733} {:answer/id #uuid "00babb9c-00d3-5963-90bb-09bcb833a581" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id 17592186364734} {:answer/id #uuid "0a1d2efc-04c3-5a01-842a-a45dbaa561a4" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id 17592186364735} {:answer/id #uuid "a4ab6ab6-a954-5fc9-9cb2-6c09f0a46016" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id 17592186364736}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "60dd5158-d283-5453-8d5f-8930bf63cd91" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id 17592186364732} :answer-field/id #uuid "6a8e9c59-4db2-5da4-aa1b-9e9025a98565" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id 17592186364731}] :question/difficulty {:db/id 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "2ad8a280-94c8-5978-909a-9e22277ca8ba" :question/math-academy-id "q-106918" :question/problem "Which of the following vector spaces are finite-dimensional?\n\n- $D(R),$ the vector space of all differentiable functions on $R$\n- ${R}^{100}$\n- ${R}_{2000}[t],$ the vector space of all polynomials in the variable $t$ of degree at most $2\\,000$" :question/worked-solution "Let's examine our vector spaces.\n\n- $D(R)$ is *not* finite-dimensional. To understand why, we first note that the following subset of $D(R)$ is linearly independent:\n\n$$\n{1,\\,t,\\,{t}^{2},\\,…,{t}^{n}},\\,n\\in N.\n$$\n\nIndeed, we have that \n\n$$\n{\\sum }_{i=0}^{n}{x}_{i}{t}^{i}=0\n$$\n\nfor all $t\\in R$ if and only if ${x}_{i}=0$ for all $i=0,1,2,…,n.$ Therefore,  for any  $n\\in N,$  there exists a linearly independent subset of $D(R)$  of size $n,$ which imples that $D(R)$ cannot have finite dimension.     .\n\n- ${R}^{100}$ and ${R}_{2000}[t]$ are finite-dimensional. Indeed, \n\n$$\n\\text{dim}({R}^{100})=100\n$$\n\n \nand\n\n$$\n\\text{dim}({R}_{2000}[t])=2000+1=2\\,001,\n$$\n\nare both finite numbers.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II and III only.\""}] [{:db/id 17592186365010 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id 17592186172029 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "7a6bbb34-a43f-5e16-8a24-25c0d6970a38" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying a Quadratic Curve Type From Its Reduced Equation: No Cross-Product Term"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "ebbc61b3-da42-52b4-8b9a-1c8bfc6e51d4" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, II, and III" :db/id 17592186365012} {:answer/id #uuid "e57b11cb-0a00-5658-98f3-d87f6a7775f5" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id 17592186365013} {:answer/id #uuid "259d1b4b-cb8c-5458-acca-8cb674f94596" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id 17592186365014} {:answer/id #uuid "e7d2ca84-53fb-576d-85e4-ed21ca559e67" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id 17592186365015} {:answer/id #uuid "088b9f24-a8df-521a-bfbe-dbaa748a06e6" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id 17592186365016}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "ebbc61b3-da42-52b4-8b9a-1c8bfc6e51d4" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, II, and III" :db/id 17592186365012} :answer-field/id #uuid "713649dd-841d-595d-ad2c-1d706d7c2e1a" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id 17592186365011}] :question/difficulty {:db/id 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "d7ec5e81-6115-5881-b6f7-16bb9226da5a" :question/math-academy-id "q-140554" :question/problem "Which of the following equations define a non-empty set of points in the ${x}_{1}{x}_{2}$-plane?\n\n- ${x}_{1}^{2}-{x}_{2}^{2}=-1$\n- ${x}_{1}^{2}+2{x}_{2}=0$\n- ${x}_{1}^{2}+{x}_{2}^{2}=0$" :question/worked-solution "Let's examine each of the equations in turn.\n\n- The equation ${x}_{1}^{2}-{x}_{2}^{2}=-1$ defines a non-empty set. It represents the hyperbola ${x}_{2}^{2}-{x}_{1}^{2}=1.$\n\n- The equation ${x}_{1}^{2}+2{x}_{2}=0$ defines a non-empty set. It represents a parabola that opens in the direction of the negative ${x}_{2}$-axis in the ${x}_{1}{x}_{2}$-plane.\n\n- The equation ${x}_{1}^{2}+{x}_{2}^{2}=0$ defines a non-empty set. It represents a single point $(0,0)$ in the ${x}_{1}{x}_{2}$-plane.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I, II, and III.\""}] [{:db/id 17592186365603 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id 17592186166896 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "276898e7-57a4-5ca6-8df7-99fd318a2702" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying a Decoupled System of Linear ODEs"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "9ecdc87a-c565-5163-94b6-cf505630846d" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id 17592186365605} {:answer/id #uuid "46c4174c-aa3e-55f8-92f7-9d297693180d" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id 17592186365606} {:answer/id #uuid "4b90e9b1-c987-5d5c-9dd6-5a83cecd1d2a" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id 17592186365607} {:answer/id #uuid "ec1a127d-dd8a-5db2-b7d1-b71ac9c14069" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id 17592186365608} {:answer/id #uuid "39f72397-ab04-549b-8b5d-7c41730005e0" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id 17592186365609}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "39f72397-ab04-549b-8b5d-7c41730005e0" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id 17592186365609} :answer-field/id #uuid "324e36a2-8799-5a66-9f08-fe3fa316e513" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id 17592186365604}] :question/difficulty {:db/id 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "b1bf4017-8b21-55a6-92c8-e49841f3879b" :question/math-academy-id "q-331092" :question/problem "Which of the following systems of linear ODEs are decoupled?\n\n- ${x}^{′}(t)=[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 3\\end{aligned}]x(t)$\n- ${x}^{′}(t)=[\\begin{aligned}6 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -2\\end{aligned}]x(t)$\n- ${x}^{′}(t)=[\\begin{aligned}-4 & -5 \\\\ 2 & 0\\end{aligned}]x(t)$" :question/worked-solution "A *decoupled system* consists of equations depending on only one variable, such as\n\n$$\n{x}_{i}^{′}(t)={\\lambda }_{i}{x}_{i}(t),\n$$\n\nwhere ${\\lambda }_{i}$ is a constant. This means that we can solve each equation independently.  \n\nIn matrix form, a linear system ${x}^{′}(t)=Ax(t)$ is decoupled if $A$ is a diagonal matrix.\n\nWith this in mind, let's examine our systems.\n\n- System II is decoupled since its corresponding matrix is diagonal.\n\n- Systems I and III are *not* decoupled because their matrices are not diagonal.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II only\"."}] [{:db/id 17592186366039 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id 17592186188273 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "e12609b7-286b-50a8-8373-a331d60ab904" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying True Statements Using the Rank-Nullity Theorem"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "39a93edd-ad31-531a-b11d-331e83744aa1" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "None of the statements are true" :db/id 17592186366041} {:answer/id #uuid "0a72ba19-8116-5007-a175-93b746866f6b" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id 17592186366042} {:answer/id #uuid "78e13641-9025-567e-838f-cff98c736bdf" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id 17592186366043} {:answer/id #uuid "aa8c94f6-289c-5031-af4e-977788f3dfef" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id 17592186366044} {:answer/id #uuid "8c70843d-666b-57fc-87c0-bf584dd95392" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id 17592186366045}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "8c70843d-666b-57fc-87c0-bf584dd95392" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id 17592186366045} :answer-field/id #uuid "1713e681-b157-5242-b361-ddd94b6260d0" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id 17592186366040}] :question/difficulty {:db/id 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "006b1b75-818e-5ba9-8f81-bc35ede44097" :question/math-academy-id "q-38141" :question/problem "Let $T:{R}^{3}\\to {R}^{3}$ be a linear transformation whose standard matrix is $T.$ Which of the following statements are true?\n\n- If $\\text{rank}(T)=2,$ then $\\text{Ker}(T)={0}$\n- If $[-2,0,0]^{T}$ is a solution of $T(x)=0$ then $\\text{nullity}(T)\\ne 0$\n- If $T(x)=[1,0,0]^{T}$ has no solution then $\\text{rank}(T)=3$" :question/worked-solution "Since $T$ is a linear transformation from ${R}^{3}$ into ${R}^{3},$ the invertible matrix theorem states the following:\n\n $T$ is a bijection (an invertible transformation) $\\,⟺\\,{\\,\\begin{aligned} & \\text{Im}(T)={R}^{3} \\\\  & \\text{rank}(T)=3 \\\\  & \\text{Ker}(T)={0} \\\\  & \\text{nullity}(T)=0\\end{aligned}$ \n\nAlso, according to the rank-nullity theorem, \n\n$$\n\\text{rank}(T)+\\text{nullity}(T)=3.\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each statement in turn.\n\n- Statement I is false. According to the rank-nullity theorem, we obtain \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{nullity}(T) & =3-\\text{rank}(T) \\\\  & =3-2 \\\\  & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $\\text{Ker}(T)\\ne {0}.$\n\n- Statement II is true. Since $[\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]$ is a solution of $Tx=0$, we have that $[\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]\\in \\text{Ker}(T)$ and therefore $\\text{nullity}(T)\\ne 0.$\n\n- Statement III is false. Since $T(x)=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]$ has no solution, we have that $\\text{Im}(T)\\ne {R}^{3}.$ Hence, $\\text{rank}(T)\\ne 3.$\n\nTherefore, the correct option is \"II only.\""}] [{:db/id 17592186366052 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id 17592186176089 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "ba9d2401-4e30-5327-b8eb-b4db7108dd0c" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying True Statements Regarding Linear Transformations in the Cartesian Plane"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0b627793-3225-5deb-92ee-2435f9cfe821" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id 17592186366054} {:answer/id #uuid "8041e009-04fa-554e-a103-43a41c4be622" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "None of the listed" :db/id 17592186366055} {:answer/id #uuid "57205720-729e-5df5-b191-b16752215689" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id 17592186366056} {:answer/id #uuid "a2d07d55-84fe-5299-bf65-f860acba1c1b" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "All of the listed" :db/id 17592186366057} {:answer/id #uuid "46b993d9-0c06-5c28-8e26-7ee0692c4652" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id 17592186366058}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "46b993d9-0c06-5c28-8e26-7ee0692c4652" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id 17592186366058} :answer-field/id #uuid "4adfa4bb-a01a-5ffe-bd19-be00b56b101d" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id 17592186366053}] :question/difficulty {:db/id 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "34581764-4087-5b06-9eff-4679fe703405" :question/math-academy-id "q-38300" :question/problem "If $T$ maps ${R}^{2}$ onto the line $y=-3x$ then, which of the following statements are true?\n\n- $⟨1,3⟩$ belongs to the range of $T$\n- $\\text{Ker}(T)\\ne {0}$\n- $\\text{Im}(T)={0}$" :question/worked-solution "Recall that when the image of a linear transformation $T$ in ${R}^{2}$ with the standard matrix $T$ is a one-dimensional line, then\n\n$$\n\\text{rank}(T)=\\text{rank}(T)=1\n$$\n\nand, according to the rank-nullity theorem, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{nullity}(T)=\\text{nullity}(T) & =2-\\text{rank}(T) \\\\  & =2-1 \\\\  & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our statements in turn. \n\n- Statement I is false. \nNotice that the point $(1,3)$ does not satisfy the equation of the line $y=-3x$ since $3\\ne -3\\cdot 1.$ Therefore, the vector $⟨1,3⟩\\notin \\text{Im}(T).$\n\n- Statement II is true. Indeed, \nsince $T$ maps ${R}^{2}$ onto a line passing through the origin, we have that the standard matrix $T$ is singular. Hence, $\\text{Ker}(T)\\ne {0}.$\n\n- Statement III is false. \nFor example,  the point $(1,-3)$ satisfies the equation  $y=-3x$ since $-3=-3\\cdot 1.$ Therefore, the vector $⟨1,-3⟩\\in \\text{Im}(T).$ Thus, $\\text{Im}(T)\\ne {0}.$"}] [{:db/id 17592186366059 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id 17592186176089 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "ba9d2401-4e30-5327-b8eb-b4db7108dd0c" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying True Statements Regarding Linear Transformations in the Cartesian Plane"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "6ad82c63-09eb-5db5-a423-cb22d7590b06" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "None of the listed" :db/id 17592186366061} {:answer/id #uuid "77a61faf-4e00-545a-b2e4-e704769e3e06" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id 17592186366062} {:answer/id #uuid "e857bdb4-10de-5d73-aae4-c628eae4e59e" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id 17592186366063} {:answer/id #uuid "724a5c19-9e50-5e69-92f1-fa998b538696" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "All of the listed" :db/id 17592186366064} {:answer/id #uuid "e5ff393d-6aa6-52e0-80d1-5e6793a9819d" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id 17592186366065}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "e5ff393d-6aa6-52e0-80d1-5e6793a9819d" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id 17592186366065} :answer-field/id #uuid "1718619d-4a2a-5ce8-936e-96766764ba24" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id 17592186366060}] :question/difficulty {:db/id 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "07bd3910-da00-5269-ba42-c4e894fc0a77" :question/math-academy-id "q-38305" :question/problem "If $T$ maps ${R}^{2}$ onto the line $5y=6x$ then, which of the following statements are true?\n\n- $⟨\\frac{6}{5},1⟩\\in \\text{Im}(T)$\n- $\\text{Ker}(T)\\ne {0}$\n- $\\text{nullity}(T)=0$, where $T$ is the standard matrix of $T$" :question/worked-solution "Recall that when the image of a linear transformation $T$ in ${R}^{2}$ with the standard matrix $T$ is a one-dimensional line, then\n\n$$\n\\text{rank}(T)=\\text{rank}(T)=1\n$$\n\nand, according to the rank-nullity theorem, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{nullity}(T)=\\text{nullity}(T) & =2-\\text{rank}(T) \\\\  & =2-1 \\\\  & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our statements in turn. \n\n- Statement I is false.\nNotice that the point $(\\frac{6}{5},1)$ does not satisfy the equation of the line $5y=6x$ since $5\\cdot 1\\ne \\frac{6}{5}\\cdot 6.$ Therefore, \n$⟨\\frac{6}{5},1⟩\\notin \\text{Im}(T).$\n\n- Statement II is true.\nSince $T$ maps ${R}^{2}$ onto a line passing through the origin, we have that the standard matrix $T$ is singular. So, $\\text{Ker}(T)\\ne {0}.$\n\n- Statement III is false.\nSince  statement II is true we have that $\\text{nullity}(T)=\\text{dim(Ker}(T))\\ne 0.$"}] [{:db/id 17592186366097 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id 17592186188273 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "e12609b7-286b-50a8-8373-a331d60ab904" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying True Statements Using the Rank-Nullity Theorem"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "52261ad5-06ec-5820-b379-2fa7e2076f68" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "None of the statements are true" :db/id 17592186366099} {:answer/id #uuid "2adddfdb-1396-5670-af1d-5f244b91a536" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id 17592186366100} {:answer/id #uuid "18a44539-28f0-5861-b3bf-f00b77964913" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id 17592186366101} {:answer/id #uuid "8e23dce7-f9e9-528b-b5da-facf341c2566" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id 17592186366102} {:answer/id #uuid "aedc4dae-4a5e-589f-b565-ed70c37e8829" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id 17592186366103}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "52261ad5-06ec-5820-b379-2fa7e2076f68" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "None of the statements are true" :db/id 17592186366099} :answer-field/id #uuid "969eea6e-2298-5a9b-b572-aaf060b2e3e8" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id 17592186366098}] :question/difficulty {:db/id 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "936f721f-93be-5c51-8233-7da49b500101" :question/math-academy-id "q-38979" :question/problem "Let $T:{R}^{n}\\to {R}^{n}$ be a linear transformation whose standard matrix is $T.$ Which of the following statements are true?\n\n- If $\\text{Ker}(T)={⟨x,y,z⟩\\,|\\,x+y=0}$ then $\\text{rank}(T)+1=n$\n- If there exists a vector $v\\notin \\text{Im}(T),$ then $|T|\\ne 0$\n- If ${{v}_{1},{v}_{2}}$ is linearly independent and $T({v}_{1})=T({v}_{2})=0$ then $\\text{nullity}(T)=1$" :question/worked-solution "Since $T$ is a linear transformation from ${R}^{n}$ into ${R}^{n},$ the invertible matrix theorem states the following:\n\n $T$ is a bijection (an invertible transformation) $\\,⟺\\,{\\,\\begin{aligned} & \\text{Im}(T)={R}^{n} \\\\  & \\text{rank}(T)=n \\\\  & \\text{Ker}(T)={0} \\\\  & \\text{nullity}(T)=0\\end{aligned}$ \n\nAlso, according to the rank-nullity theorem, \n\n$$\n\\text{rank}(T)+\\text{nullity}(T)=n.\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each statement in turn.\n\n- Statement I is false. Solving $x+y=0$ for $x,$ we get $x=-y.$ Therefore, any vector from $\\text{Im}(T)$ can be written as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y \\\\ z\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}-y \\\\ y \\\\ z\\end{aligned}]=y{{[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{{v}_{1}}+z{{[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{{v}_{2}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis means that $\\text{Ker}(T)=\\text{Span}{{v}_{1},{v}_{2}}.$ Therefore, $\\text{nullity}(T)=2.$ Finally, according the rank-nullity theorem, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{rank}(T)+\\text{nullity}(T) & =n \\\\ \\text{rank}(T)+2 & =n.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement II is false. If $v\\notin \\text{Im}(T)$ then $\\text{Im}(T)\\ne {R}^{n}$, so, according to the invertible matrix theorem, $T$ is not a bijection. Thus, the matrix $T$ is not invertible, and, consequently, $|T|=0.$\n\n- Statement III is false. Since $T({v}_{1})=T({v}_{2})=0$, then ${v}_{1},{v}_{2}\\in \\text{Ker}(T).$ But the vectors are linearly independent, therefore, the dimension of  $\\text{Ker}(T)$ is at least $2.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"None of the statements are true.\""}] [{:db/id 17592186366427 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id 17592186188273 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "e12609b7-286b-50a8-8373-a331d60ab904" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying True Statements Using the Rank-Nullity Theorem"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "e48f08ab-e789-52b5-90a4-2c5c3b1dec2d" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id 17592186366429} {:answer/id #uuid "66559173-9f8e-5a08-9ea5-6edc898fe959" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id 17592186366430} {:answer/id #uuid "7b616462-d520-5e6e-997d-be197c87b620" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id 17592186366431} {:answer/id #uuid "7e5c2d6d-eb93-517f-a9d8-9411a7f6cf62" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id 17592186366432} {:answer/id #uuid "fd87133a-e8f2-5b48-9a50-07332a6fc47d" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id 17592186366433}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "7e5c2d6d-eb93-517f-a9d8-9411a7f6cf62" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id 17592186366432} :answer-field/id #uuid "87c146b7-c44d-5014-a4a1-646dc9ff420e" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id 17592186366428}] :question/difficulty {:db/id 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "3f6a64ec-417d-5f3c-a079-819cbc656ca3" :question/math-academy-id "q-83440" :question/problem "Let $T:{R}^{3}\\to {R}^{3}$ be a linear transformation whose standard matrix is $T,$ and let ${{v}_{1},{v}_{2}}$ be a set of linearly independent vectors in ${R}^{3}.$ Consider the following statement:\n\n*If  $\\text{Ker}(T)=\\text{Span}{{v}_{1},{v}_{2}}$, then $\\text{__________}.$ *\n\nWhich of the following can be placed into the blank space to make a true statement?\n\n- $\\text{nullity}(T)=2$\n- $\\text{rank}(T)=1$\n- $T$ is a bijection" :question/worked-solution "Since $T$ is a linear transformation from ${R}^{3}$ into ${R}^{3},$ the invertible matrix theorem states the following:\n\n $T$ is a bijection (an invertible transformation) $\\,⟺\\,{\\,\\begin{aligned} & \\text{Im}(T)={R}^{3} \\\\  & \\text{rank}(T)=3 \\\\  & \\text{Ker}(T)={0} \\\\  & \\text{nullity}(T)=0\\end{aligned}$ \n\nAlso, according to the rank-nullity theorem, \n\n$$\n\\text{rank}(T)+\\text{nullity}(T)=3.\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each statement in turn.\n\n- Statement I is true. Since $\\text{Ker}(T)=\\text{Span}{{v}_{1},{v}_{2}}$ and the set ${{v}_{1},{v}_{2}}$ is linearly independent, we get that $\\text{nullity}(T)=2.$\n\n- Statement II is true. According to the rank-nullity theorem, we obtain \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{rank}(T) & =3-\\text{nullity}(T) \\\\  & =3-2 \\\\  & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement III is false. Since $\\text{rank}(T)\\ne 3$ (or since $\\text{nullity}(T)\\ne 0$), the linear transformation $T$ cannot be a bijection according to the invertible matrix theorem.\n\nTherefore, the correct option is \"I and II only.\""}] [{:db/id 17592186366483 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id 17592186133951 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "da20ffe7-173b-5fc0-8629-3b9b70222860" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying True Statements About Orthogonal Matrices"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "ca1c9011-4ae6-5782-bddd-b9bf7a3e38b4" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "None of the statements are true" :db/id 17592186366485} {:answer/id #uuid "61914b14-f5b4-52cc-9c62-15192bb09db9" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, II, and III" :db/id 17592186366486} {:answer/id #uuid "a31df482-5dfb-5c2d-8201-f46fa3b37265" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id 17592186366487} {:answer/id #uuid "0005a8ea-913e-5b7b-b095-e71af063b627" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id 17592186366488} {:answer/id #uuid "b5033569-8b16-5f3d-bb71-10e07bf5a1d5" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id 17592186366489}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "ca1c9011-4ae6-5782-bddd-b9bf7a3e38b4" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "None of the statements are true" :db/id 17592186366485} :answer-field/id #uuid "d824b74c-9e1f-5141-804b-27c8039bcb40" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id 17592186366484}] :question/difficulty {:db/id 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "e6401598-f82b-58db-b408-23496dde6519" :question/math-academy-id "q-84344" :question/problem "Which of the following statements are true regarding the $n\\times n$ orthogonal matrices $P$ and $Q?$\n\n- $2PQ$ is always orthogonal\n- $P-Q$ is always orthogonal\n- $P$ is **not** invertible" :question/worked-solution "Recall that a matrix $Q$ is orthogonal if and only if\n\n$$\nQ{Q}^{T}={Q}^{T}Q=I.\n$$\n\nTo check that a matrix is orthogonal, it is sufficient to check that the above equation is satisfied.\n\nMoreover, we have the following properties:\n\n- For any orthogonal matrix $Q,$ we have ${Q}^{-1}={Q}^{T}.$\n\n- $(AB)^{T}={B}^{T}{A}^{T}$ for conformable matrices $A$ and $B.$\n\nWith that in mind, let's inspect each of the statements in turn.\n\n- Statement I is false. Computing the product $(2PQ)\\cdot (2PQ)^{T},$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(2PQ)\\cdot (2PQ)^{T} & =2PQ\\cdot 2{Q}^{T}{P}^{T} \\\\  & =4P(Q{Q}^{T}){P}^{T} \\\\  & =4P(I){P}^{T} \\\\  & =4I \\\\  & \\ne I.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement II is false. As a counterexample, consider the following subtraction of two orthogonal matrices:\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]-[\\begin{aligned}0 & -1 \\\\ -1 & 0\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 1\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nThe resulting matrix is not orthogonal since, for example, its first column is not normalized.\n\n- Statement III is false. The inverse of an orthogonal matrix $P$ is given by ${P}^{-1}={P}^{T}.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"None of the statements are true\"."}] [{:db/id 17592186366490 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id 17592186133951 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "da20ffe7-173b-5fc0-8629-3b9b70222860" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying True Statements About Orthogonal Matrices"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "e52ee037-64df-55a7-b783-d32c998fe3ac" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "None of the statements are true" :db/id 17592186366492} {:answer/id #uuid "04f54020-5ff2-5ad2-afc8-663e0a9e5727" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, II, and III" :db/id 17592186366493} {:answer/id #uuid "84196f39-0db9-58f2-9bc2-f0718b6e1e3e" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id 17592186366494} {:answer/id #uuid "b88d42a1-4ee6-55f0-a80d-0a04d29989d1" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id 17592186366495} {:answer/id #uuid "a01565f9-4951-5ba5-a2e2-3ba4906940bb" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id 17592186366496}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "04f54020-5ff2-5ad2-afc8-663e0a9e5727" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, II, and III" :db/id 17592186366493} :answer-field/id #uuid "a4596c93-1f98-56fd-be89-7326232e9627" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id 17592186366491}] :question/difficulty {:db/id 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "dcdd2917-752d-5fd1-ac2a-6a285cbe6a4f" :question/math-academy-id "q-84353" :question/problem "Which of the following statements are true regarding the $n\\times n$ orthogonal matrices $P$ and $Q?$\n\n- $Q$ is invertible\n- ${P}^{T}$ is orthogonal\n- ${Q}^{-1}$ is always orthogonal" :question/worked-solution "Recall that a matrix $Q$ is orthogonal if and only if\n\n$$\nQ{Q}^{T}={Q}^{T}Q=I.\n$$\n\nTo check that a matrix is orthogonal, it is sufficient to check that the above equation is satisfied.\n\nMoreover, we have the following properties:\n\n- For any orthogonal matrix $Q,$ we have ${Q}^{-1}={Q}^{T}.$\n\n- $(AB)^{T}={B}^{T}{A}^{T}$ for conformable matrices $A$ and $B.$\n\nWith that in mind, let's inspect each of the statements in turn.\n\n- Statement I is true. The inverse of an orthogonal matrix $Q$ is given by ${Q}^{-1}={Q}^{T}.$\n\n- Statement II is true. Computing the product ${P}^{T}\\cdot ({P}^{T})^{T},$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{P}^{T}\\cdot ({P}^{T})^{T} & ={P}^{T}\\cdot P \\\\  & =I.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement III is true. Computing the product ${Q}^{-1}\\cdot ({Q}^{-1})^{T},$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{Q}^{-1}\\cdot ({Q}^{-1})^{T} & ={Q}^{T}\\cdot ({Q}^{T})^{T} \\\\  & ={Q}^{T}Q \\\\  & =I.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I, II, and III.\""}] [{:db/id 17592186366553 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id 17592186161979 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "5c2da07e-23b5-58d0-aeef-14e21b329368" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Symmetric Matrices"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "4e2ef4e8-4bc8-5f22-bcea-26f300695fa2" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$P$ and $R$ only" :db/id 17592186366555} {:answer/id #uuid "aff63ab0-12c1-505c-b7b8-b899c34bdaf0" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$P,$ $Q,$ and $R$" :db/id 17592186366556} {:answer/id #uuid "d632b7d7-7d0f-5b18-bdc5-73cc9f693c99" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$Q$ and $R$ only" :db/id 17592186366557} {:answer/id #uuid "da92ebc4-1e7b-50f9-b430-bacb3c007ec3" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$P$ only" :db/id 17592186366558} {:answer/id #uuid "02d02b66-896a-59c9-ab06-541e83d26052" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$P$ and $Q$ only" :db/id 17592186366559}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "02d02b66-896a-59c9-ab06-541e83d26052" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$P$ and $Q$ only" :db/id 17592186366559} :answer-field/id #uuid "d40a1f3b-b768-5864-948b-133ae0173b2f" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id 17592186366554}] :question/difficulty {:db/id 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "1c1ef725-7db0-58fe-a7db-0097c019b6b6" :question/math-academy-id "q-93987" :question/problem "Which of the following matrices are symmetric?\n\n$$\nP=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 5 \\\\ 5 & -1\\end{aligned}],\\,Q=[\\begin{aligned}1 & -2 & 0.5 \\\\ -2 & 0 & 3 \\\\ 0.5 & 3 & -1\\end{aligned}],\\,R=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 \\\\ -2 & 1\\end{aligned}]\n$$" :question/worked-solution "A square matrix $A$ is symmetric if it is equal to its transpose:\n\n$$\nA={A}^{T}\n$$\n\nEquivalently, ${a}_{ij}={a}_{ji}$ for all $i,j.$ In other words, $A$ is symmetric across its main diagonal. \n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each matrix in turn.\n\n- $P$ is a symmetric matrix. Indeed, $P={P}^{T}.$\n\n- $Q$ is a symmetric matrix. Indeed, $Q={Q}^{T}.$\n\n- $R$ is *not* a symmetric matrix. Since ${r}_{12}\\ne {r}_{21},$ we obtain $R\\ne {R}^{T}.$\n\n$$\nR=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 \\\\ -2 & 1\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"$P$ and $Q$ only.\""}] [{:db/id 17592186366560 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id 17592186161979 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "5c2da07e-23b5-58d0-aeef-14e21b329368" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Symmetric Matrices"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "37f0f58d-6200-5015-b95e-2a64465d45eb" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$P$ and $R$ only" :db/id 17592186366562} {:answer/id #uuid "3116cfe4-f0aa-52cf-a243-91123481e80e" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$R$ only" :db/id 17592186366563} {:answer/id #uuid "98fa28e5-8900-569f-8244-1738cf4d3337" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$Q$ only" :db/id 17592186366564} {:answer/id #uuid "aa33aee1-db89-5bc9-a347-03c8491c0e4b" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$P$ only" :db/id 17592186366565} {:answer/id #uuid "d2dfd12e-4619-5cda-89cb-dea84f9591ad" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$P$ and $Q$ only" :db/id 17592186366566}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "aa33aee1-db89-5bc9-a347-03c8491c0e4b" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$P$ only" :db/id 17592186366565} :answer-field/id #uuid "edf5fcb7-d0b1-53f3-bb5e-18cea24a5ecf" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id 17592186366561}] :question/difficulty {:db/id 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "a97d726e-d379-5097-84d8-abb6b0a769af" :question/math-academy-id "q-93992" :question/problem "Which of the following matrices are symmetric?\n\n$$\nP=[\\begin{aligned}-1 & \\frac{1}{2} \\\\ \\frac{1}{2} & 1\\end{aligned}],\\,Q=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}],\\,R=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 & 1 \\\\ -1 & 2 & 3 \\\\ 1 & 3 & 3\\end{aligned}]\n$$" :question/worked-solution "A square matrix $A$ is symmetric if it is equal to its transpose:\n\n$$\nA={A}^{T}\n$$\n\nEquivalently, ${a}_{ij}={a}_{ji}$ for all $i,j.$ In other words, $A$ is symmetric across its main diagonal. \n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each matrix in turn.\n\n- $P$ is a symmetric matrix. Indeed, $P={P}^{T}.$\n\n- $Q$ is *not* a symmetric matrix. Since ${q}_{23}\\ne {q}_{32},$ we obtain $Q\\ne {Q}^{T}.$\n\n$$\nQ=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\n- $R$ is *not* a symmetric matrix. Since ${r}_{12}\\ne {r}_{21},$ we obtain $R\\ne {R}^{T}.$\n\n$$\nR=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 & 1 \\\\ -1 & 2 & 3 \\\\ 1 & 3 & 3\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"$P$ only.\""}] [{:db/id 17592186366608 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id 17592186152221 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "e609aabf-7e7e-5997-b8dc-c66e7a14a36f" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Finite and Infinite-Dimensional Vector Spaces"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "8f517337-3486-5e41-9b39-0c180220781a" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, II, and III" :db/id 17592186366610} {:answer/id #uuid "4dfab1e2-c1de-50ed-8416-0b78f6b3fd0c" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id 17592186366611} {:answer/id #uuid "3dc9d420-6eff-5ee6-af41-08b96ceba9f6" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id 17592186366612} {:answer/id #uuid "61bd780b-bc33-5556-869d-03c7ee8e861e" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id 17592186366613} {:answer/id #uuid "437a131a-8465-554b-9149-de725c2e5fb4" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id 17592186366614}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "4dfab1e2-c1de-50ed-8416-0b78f6b3fd0c" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id 17592186366611} :answer-field/id #uuid "d66b6070-cabf-55e5-9e72-a374aef6d9b7" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id 17592186366609}] :question/difficulty {:db/id 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "bd51b750-1389-5155-b65d-c0946321f8d3" :question/math-academy-id "q-95388" :question/problem "Which of the following vector spaces are finite-dimensional?\n\n- $D(R),$ the vector space of all differentiable functions on $R$\n- ${\\text{M}}_{10\\times 100}(R),$ the vector space of all $10\\times 100$ matrices over $R$\n- ${R}_{200}[t],$ the vector space of all polynomials in the variable $t$ of degree at most $200$" :question/worked-solution "Let's examine our vector spaces.\n\n- $D(R)$ is *not* finite-dimensional. To understand why, we first note that the following subset of $D(R)$ is linearly independent:\n\n$$\n{1,\\,t,\\,{t}^{2},\\,…,{t}^{n}},\\,n\\in N.\n$$\n\nIndeed, we have that \n\n$$\n{\\sum }_{i=0}^{n}{x}_{i}{t}^{i}=0\n$$\n\nfor all $t\\in R$ if and only if ${x}_{i}=0$ for all $i=0,1,2,…,n.$ Therefore,  for any  $n\\in N,$  there exists a linearly independent subset of $D(R)$  of size $n,$ which imples that $D(R)$ cannot have finite dimension.     .\n\n- ${\\text{M}}_{10\\times 100}(R)$ and ${R}_{200}[t]$ are finite-dimensional. Indeed, \n\n$$\n\\text{dim}({\\text{M}}_{10\\times 100}(R))=10\\cdot 100=1\\,000\n$$\n\n \nand\n\n$$\n\\text{dim}({R}_{200}[t])=200+1=201,\n$$\n\nare both finite numbers.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II and III only.\""}] [{:db/id 17592186366718 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id 17592186172029 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "7a6bbb34-a43f-5e16-8a24-25c0d6970a38" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying a Quadratic Curve Type From Its Reduced Equation: No Cross-Product Term"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "74284b82-6c00-50fb-87d1-3ddbcaf76ea7" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id 17592186366720} {:answer/id #uuid "e6f287e6-fc5a-5612-b6dc-6230a07c50e7" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id 17592186366721} {:answer/id #uuid "c06a6c66-9a0d-5f1b-9652-7f1a9fa3de3b" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id 17592186366722} {:answer/id #uuid "104fff1c-e7d5-577f-9a3f-09e2515d3988" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id 17592186366723} {:answer/id #uuid "8de9089d-d7bc-5109-9499-41d65a155982" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id 17592186366724}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "c06a6c66-9a0d-5f1b-9652-7f1a9fa3de3b" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id 17592186366722} :answer-field/id #uuid "9b490a57-fb54-5a0a-88db-c6476bcf4020" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id 17592186366719}] :question/difficulty {:db/id 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "66610d66-38e7-5d9e-b883-9467a5f6ad73" :question/math-academy-id "q-95897" :question/problem "Which of the following equations define a non-empty set of points in the ${x}_{1}{x}_{2}$ plane?\n\n- $2{x}_{2}^{2}+1=0$\n- $-2{x}_{1}^{2}-3{x}_{2}^{2}=2$\n- ${x}_{1}^{2}+{x}_{2}=0$" :question/worked-solution "Let's examine each of the equations in turn.\n\n- The equation $2{x}_{2}^{2}+1=0$ defines an empty set. Notice that the left-hand side is positive for all ${x}_{2},$ while the right-hand side is zero.\n\n- The equation $-2{x}_{1}^{2}-3{x}_{2}^{2}=2$ defines an empty set. Notice that the left-hand side is non-positive for all ${x}_{1}$ and ${x}_{2},$ while the right-hand side is  positive.\n\n- The equation ${x}_{1}^{2}+{x}_{2}=0$ defines a non-empty set in the ${x}_{1}{x}_{2}$-plane. It represents a parabola that opens in the direction of the negative ${x}_{2}$-axis.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"III only.\""}] [{:db/id 17592186366945 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id 17592186115334 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "bf19e2ee-7c42-58b0-9254-5a44f315288a" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Block Diagonal and Block Triangular Matrices"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "69d36ff0-1b22-50b5-af1f-216011cbce4d" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$A$ and $C$ only" :db/id 17592186366947} {:answer/id #uuid "71adef4e-c668-593f-bf0e-363de3313319" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$A$ only" :db/id 17592186366948} {:answer/id #uuid "abf3e242-bc6f-51cb-b1a1-baaccec73360" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "None" :db/id 17592186366949} {:answer/id #uuid "c9487d7a-239c-541e-a823-dd26d4bfdfb3" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$A$ and $B$ only" :db/id 17592186366950} {:answer/id #uuid "c4e4ccef-1729-57af-8c64-b5c59745921b" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$C$ only" :db/id 17592186366951}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "c4e4ccef-1729-57af-8c64-b5c59745921b" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$C$ only" :db/id 17592186366951} :answer-field/id #uuid "04ff00ee-7b3e-55f0-8fb1-096561394844" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id 17592186366946}] :question/difficulty {:db/id 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "3da29d09-2c10-59b3-aafb-a3f2d64ea706" :question/math-academy-id "q-109246" :question/problem "Which of the following matrices are block upper triangular?\n\n$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}4 & 0 \\\\ 9 & -2\\end{aligned}],\\,B=[\\begin{aligned}3 & 8 & 9 \\\\ 0 & -1 & 3 \\\\ 4 & 0 & 2\\end{aligned}],\\,C=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 5 \\\\ 4 & 1 & 4 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}].\n$$" :question/worked-solution "A block upper triangular matrix can be partitioned so that it has only square blocks on the main diagonal, and the blocks below the main diagonal contain only zeros.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our matrices.\n\n- $A$is *not* a block upper triangular matrix since it can't be partitioned in the way described above:\n\n$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}4 & 0 \\\\ 9 & -2\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\n- $B$ is *not* a block upper triangular matrix since it can't be partitioned in the way described above:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}B=[\\begin{aligned}3 & 8 & 9 \\\\ 0 & -1 & 3 \\\\ 4 & 0 & 2\\end{aligned}],\\,B=[\\begin{aligned}3 & 8 & 9 \\\\ 0 & -1 & 3 \\\\ 4 & 0 & 2\\end{aligned}],\\,B=[\\begin{aligned}3 & 8 & 9 \\\\ 0 & -1 & 3 \\\\ 4 & 0 & 2\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- $C$ is a block upper triangular matrix. Indeed, we can partition it as follows:\n\n$$\nC=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 5 \\\\ 4 & 1 & 4 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"$C$ only.\""}] [{:db/id 17592186367338 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id 17592186152193 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "b0829d4a-239a-5a8e-9c8e-3cdc76a993bc" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Solutions of Linear ODEs Using the Constant Multiple Rule"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "7eb0a67d-d925-5d22-b428-c6a5e1df2532" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id 17592186367340} {:answer/id #uuid "57d49a75-850a-580c-911f-1b7521839673" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id 17592186367341} {:answer/id #uuid "9f341b2f-f73f-57f8-9565-0922cd0dee9d" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id 17592186367342} {:answer/id #uuid "f91f2315-a41a-5693-9f45-09f93132b066" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id 17592186367343} {:answer/id #uuid "3e571ba3-1fc9-52bc-a38b-8d8a46aefa34" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id 17592186367344}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "57d49a75-850a-580c-911f-1b7521839673" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id 17592186367341} :answer-field/id #uuid "7e28e57c-d582-5079-8b43-c2947583f2bf" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id 17592186367339}] :question/difficulty {:db/id 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "5c98c0e9-7419-5d7e-b919-df2475fb8298" :question/math-academy-id "q-131025" :question/problem "Given that ${y}_{1}(x)={e}^{-2x}$ is a solution to the equation $(1+2x){y}^{″}+4x{y}^{′}-4y=0,$ then according to the superposition principle which of the following must also be a solution to the equation?\n\n- $y(x)={e}^{4-2x}$\n- $y(x)=x{e}^{-2x}$\n- $y(x)=\\frac{2}{{e}^{2x}}$" :question/worked-solution "According to the superposition principle, if the function ${y}_{1}(x)$ is a solution to a homogeneous, linear differential equation, and $c$ is a constant, then $y(x)=c{y}_{1}(x)$ is also a solution to the equation.\n\nLet's consider each option:\n\n- The function $y(x)={e}^{4-2x}={e}^{4}\\cdot {e}^{-2x}$ is a solution of the equation by the superposition principle. In this case, $c={e}^{4}.$\n\n- The function $y(x)=x{e}^{-2x}$ is **not** guaranteed to be a solution of the equation by the superposition principle. The superposition principle does not extend to the product of a non-constant function and a known solution.\n\n- The function $y(x)=\\frac{2}{{e}^{2x}}=2\\cdot {e}^{-2x}$ is a solution of the equation by the superposition principle. In this case, $c=2.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and III only.\""}] [{:db/id 17592186367345 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id 17592186152193 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "b0829d4a-239a-5a8e-9c8e-3cdc76a993bc" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Solutions of Linear ODEs Using the Constant Multiple Rule"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "eb5bafa6-ea18-5d1f-94f3-c9702413a2ca" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, II, and III" :db/id 17592186367347} {:answer/id #uuid "aec31c83-427c-5433-be3c-d3aaa1a170f0" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id 17592186367348} {:answer/id #uuid "b9fee1a3-6f09-5ce5-b634-79b4cf65cc0d" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id 17592186367349} {:answer/id #uuid "7f0a9e3e-145e-5e00-a5b0-538c19faaaa3" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id 17592186367350} {:answer/id #uuid "a30660cf-2fd5-5002-800b-0597189e69d5" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id 17592186367351}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "b9fee1a3-6f09-5ce5-b634-79b4cf65cc0d" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id 17592186367349} :answer-field/id #uuid "b9a9a9d7-cbc3-5982-bbd6-90ac6d838ddf" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id 17592186367346}] :question/difficulty {:db/id 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "e6f1fe4c-12f4-51c1-8c80-ca5bb6fcfc47" :question/math-academy-id "q-131027" :question/problem "Given that ${y}_{1}(x)={e}^{x/3}$ is a solution to the equation $6{y}^{″}+{y}^{′}-y=0,$ then according to the superposition principle which of the following must also be a solution to the equation?\n\n- $y(x)={e}^{(x-3)/3}$\n- $y(x)=\\frac{x}{3}{e}^{x/3}$\n- $y(x)=\\frac{5{e}^{x}}{{e}^{2x/3}}$" :question/worked-solution "According to the superposition principle, if the function ${y}_{1}(x)$ is a solution to a homogeneous, linear differential equation, and $c$ is a constant, then $y(x)=c{y}_{1}(x)$ is also a solution to the equation.\n\nLet's consider each option:\n\n- The function $y(x)={e}^{(x-3)/3}={e}^{-1}\\cdot {e}^{x/3}$ is a solution of the equation by the superposition principle. In this case, $c={e}^{-1}.$\n\n- The function $y(x)=\\frac{x}{3}{e}^{x/3}$ is **not** guaranteed to be a solution of the equation by the superposition principle. The superposition principle does not extend to the product of a non-constant function and a known solution.\n\n- The function $y(x)=\\frac{5{e}^{x}}{{e}^{2x/3}}=5\\cdot {e}^{x-2x/3}=5{e}^{x/3}$ is a solution of the equation by the superposition principle. In this case, $c=5.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and III only.\""}] [{:db/id 17592186367352 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id 17592186152169 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "101e0c22-fbb4-5865-af5f-d669e2488d09" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Solutions to Linear ODEs Using the Superposition Principle"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "3d45716c-2fad-5726-95ee-c6bbc118d67c" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, II, and III" :db/id 17592186367354} {:answer/id #uuid "5d218f3c-017a-5173-a17f-6b465daff3ac" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id 17592186367355} {:answer/id #uuid "bc41caf8-bda8-5d39-b161-7900af7b7bf7" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id 17592186367356} {:answer/id #uuid "e73c7757-1072-5625-8352-302ce70aa8ee" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id 17592186367357} {:answer/id #uuid "56a7aad0-4f03-5115-851a-6114b8fa377a" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id 17592186367358}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "3d45716c-2fad-5726-95ee-c6bbc118d67c" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, II, and III" :db/id 17592186367354} :answer-field/id #uuid "0f9d91eb-ad21-5686-9de6-f6a5e523e1ef" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id 17592186367353}] :question/difficulty {:db/id 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "5af618e0-efed-5d52-a5fe-f92221b7400c" :question/math-academy-id "q-131038" :question/problem "Given that ${y}_{1}(t)={e}^{-3t}$ and ${y}_{2}(t)={e}^{-2t}$ are solutions to the equation ${y}^{″}+5{y}^{′}+6y=0,$ which of the following must also be a solution to the equation?\n\n- $y(t)=2{e}^{-3t}+3{e}^{-2t}$\n- $y(t)=(4+{e}^{t}){e}^{-3t}$\n- $y(t)=5{e}^{-3t}$" :question/worked-solution "According to the superposition principle, if the functions ${y}_{1}(t)$ and ${y}_{2}(t)$ are solutions to a homogeneous, linear differential equation, and ${c}_{1}$ and ${c}_{2}$ are constants, then $y(t)={c}_{1}{y}_{1}(t)+{c}_{2}{y}_{2}(t)$ is also a solution to the equation.\n\nWe're given that ${y}_{1}(t)={e}^{-3t}$ and ${y}_{2}(t)={e}^{-2t}.$ Therefore, the following function is also a solution:\n\n$$\ny(t)={c}_{1}{e}^{-3t}+{c}_{2}t{e}^{-2t}\n$$\n\nLet's consider each option:\n\n- The function $y(t)=2{e}^{-3t}+3{e}^{-2t}$ is a solution of the given equation. Here, ${c}_{1}=2,{c}_{2}=3.$\n\n- The function $y(t)=(4+{e}^{t}){e}^{-3t}=4{e}^{-3t}+{e}^{-2t}$ is a solution of the given equation. Here, ${c}_{1}=4,{c}_{2}=1.$\n\n- The function $y(t)=5{e}^{-3t}$ is a solution of the given equation. Here, ${c}_{1}=5,{c}_{2}=0.$"}] [{:db/id 17592186367387 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id 17592186115334 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "bf19e2ee-7c42-58b0-9254-5a44f315288a" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Block Diagonal and Block Triangular Matrices"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "5ef9cea3-974f-528a-8a79-d769560f6f23" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$A$ and $C$ only" :db/id 17592186367389} {:answer/id #uuid "2d4039a2-724f-5084-a178-230ee24c7ab4" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$A$ only" :db/id 17592186367390} {:answer/id #uuid "79cfabe6-5e8a-5586-b435-8d612b308e2c" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$A$ and $B$ only" :db/id 17592186367391} {:answer/id #uuid "99b24c2d-4353-53c8-a796-8d1caa274c2f" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$C$ only" :db/id 17592186367392} {:answer/id #uuid "e9ecd1cb-4821-5b77-bb54-6d54d427e49e" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$B$ only" :db/id 17592186367393}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "5ef9cea3-974f-528a-8a79-d769560f6f23" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$A$ and $C$ only" :db/id 17592186367389} :answer-field/id #uuid "de0f3f59-de03-58a3-8a89-5751a78710ff" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id 17592186367388}] :question/difficulty {:db/id 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "ba25631a-3eab-56f2-a3d7-d0c6da834d90" :question/math-academy-id "q-131447" :question/problem "Which of the following matrices are block diagonal?\n\n$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -2 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 3 & 7 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 2 & 1\\end{aligned}],\\,B=[\\begin{aligned}5 & 2 \\\\ 7 & 0\\end{aligned}],\\,C=[\\begin{aligned}4 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 3 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 5\\end{aligned}].\n$$" :question/worked-solution "A block diagonal matrix can be partitioned so that it has only square blocks on the main diagonal, and all the other blocks contain only zeros.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our matrices.\n\n- $A$ is a block diagonal matrix. Indeed, we can partition it as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -2 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 3 & 7 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 2 & 1\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- $B$ is *not* a block diagonal matrix since it can't be partitioned in the way described above:\n\n$$\nB=[\\begin{aligned}5 & 2 \\\\ 7 & 0\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\n- $C$ is a block diagonal matrix. Indeed, we can partition it as follows:\n\n$$\nC=[\\begin{aligned}4 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 3 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 5\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"$A$ and $C$ only.\""}] [{:db/id 17592186367881 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id 17592186124570 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "40d58ee6-1121-5ddc-a96f-1a7fa7a193dd" :knowledge-point/title "Classifying Local Extrema Using Sylvester's Criterion"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2bdcb378-9b17-5091-8e17-1a127ff923c3" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "None of the statements are true" :db/id 17592186367883} {:answer/id #uuid "19754d07-0117-5cd5-9bef-485b95bb1895" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, II, and III" :db/id 17592186367884} {:answer/id #uuid "3632ca5c-ec54-5176-ae55-ab8f0ccda827" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id 17592186367885} {:answer/id #uuid "8453f8ea-6574-5d78-acbc-6fa00e579336" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id 17592186367886} {:answer/id #uuid "65455843-bb52-543e-9930-2c27a520e751" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id 17592186367887}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "19754d07-0117-5cd5-9bef-485b95bb1895" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, II, and III" :db/id 17592186367884} :answer-field/id #uuid "98cace57-36bb-5a76-b3a7-cc33bc716166" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id 17592186367882}] :question/difficulty {:db/id 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "883c276b-11e1-503d-959e-3a4c3a5e61c4" :question/math-academy-id "q-163777" :question/problem "The quadratic Taylor approximation of the function $f(x)$ about $x=a\\in {R}^{2}$ is given by\n\n$$\nf(x)≈3+\\frac{1}{2}(x-a)^{T}A(x-a),\n$$\n\nwhere \n\n$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}-2 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 3\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\n \n\nWhich of the following statements are true?\n\n- ${a}_{11}=-2$ and $det(A)=-7$\n- $A$ is neither positive-definite nor negative-definite\n- $a$ is a saddle point of $f$" :question/worked-solution "The point $a\\in {R}^{n}$ is a critical point of the function $f:{R}^{n}\\to R$ if ${f}^{′}(a)={0}^{T}.$ In such cases, we can classify $a$ as follows:\n\n- if ${f}^{″}(a)$ is positive definite, then $a$ is a local minimum\n\n- if ${f}^{″}(a)$ is negative definite, then $a$ is a local maximum\n\n- if ${f}^{″}(a)$ is neither positive-definite nor negative-definite, then $a$ is a saddle point\n\nIn this case, we have $A={f}^{″}(a).$ Now, before we examine the statements, let's recall how to categorize quadratic forms.\n\nLet ${Δ}_{i}$ for $i=1,2,…,n$  denote the leading principal minors of an $n\\times n$ matrix $A.$ Then, according to Sylvester's criterion, the quadratic form ${x}^{T}\\,A\\,x$ (as well as the matrix $A$) is \n\n- positive-definite if and only if ${Δ}_{i}>0$ for all $i=1,2,…,n,$\n\n- negative-definite if and only if, for all $i=1,2,…,n,$ we have ${Δ}_{i}<0$ when $i$ is odd, and ${Δ}_{i}>0$ when $i$ is even.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each statement in turn.\n\n- Statement I is true. Indeed, ${a}_{11}=-2$ and\n\n$$\ndet(A)=|\\begin{aligned}-2 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 3\\end{aligned}|=-7.\n$$\n\n- Statement II is true. Notice that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{Δ}_{1} & ={a}_{11}=-2<0, \\\\ {Δ}_{2} & =|\\begin{aligned}-2 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 3\\end{aligned}|=-7<0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, by Sylvester's criterion, the matrix $A$ is neither positive-definite nor negative-definite.\n\n- Statement III is true. Since the matrix $A$ is neither positive-definite nor negative-definite, the critical point $a$ is a saddle point of $f.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I, II, and III.\""}] [{:db/id 17592186367888 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id 17592186124570 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "40d58ee6-1121-5ddc-a96f-1a7fa7a193dd" :knowledge-point/title "Classifying Local Extrema Using Sylvester's Criterion"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "6cd99cd5-ac0d-53d1-865b-83d8b73fd3a2" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "None of the statements are true" :db/id 17592186367890} {:answer/id #uuid "34b19b11-5aa1-505b-9f36-6f2d94b4f32b" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id 17592186367891} {:answer/id #uuid "e4ebc83e-3b44-54d2-95a8-0de91882ddf1" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id 17592186367892} {:answer/id #uuid "afa00808-4640-5931-99bc-fd53f585d867" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id 17592186367893} {:answer/id #uuid "9f895f5a-45c6-5e98-96ad-9e05f2f12b6a" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id 17592186367894}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "e4ebc83e-3b44-54d2-95a8-0de91882ddf1" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id 17592186367892} :answer-field/id #uuid "f63163a1-bf8c-56be-b743-efedda7d3b91" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id 17592186367889}] :question/difficulty {:db/id 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "61ffb698-9cc2-5e02-b6d9-afec7d1f3422" :question/math-academy-id "q-163790" :question/problem "The quadratic Taylor approximation of the function $f(x)$ about $x=a\\in {R}^{3}$ is given by\n\n$$\nf(x)≈-7+\\frac{1}{2}(x-a)^{T}A(x-a),\n$$\n\nwhere \n\n$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}-5 & 2 & 4 \\\\ 2 & -3 & -1 \\\\ 4 & -1 & -4\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\n \n\nWhich of the following statements are true?\n\n- $det(A)>0$\n- $A$ is positive-definite\n- $a$ is a local maximum of $f$" :question/worked-solution "The point $a\\in {R}^{n}$ is a critical point of the function $f:{R}^{n}\\to R$ if ${f}^{′}(a)={0}^{T}.$ In such cases, we can classify $a$ as follows:\n\n- if ${f}^{″}(a)$ is positive definite, then $a$ is a local minimum\n\n- if ${f}^{″}(a)$ is negative definite, then $a$ is a local maximum\n\n- if ${f}^{″}(a)$ is neither positive-definite nor negative-definite, then $a$ is a saddle point\n\nIn this case, we have $A={f}^{″}(a).$ Now, before we examine the statements, we recall how to categorize quadratic forms.\n\nLet ${Δ}_{i}$ for $i=1,2,…,n$  denote the leading principal minors of an $n\\times n$ matrix $A.$ Then, according to Sylvester's criterion, the quadratic form ${x}^{T}\\,A\\,x$ (as well as the matrix $A$) is \n\n- positive-definite if and only if ${Δ}_{i}>0$ for all $i=1,2,…,n,$\n\n- negative-definite if and only if, for all $i=1,2,…,n,$ we have ${Δ}_{i}<0$ when $i$ is odd, and ${Δ}_{i}>0$ when $i$ is even.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each statement in turn.\n\n- Statement I is false. Indeed,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}det(A) & =|\\begin{aligned}-5 & 2 & 4 \\\\ 2 & -3 & -1 \\\\ 4 & -1 & -4\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =-5\\cdot |\\begin{aligned}-3 & -1 \\\\ -1 & -4\\end{aligned}|-2\\cdot |\\begin{aligned}2 & -1 \\\\ 4 & -4\\end{aligned}|+4\\cdot |\\begin{aligned}2 & -3 \\\\ 4 & -1\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =-5(11)-2(-4)+4(10) \\\\  & =-7<0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement II is false. Notice the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{Δ}_{1} & ={a}_{11}=-5<0 \\\\ {Δ}_{2} & =|\\begin{aligned}-5 & 2 \\\\ 2 & -3\\end{aligned}|=11>0 \\\\ {Δ}_{3} & =|A|=-7<0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, by Sylvester's criterion, the matrix $A$ is negative-definite.\n\n- Statement III is true. Since the matrix $A$ is negative-definite, the critical point $a$ is a local maximum of $f.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"III only.\""}] [{:db/id 17592186368243 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id 17592186152281 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "a34d342c-e7fc-581b-b3d1-ed6a49f1a62f" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Boundary Value Problems"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "dc469ea1-79c9-52d1-81a6-2552c0d7b7a5" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id 17592186368245} {:answer/id #uuid "c9f5c5e9-8572-5a90-8258-c0b86e590584" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id 17592186368246} {:answer/id #uuid "286b767a-7e4b-5004-96bc-dfa2fa2a10d2" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id 17592186368247} {:answer/id #uuid "1deef986-7713-552b-bba4-ed0a01c44300" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id 17592186368248} {:answer/id #uuid "fff00a93-c9be-561a-844f-19a7dab63479" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id 17592186368249}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "286b767a-7e4b-5004-96bc-dfa2fa2a10d2" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id 17592186368247} :answer-field/id #uuid "8caa9690-b35c-5a66-a23b-26780bada7bd" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id 17592186368244}] :question/difficulty {:db/id 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "67ebcbc8-9a0b-5eef-bc58-dbb711498ad0" :question/math-academy-id "q-338536" :question/problem "Which of the following systems is a boundary value problem?\n\n- ${y}^{″}+{y}^{′}-y={x}^{2},\\,y(0)=1,\\,y(2)=3$\n- ${y}^{″}-4y=\\operatorname{sin}⁡x,\\,y(1)=0,\\,{y}^{′}(1)=2$\n- ${y}^{″}+2{y}^{′}+y={e}^{x}$" :question/worked-solution "If a second-order differential equation for the function $y(x)$ is given with two conditions specified at *different* locations in the domain of $y,$ then this is a **boundary value problem**.\n\nLet's go through each equation in turn.\n\n- The first system is a boundary value problem. It consists of a differential equation together with two conditions at different points in the domain of $y(x)$ (i.e., at $x=0$ and $x=2$).\n\n- The second system is not a boundary value problem. The differential equation is together with two conditions at the *same* location in the domain of $y(x)$ (i.e., at $x=1$). This is an **initial value problem.**\n\n- The third system is not a boundary value problem. It contains an equation only. No conditions are specified."}] [{:db/id 17592186368948 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id 17592186147492 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "6e71c0c9-7dd1-5f09-a55c-96b5bed1ee9a" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying a Differential Equation Corresponding to a Phase Line"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "aa11f230-ac01-54c6-a241-9eb830650515" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "None of the listed" :db/id 17592186368950} {:answer/id #uuid "444b69f8-1cac-5f17-9963-b92e89f8ec0e" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id 17592186368951} {:answer/id #uuid "4a0c2a5a-5cf5-5c75-808a-065f807aad3f" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id 17592186368952} {:answer/id #uuid "124f7a70-3b63-5ab2-a4c5-037f5441d98c" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id 17592186368953} {:answer/id #uuid "b27177c1-e96c-5686-be29-b410d18356a1" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id 17592186368954}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "444b69f8-1cac-5f17-9963-b92e89f8ec0e" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id 17592186368951} :answer-field/id #uuid "e86b64bc-7b59-5c75-87d1-36e710ec3802" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id 17592186368949}] :question/difficulty {:db/id 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "5a7147e0-2667-5230-a581-5d8792b92824" :question/math-academy-id "q-98642" :question/problem "![](images/cf/cff9a8967582489286a62b78234e147684d1e453c20967fe1190cf515afe246f.png)\n\nConsider the phase line shown above. Which of the following differential equations could have this phase line?\n\n- $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}={y}^{2}(y+3)$\n- $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=y(y+3)^{2}$\n- $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}={y}^{2}+3y$" :question/worked-solution "First, notice that all three differential equations are of the form\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=f(y).\n$$\n\nThe phase line gives us the following information:\n\n- The equilibrium solutions are $y=-3$ and $y=0.$\n\n- $f(y)$ is positive for $y\\in (-3,0)\\cup (0,\\infty ).$\n\n- $f(y)$ is negative for $y\\in (-\\infty ,-3).$\n\nWith this in mind, let's study each of the given equations.\n\n- We start by considering ${y}^{′}={y}^{2}(y+3).$\n\nFirst, we see that $f(y)={y}^{2}(y+3),$ so $f(y)=0$ if $y=-3,0.$ So, the equilibrium solutions correspond to those shown in the phase line.\n\nNow, we can construct a sign table for $f(y)$ as follows:\n\n|  | $(-\\infty ,-3)$ | $(-3,0)$ | $(0,\\infty )$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $y+3$ | $-$ | $+$ | $+$ |\n| ${y}^{2}$ | $+$ | $+$ | $+$ |\n| $f(y)$ | $-$ | $+$ | $+$ |\n\nSo, we have that $f(y)$ is negative for  $y\\in (-\\infty ,-3),$ and $f(y)$ is positive for $y\\in (-3,0)\\cup (0,\\infty ).$ This corresponds to the arrows shown on the phase line.\n\nTherefore, equation I has the given phase line.\n\n- Next, we consider ${y}^{′}=y(y+3)^{2}.$\n\nFirst, $f(y)=y(y+3)^{2},$ so $f(y)=0$ if $y=-3,0.$ So, the equilibrium solutions correspond to those shown in the phase line.\n\nHowever, we note that $f(y)<0$ for $y\\in (-\\infty ,-3)\\cup (-3,0).$ This does *not* correspond to the arrows shown on the phase line.\n\nTherefore, equation II does not have the given phase line.\n\n- Finally, we consider ${y}^{′}={y}^{2}+3y.$\n\nFirst, $f(y)=y(y+3),$ so $f(y)=0$ if $y=-3,0.$ So, the equilibrium solutions correspond to those shown in the phase line.\n\nHowever, we note that $f(y)<0$ for $y\\in (-3,0).$ This does *not* correspond to the arrows shown on the phase line.\n\nTherefore, equation III does not have the given phase line.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only.\""}] [{:db/id 17592186368955 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id 17592186147492 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "6e71c0c9-7dd1-5f09-a55c-96b5bed1ee9a" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying a Differential Equation Corresponding to a Phase Line"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0adf3855-8b8e-50f0-87c2-5ad31db132b0" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "None of the listed" :db/id 17592186368957} {:answer/id #uuid "2376622c-effa-5c4d-9f39-6c8225c77917" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id 17592186368958} {:answer/id #uuid "11a8816d-6cad-59ac-91b1-a8b0b37f8344" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id 17592186368959} {:answer/id #uuid "7d69f29e-f9b7-5042-9707-68740332f104" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id 17592186368960} {:answer/id #uuid "d00a52ec-f698-5672-92af-792e82f00b53" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id 17592186368961}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "d00a52ec-f698-5672-92af-792e82f00b53" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id 17592186368961} :answer-field/id #uuid "84cf558a-bb8d-52c9-9e7e-54cfb455084b" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id 17592186368956}] :question/difficulty {:db/id 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "bfb99b4c-3f9c-571a-8cfe-9d0080c591ee" :question/math-academy-id "q-98647" :question/problem "![](images/a7/a7f21df22875efb2457fcf82e616778c9f61f77843b6969c964a293f9e113a5a.png)\n\nConsider the phase line shown above. Which of the following differential equations could have this phase line?\n\n- $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=(y-2)(y-4)^{2}$\n- $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}={y}^{2}-6y+8$\n- $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=-{y}^{2}+6y-8$" :question/worked-solution "First, notice that all three differential equations are of the form\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=f(y).\n$$\n\nThe phase line gives us the following information:\n\n- The equilibrium solutions are $y=2$ and $y=4.$\n\n- $f(y)$ is positive for $y\\in (-\\infty ,2)\\cup (4,\\infty ).$\n\n- $f(y)$ is negative for $y\\in (2,4).$\n\nWith this in mind, let's study each of the given equations.\n\n- We start by considering ${y}^{′}=(y-2)(y-4)^{2}.$\n\nFirst, $f(y)=(y-2)(y-4)^{2},$ so $f(y)=0$ if $y=2,4.$ So, the equilibrium solutions correspond to those shown in the phase line.\n\nHowever, we note that $f(y)>0$ for $y\\in (2,4)\\cup (4,\\infty ).$ This does *not* correspond to the arrows shown on the phase line.\n\nTherefore, equation I does not have the given phase line.\n\n- Next, we consider ${y}^{′}={y}^{2}-6y+8.$\n\nFirst, we see that $f(y)=(y-2)(y-4),$ so $f(y)=0$ if $y=2,4.$ So, the equilibrium solutions correspond to those shown in the phase line.\n\nNow, we can construct a sign table for $f(y)$ as follows: \n\n|  | $(-\\infty ,2)$ | $(2,4)$ | $(4,\\infty )$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $y-2$ | $-$ | $+$ | $+$ |\n| $y-4$ | $-$ | $-$ | $+$ |\n| $f(y)$ | $+$ | $-$ | $+$ |\n\nSo, we have that $f(y)$ is positive for  $y\\in (-\\infty ,2)\\cup (4,\\infty ),$ and $f(y)$ is negative for $y\\in (2,4).$ This corresponds to the arrows shown on the phase line.\n\nTherefore, equation II has the given phase line.\n\n- Finally, we consider ${y}^{′}=-{y}^{2}+6y-8.$\n\nFirst, $f(y)=-(y-2)(y-4),$ so $f(y)=0$ if $y=2,4.$ So, the equilibrium solutions correspond to those shown in the phase line.\n\nHowever, we note that $f(y)>0$ for $y\\in (2,4).$ This does *not* correspond to the arrows shown on the phase line.\n\nTherefore, equation III does not have the given phase line.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II only.\""}] [{:db/id 17592186368969 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id 17592186083201 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "dc6d9b69-0459-515a-ba7b-c62cab96ca00" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Divergent Improper Integrals"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "5cc7e89f-d22e-5fbb-a9dd-d145070270f1" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id 17592186368971} {:answer/id #uuid "580976b8-b37c-5a64-a98c-ea4717754d60" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id 17592186368972} {:answer/id #uuid "6b589691-e0b9-535e-8234-ba50ef3364a5" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id 17592186368973} {:answer/id #uuid "d335b0bb-2166-58cf-b5b9-f0203c1ebcaf" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id 17592186368974} {:answer/id #uuid "5c7f5aa4-bd8d-52e9-9abd-02aed92c23ca" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id 17592186368975}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "d335b0bb-2166-58cf-b5b9-f0203c1ebcaf" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id 17592186368974} :answer-field/id #uuid "6e0d9dea-29ca-5077-8ef9-85315fc69e10" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id 17592186368970}] :question/difficulty {:db/id 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "ebe5f91b-e612-5062-b7df-947a793c33b3" :question/math-academy-id "q-101734" :question/problem "Which of the following improper integrals are divergent?\n\n- ${\\int }_{6}^{\\infty }\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{x-5}}\\,\\text{d}x$\n- ${\\int }_{1}^{\\infty }\\frac{1}{3\\sqrt[3]{{x}^{2}}}\\,\\text{d}x$\n- ${\\int }_{-\\infty }^{-1}\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x$" :question/worked-solution "Let's work out each integral.\n\nFor the first integral, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{6}^{\\infty }\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{x-5}}\\,\\text{d}x & ={lim}_{a\\to \\infty }{\\int }_{6}^{a}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{x-5}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={lim}_{a\\to \\infty }{[2\\sqrt{x-5}]}_{6}^{a} \\\\  & ={lim}_{a\\to \\infty }(2\\sqrt{a-5}-2\\sqrt{6-5}) \\\\  & ={lim}_{a\\to \\infty }(2\\sqrt{a-5}-2) \\\\  & =\\infty ,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nso the integral diverges.\n\nFor the second integral, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{1}^{\\infty }\\frac{1}{3\\sqrt[3]{{x}^{2}}}\\,\\text{d}x & ={lim}_{a\\to \\infty }{\\int }_{1}^{\\infty }\\frac{1}{3}\\,{x}^{-2/3}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={lim}_{a\\to \\infty }{[{x}^{1/3}]}_{1}^{a} \\\\  & ={lim}_{a\\to \\infty }(\\sqrt[3]{a}-1) \\\\  & =\\infty ,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nso the integral diverges.\n\nFor the third integral, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-\\infty }^{-1}\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x & ={lim}_{a\\to -\\infty }{\\int }_{a}^{-1}\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={lim}_{a\\to -\\infty }{[-\\frac{1}{x}]}_{a}^{-1} \\\\  & ={lim}_{a\\to -\\infty }(1+\\frac{1}{a}) \\\\  & =1,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nso the integral is convergent.\n\nIn conclusion, only integrals I and II are divergent."}]] "items" [] "response_users" [] "field_owners" [[17592186347681 17592186348297] [17592186347714 17592186348399] [17592186347718 17592186348242] [17592186347719 17592186348241] [17592186347726 17592186348355] [17592186347758 17592186348410] [17592186347760 17592186348364] [17592186347767 17592186348445] [17592186347782 17592186348353] [17592186347783 17592186348439] [17592186347797 17592186348280] [17592186347845 17592186348281] [17592186347871 17592186348285] [17592186347879 17592186348244] [17592186347882 17592186348298] [17592186347909 17592186348365] [17592186347915 17592186348299] [17592186347931 17592186348279] [17592186347961 17592186348239] [17592186347963 17592186348444] [17592186347967 17592186348412] [17592186348012 17592186348278] [17592186348014 17592186348230] [17592186348030 17592186348296] [17592186348046 17592186348366] [17592186350760 17592186351471] [17592186350774 17592186351422] [17592186350793 17592186351375] [17592186350821 17592186351472] [17592186350831 17592186351373] [17592186350847 17592186351548] [17592186350849 17592186351309] [17592186350892 17592186351423] [17592186350907 17592186351372] [17592186350926 17592186351308] [17592186350930 17592186351277] [17592186350951 17592186351166] [17592186350980 17592186351223] [17592186350988 17592186351374] [17592186351007 17592186351546] [17592186351050 17592186351547] [17592186351052 17592186351535] [17592186351066 17592186351542] [17592186351096 17592186351543] [17592186351138 17592186351159] [17592186351142 17592186351421] [17592186353839 17592186354656] [17592186353848 17592186354533] [17592186353869 17592186354401] [175921